      "summary": "Preached the week of the NATO summit and the Biden-Putin summit of June 2021 — both touted as the most consequential meetings of the decade — Pastor Lyro opens Isaiah 40:12-26, the middle section of one of the Bible's great chapters of comfort. The passage stands between the announcement of comfort (vv. 1-11) and the delivery of comfort (vv. 27-31). Its purpose is to describe the kind of God who will comfort: a God independent from creation, immense beyond measure, all-wise and undisturbed, incomparable to any idol, and absolutely sovereign.\n\nThe sermon walks through each of these attributes with care. On immensity: the nations — and their great summits — are like dust on an atomic scale before God. On wisdom: God is not improvising as history unfolds; nothing surprises him. On idolatry: the ancient idol-maker chaining his carved image to a stand so it would not tip is funny — but we do the same thing when we remake God in our own image to accommodate our preferences. On sovereignty: he brings princes to nothing with a breath, calls every star by name, and not one is missing.\n\nThe sermon is a pastoral call to keep eternal perspective during a week when the political world seemed to hang on two diplomatic summits. The application is direct: some trust in chariots and some in horses, but we will remember the name of the Lord our God. Midterm elections, drought cycles, hurricane seasons — none of it falls outside his sovereign rule. The sermon closes by pointing to the incarnation as the supreme act of this sovereign God: the infinite, independent, immense, all-wise God joined himself to human nature in Christ — and that is where we behold him most clearly."
