      "summary": "Opening the year's four-part series on humility, Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from Ephesians 4:1-6 on the connection between humility and community. Christianity, he argues, is always relational — it cannot be lived in isolation — and every relational problem, whether with God or with others, has pride as its root. Life in community requires humility as its engine. The gospel transforms relationships precisely because it humbles the person who truly grasps its content.\n\nPaul writes Ephesians 4 from prison, which itself demonstrates that the gospel life can be lived under any circumstances. Verses 2-3 function as the operating system for everything that follows in the letter: all loneliness, gentleness, longsuffering, bearing with one another in love, keeping the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace. These virtues are not incidental — they are the fruit of the Spirit. The bond of the church's unity is not sameness but love, and God equips the church with diverse gifts precisely so that its members need one another.\n\nDiscord in the church is not a neutral circumstance — it is evidence that the Spirit is not present and that we are being used as instruments of Satan. Conversely, Charles Hodge observes that knowing the depth from which we have been raised (dead in sin) and the height to which we have been brought (sons of God) must produce humility. If it does not, we likely do not truly understand what God has done. The sermon closes with C.S. Lewis's portrait of the genuinely humble person — not someone constantly advertising his humility, but someone who simply is not thinking about himself at all, fully absorbed in others."
