      "summary": "Preached on January 23, 2022, as the final installment of a four-part vision series for the new year, this sermon by Pastor Tito Lyro moves from defining humility to providing concrete, practical instruction for how to grow in it. He opens by clarifying the difference between pride (excessive preoccupation with self) and humility (total dependence on God through Christ), and firmly grounds the importance of humility in God's own character: he dwells with and pours grace upon the humble, while resisting the proud (Isaiah 57:15; 1 Peter 5:5).\n\nThe practical content is structured around the arc of a day. In the morning, the believer should begin by contemplating the cross -- Martin Lloyd-Jones's observation that nothing crushes pride like gazing at the Son of God -- then acknowledge dependence on God, express gratitude, read large portions of Scripture, meditate, pray, speak about God to others, worship intentionally, and cast all anxieties on the Lord. Anxiety, Pastor Lyro argues, is itself an act of pride: it assumes God will get tomorrow wrong. Commuting time and other idle moments should be redeemed for memorization, prayer, or gospel listening.\n\nIn the evening, the believer should review the day and attribute every grace experienced to God, not to self. Accepting sleep as God's gracious gift is itself a form of humility -- refusing it by overworking, binge-watching, or lying awake in anxiety all express a prideful independence from God's designed limits.\n\nBeyond the rhythm of the day, Pastor Lyro urges his congregation to study God's attributes and the doctrines of grace, to actively identify evidences of grace in the lives of others (including spouses and children), and to deploy encouraging words -- noting that the average person speaks the equivalent of a hundred-page book every day, and that humble hearts will increasingly devote those words to building others up. The sermon closes with the most challenging exhortation: invite and pursue correction from trusted people in the community, because pride also deceives, and the war against it will not end until we see our Savior face to face."
