      "summary": "This fourth and final sermon in the 2022 humility series examines 1 Peter 5:1-7, arguing that humility is not the calling of a particular class within the church — it is the calling of everyone without exception. Pastor Tito Lyro works through each group Peter addresses: elders are to shepherd willingly, not for gain, not lording it over but serving as examples; younger people are to submit to the authority of the elders; and the whole congregation is to be clothed with humility toward one another. The word Peter uses for 'clothed' uniquely refers to a slave putting on a work apron — perhaps recalling Christ's own towel-wrapped service at the Last Supper.\n\nThe sermon then addresses how humility is grown: through mutual submission that exposes our blind spots, through slave-like service of others, and above all through hurling ourselves upon God. The word 'casting' in verse 7 appears only twice in the New Testament and means to throw or hurl — it is a decisive act, not a gradual process. Anxiety, Pastor Lyro argues, is usually a form of pride — the proud thought that God may get tomorrow wrong and our worrying can fix it. The humble cast their cares on God because he cares for them.\n\nThe sermon closes with the double edge of the passage's warning and promise: God is literally at war with the proud, but gives grace — his infinite, marvelous, transforming grace — to the humble. And the humble will be exalted in due time, as God exalted his Son who humbled himself to the point of death on a cross."
