      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from Psalm 119:89-96 on the theme that God's Word is forever settled in heaven—unchanging, reliable, and sufficient for every aspect of human life. The sermon opens by grounding the psalmist's confidence in God's intertrinitarian covenant with Himself, from which flows the central promise of Scripture: 'I will be your God and you will be My people.' Because the Word is the expression of that covenant, it shares in God's own unchangeableness.\n\nThe pastor traces the implications of the Word being settled forever through three major themes in the passage: (1) God's faithfulness endures to all generations—even the regularity of creation, guaranteed by the Noahic covenant, testifies to this; (2) the Word is sufficient for every issue of life and faith, addressing questions of identity, marriage, and child-rearing that the contemporary church unwisely seeks to answer by turning to secular sources; and (3) the settled Word gives hope in the midst of affliction. The psalmist's nine-fold delight in the law is set against a backdrop of persecution, illustrating that the Word sustains God's people precisely when circumstances are most difficult.\n\nDrawing on Isaiah 40-43, Pastor Lyro shows that God's answer to the prophet's cry for something to comfort the people is the proclamation that His Word stands forever. The great promises of Isaiah 41-43—'Fear not, I am with you, I am your God'—belong equally to Gentile believers who have been incorporated into the Israel of God through Christ (Ephesians 2:11-13; Galatians 3:29).\n\nThe sermon closes with a pointed application: the Word has been given to us by God's covenant grace—we were not owed a revelation—and the only proper response is to submit to it wholly, delight in it deeply, and allow it to rule our lives through the Spirit. The listener is asked plainly: 'What are you going to do with this Word?'"
