      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro draws from scraps of material he had been researching while writing, presenting 25 characteristics of the fool drawn from the book of Proverbs. He begins by establishing the proper framework for reading Proverbs: it is wisdom literature written for the covenant community, presupposing faith in Christ and a transformed heart. The 'fool' in Proverbs is not simply a morally ignorant outsider but specifically one who professes membership in the covenant community yet acts as if he does not belong to it — making these characteristics directly applicable to the church.\n\nThe 25 characteristics range across attitudes, speech, relationships, and conduct: the fool despises wisdom and instruction, hates knowledge, grieves his parents, enjoys wickedness, is always right in his own eyes, is quick to anger, deceives in many ways, is arrogant, worldly-focused, quarrelsome, a poor steward, unable to resolve conflict, and given to venting uncontrolled anger. Pastor Lyro highlights that deceit encompasses not only outright lying but flattery, half-truths, and strategic omissions — all of which are sinful. Pride manifests not just in boasting but in self-pity and low self-esteem rooted in a sense of entitlement.\n\nThe lesson closes with an application: because biblical foolishness is not childishness but spiritual unfaithfulness, examining these characteristics in ourselves is an act of self-examination before God. The fool claims to be part of the covenant community but walks as one unredeemed by Christ. The church is called to act according to its profession of faith, rooted in the fear of the Lord."
