      "summary": "An afternoon doctrinal study developing the morning's introduction to the new birth. The doctrine of regeneration — also called effectual calling or being born again — is examined in depth. Key distinctions: regeneration vs. conversion (the former is a moment, the latter a process); the general call to all people vs. the effectual call given only to the elect (Romans 8:30). Three elements of regeneration: enlightening the mind, renewing the heart/nature (Ezekiel 36:26), producing a new will (Ezekiel 36:27). Regeneration precedes faith: 1 John 5:1 in the Greek shows that 'has been born' (perfect tense, completed prior action) precedes 'believes' (present tense); faith is our own genuine act, but the capacity to believe is given by God in the new birth. We are completely passive: Ephesians 2:5 — even when dead in trespasses, God made us alive. On infants dying in infancy: the Westminster Confession affirms elect infants are regenerated and saved; covenant theology gives confidence to believing parents that their children who die in infancy are in heaven. Applications: act as the new person you are; Christians cannot claim inability to obey; regeneration as entirely God's work produces profound thankfulness.",
