      "summary": "An Easter Sunday sermon on 1 Corinthians 15:12-28. Every Lord's Day is a resurrection celebration; Easter is not uniquely special — we meet weekly because Christ rose weekly. Paul's letter addresses teachers in Corinth who were denying the bodily resurrection, a denial that has continued in every era. The sermon works through Paul's ironclad logic: to deny the resurrection is to deny the gospel itself — if Christ is not raised, preaching is empty, sins are unforgiven, the dead in Christ have perished, and the Christian life is not worth living (v.19). Four alternative theories (enemies hid the body; disciples stole it; Jesus swooned; the resurrection was merely spiritual) are examined and dismissed. Verse 20 pivots everything: 'But now Christ is risen.' He is the first fruits of an uncountable harvest (Revelation 7:9). The Adam-Christ parallel (vv.21-22) grounds the resurrection in federal theology. Death is the last enemy, already defeated in Christ's rising and to be finally destroyed at the last day. The resurrection gives every suffering meaning — 'our light and momentary troubles are achieving for us an eternal glory that far outweighs them all' (2 Corinthians 4:17).",
