      "summary": "Mr. Isaiah Couch continues his study of the Belgic Confession in an adult Sunday School lesson, examining two articles that have been personally formative in his own theological journey. The lesson addresses Article 19 on the two natures of Christ and Article 37 on the last judgment, showing how a proper Christology grounds a proper eschatology.\n\nOn Christology, Couch traces the church's historic confession that Jesus is truly God and truly man — two natures united in one person without confusion, change, division, or separation, as defined by the Council of Chalcedon in 451. He engages the historical heresies of the Anabaptists (who denied Christ's true humanity) and Servetus (who denied his true divinity), showing that Debrae in the Belgic Confession stands with the full weight of church history at Nicaea, Constantinople, Ephesus, and Chalcedon. The Heidelberg Catechism's Q&A 16–17 provides the practical answer: Jesus must be truly man to pay for sin in human nature, and truly God to endure the infinite wrath of God and give worth and efficacy to his obedience.\n\nThe transition to eschatology is made through a single line in Article 19: \"our salvation and resurrection depend also on the reality of his body.\" This leads into Article 37, the Belgic's article on the last judgment, which Couch reads in full. He emphasizes that all the dead will be raised and their souls reunited with their proper bodies — a truth that is both comfort to the elect and warning to the unbeliever. Couch closes with a personal testimony about the unexpected death of his father-in-law, noting that a proper understanding of the bodily resurrection provided a peace and comfort he had never before experienced in bereavement."
