      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro introduces the first lesson in a series on the moral law of God, using the Westminster Larger Catechism as a guide. He begins with the foundational truth that all humanity is created in the image of God and is therefore inherently moral — no person is neutral before God. The moral law is not a later addition but was written on the hearts of all humanity at creation. After the fall, the darkness of the human heart makes the written Word necessary, since sinful nature inevitably suppresses the truth of the law written within.\n\nDrawing from WLC Question 93, he defines the moral law as \"the declaration of the will of God to mankind, directing and binding everyone to personal, perfect, and perpetual conformity and obedience.\" Several implications flow from this: all humanity owes obedience to God; humanity's primary duty is God-directed; no one may exempt himself from any part of God's revealed will; and God requires not just goodness but absolute moral perfection — a standard that cannot be lowered without eliminating the need for Christ.\n\nThe lesson concludes with the gospel truth: since no one since Adam has fulfilled the law perfectly, the only avenue to fellowship with God is through the Lord Jesus Christ, the second Adam. His perfect life — not just his atoning death — is imputed to believers. The death of Christ removes guilt; the life of Christ provides the positive righteousness God requires. Pastor Lyro is emphatic that the standard remains perfection and that only Christ meets it on our behalf."
