      "summary": "In the first installment of a two-part series on fears, worries, and anxiety, Pastor Tito Lyro surveys the nature and sinfulness of anxious worry, primarily from Matthew 6:25-34. He begins by placing fear, worry, and anxiety on a moral spectrum: not all of them are always sinful, but anxiety in Scripture appears consistently negative — not something that happens to us but something we actively do, since it flows from sinful choices of the heart.\n\nGodly fear includes reverence for God and appropriate alertness to genuine danger. Godly worry is concern that drives us to prayer, like casting cares upon the Lord in 1 Peter 5:6-7. But sinful worry holds on rather than releases, revealing a proud heart unwilling to trust God. Pastor Lyro notes the irony that the most anxious people often appear meek and mild, but Scripture exposes them as proud — trying to control what only God controls.\n\nThe bulk of the message traces Jesus's teaching in Matthew 6, noting that sinful worry characteristically lives in the future (\"what shall we eat? what shall we drink?\"), divides and distracts the attention from God, and is ultimately idolatrous — whatever commands our anxious attention has become a functional god competing with the true God. Worry is unproductive (it cannot change the future), unnecessary (God is trustworthy and provides), and amounts to unbelief. Pastor Lyro assigns a practical exercise: pair with a church member and work through a set of diagnostic questions about your anxieties, identifying the sins that accompany and flow from a worried heart."
