      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from Psalm 130, a psalm of ascent that moves from the depths of despair and conviction of sin to confident hope in God's forgiving character. He notes that Psalm 130 is often brought into conversations about depression, and while he is careful to acknowledge its complexity, he highlights that it is fundamentally sin talk and forgiveness talk that provide the Psalmist with the stairway out of the depths. Because the Psalmist knows God as a forgiving God, he is able to wait.\n\nThe sermon traces the Psalm's arc from cry to waiting to testimony. Waiting and hoping are treated as synonyms: to wait for God is to hope in God. John Calvin's meditation on the psalm is cited: when a man embraces the Word, he becomes assured of God's attention to his welfare, and that assurance becomes the mother of both waiting and patience. The Psalmist illustrates his hope with the image of a watchman on the city wall during the last, darkest, coldest watch of the night — eyes fixed on the horizon for the rising sun, knowing it is coming. The sun is identified through Malachi 4:2 as the Son of Righteousness, Jesus Christ himself.\n\nThe sermon closes with four diagnostic questions for self-examination: whether believers see sin-awareness as a good thing (as evidence of the Spirit's nearness), whether they understand sin as being against God personally, whether they recognize sin in motive and thought and not merely in action, and whether they can identify specific current sins in their lives. The inability to name any specific sin is presented as a serious spiritual warning sign — the beauty and relief of God's forgiveness can only be appreciated when one knows concretely what is being forgiven."
