      "summary": "In Part II of the series on fears, worries, and anxiety, Pastor Tito Lyro turns to solutions. He reviews diagnostic questions from the previous week and surveys the wrong ways people try to rid themselves of worry — minimizing, self-effort, manipulation, busyness, venting, and wrong praying. He then turns to Philippians 4:6-9 to lay out the right path: right praying, right thinking, right choices, and right doing.\n\nRight praying means bringing everything to God in specific prayer with thanksgiving rather than spinning out in anxious thought. The result Paul promises — the peace of God that surpasses understanding — is peace that the watching world cannot comprehend: how can a Christian be that calm? It is the shalom of God, a completeness and contentment that goes beyond the absence of conflict. Right thinking — meditating on whatever is true, noble, just, pure, lovely, and of good report — redirects the mind from anxious future-scanning to the attributes of God, his Word, and the gospel. Isaiah 26:3 is invoked: perfect peace belongs to the one whose mind is stayed on God. Right choices include biblical planning that acknowledges the Lord's control. Right doing means actually practicing what we know to be right — often our anxiety is rooted in having avoided responsibilities we know we should carry.\n\nThe second half of the lesson defines sinful fear more precisely. Ungodly fear is always directly related to faulty thinking — as illustrated by Abraham in Genesis 12 (willing to sacrifice Sarah's safety to protect himself) and the disciples in the storm of Mark 4 (terrified despite having Jesus in the boat). Sinful fear focuses on circumstances rather than God, and on self rather than God and others. The lesson ends mid-topic, to be continued next week."
