      "summary": "In the third lesson of the Moral Law series, Pastor Tito Lyro returns to the Larger Catechism's analysis of the law's usefulness (WLC Q. 95-97), now working through each group in detail. He notes that the series will ultimately be 22 lessons and will touch on every area of Christian life including parenting, living in society, and evangelism, since the moral law — summarized in the Ten Commandments and then in the two great commandments — touches everything.\n\nFor all humanity (Q. 95), the law is useful in four ways: as a revelation of God's holy nature and will; as a revelation of humanity's moral obligation to God; as a means of confronting all people with their inability to meet God's standard; and as a means of generating appreciation for Christ's perfect obedience. For the unregenerate (Q. 96), the law serves to awaken conscience and drive sinners either to Christ or to condemnation. Pastor Lyro argues forcefully that the gospel cannot be faithfully presented without the moral law — this is why Paul spends three chapters in Romans confronting Jew and Gentile with sin before arriving at the gospel declaration in 3:21.\n\nFor the regenerate (Q. 97), the law serves as the rule of obedience and a prompt for gratitude. Believers are not under the law as a covenant of works — they are neither justified nor condemned by it — but they use it to understand what God requires and to express their thankfulness for grace. The lesson engages a current debate within Reformed circles on whether natural law or biblical law should be used in public discourse, concluding that all genuine moral argument ultimately rests on the law of God."
