      "summary": "In the fourth lesson in the Moral Law series, Pastor Tito Lyro continues and completes the survey of WLC Q. 95-97 on the usefulness of the moral law to all men, to the unregenerate, and to the regenerate. The lesson revisits all three groups with additional depth and attention to practical implications, especially in the areas of evangelism, public discourse, and the believer's daily life.\n\nOn the law's usefulness to all humanity, Pastor Lyro challenges the tendency to assume the Bible only speaks to Christians. The moral law is the authoritative standard for all people — not just for the church — since it is an expression of the holy nature and will of God, who created all humanity in his image. He engages the two-kingdoms debate over whether natural law or biblical law should be used in public discourse, concluding that all genuine moral argument must ultimately rest on the law of God: the only ultimate reason abortion is wrong is that God said so.\n\nOn the law's usefulness to the unregenerate, he insists that the gospel cannot be presented faithfully without the moral law — the pattern of Romans 1-3 makes this clear. He also addresses a questioner who asks about temporal consequences for the unregenerate: the curse of the covenant of works is primarily separation from God, not temporal hardship. This life is the closest an unbeliever will ever get to heaven; this life is the closest a believer will ever get to hell — a perspective that should shape both gospel proclamation and Christian endurance.\n\nOn the law's usefulness to the regenerate, he draws on Titus 2:11-14 to show that obedience to the law is the necessary response to grace, not the means of earning grace. The law gives concrete, practical shape to becoming more like Christ — not abstract spiritual aspirations, but ten specific words that address idolatry, the Sabbath, parents, life, sexuality, property, truth, and desire. The lesson closes by announcing the next subject: how to interpret the Ten Commandments."
