      "summary": "In this third installment of the Moral Law series, Pastor Tito Lyro uses Westminster Larger Catechism questions 98 through 100 to teach how the Ten Commandments are to be properly interpreted. He reads both the Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5 versions of the commandments, noting key differences between the two lists — including the different reasons annexed to the Sabbath commandment (creation in Exodus, redemption in Deuteronomy) and a variation in the tenth commandment that has historically caused disagreement between Reformed, Lutheran, and Catholic traditions over how to number the commandments.\n\nThe heart of the lesson is an exposition of WLC Q99's eight rules of interpretation. Pastor Lyro explains that the moral law is perfect (requiring total conformity, not mere approximate obedience), spiritual (requiring heart transformation by the Holy Spirit, not just external conformity), and broadly applicable — a single commandment can require or forbid multiple related sins and duties. He illustrates these rules with examples from Scripture, such as Jesus's Sermon on the Mount teaching that murder includes anger and adultery includes lust, and Paul's description of covetousness as idolatry.\n\nPastor Lyro closes by previewing the rest of the series: beginning the following week, the congregation's elders and teachers will systematically work through each commandment guided by the Larger Catechism, covering the preface, the substance of each commandment, and the reasons annexed to certain commandments. The series is expected to conclude in early December."
