      "summary": "Elder Andrew Hoy teaches on Westminster Larger Catechism questions 103 to 106, focusing primarily on the text of the first commandment — \"You shall have no other gods before me\" — and examining six questions that emerge from it. He uses an engaging opening quiz to demonstrate that most people in the congregation do not practice overt idolatry, but then shows that the first commandment is far more comprehensive than mere rejection of false religions.\n\nA central section addresses whether the \"gods\" mentioned in Scripture are merely fictions. Drawing on 1 Corinthians 8 and 10, Elder Hoy argues that while false gods are indeed fictions as conceived by their worshipers (idols are nothing), there may be real demonic realities underlying pagan sacrifice — Paul explicitly says Gentile sacrifices are made to demons. He connects this to Old Testament examples of real but limited demonic power (Pharaoh's magicians, Balaam, the Witch of Endor), and suggests this heightens the seriousness of the temptation to idolatry: if demonic powers could offer real if temporary rewards, the sin carried a much more potent temptation than if it were merely imaginary.\n\nThe lesson also clarifies important distinctions: the phrase \"before me\" in the commandment does not imply other gods may be tolerated if ranked lower than Yahweh — it means \"in my very presence,\" emphasizing the audacious heinousness of the sin. And the first and second commandments, though they appear redundant to Catholic and Lutheran tradition (which merges them), address distinct sins: worshiping false gods versus worshiping the true God falsely. The lesson closes with a preview of next week's detailed exploration of what the Larger Catechism says the first commandment requires and forbids."
