      "outline": [
        {
          "title": "Introduction: Anger as a Descent Into Hell",
          "subpoints": [
            "File labeled WLC 118-121 but content is a Wednesday night lesson on anger",
            "James 1:19-20: Be swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath — for the wrath of man does not produce the righteousness of God",
            "Human anger will never produce what God wants in our lives",
            "Anger is always expressed in relationship — ultimately directed against God as the one who ordains all circumstances"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "James 1:19-20"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Seven Truths About Anger",
          "subpoints": [
            "Truth 1: Anger is not necessarily sinful — God is angry; Jesus was angry (Mark 3:5) — but human anger is rarely righteous",
            "Truth 2: All anger seems righteous in the moment — anger is always a response to perceived injustice",
            "Truth 3: Anger is a heart issue — from within, out of the heart (Mark 7:21-22); Matthew 5 connects murder to anger",
            "Truth 4: Anger has a physical component — affects the body (e.g., countenance falling, as with Cain in Genesis 4)",
            "Truth 5: Anger is dangerous and disruptive — Ecclesiastes 7:9; \"anger rests in the bosom of fools\"",
            "Anger is murderous by nature (Matthew 5); an angry person is like a city without walls (Proverbs 25:28)",
            "Ephesians 4: unresolved anger gives a foothold to the devil"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Mark 3:5",
            "Mark 7:21-22",
            "Matthew 5",
            "Ecclesiastes 7:9",
            "Proverbs 25:28",
            "Ephesians 4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Four Sinful Ways People Deal With Anger",
          "subpoints": [
            "Venting: expressing anger freely, hoping to release it — unbiblical (Proverbs 12:18; Proverbs 29:11)",
            "Displacement: redirecting anger at a substitute rather than the real source",
            "Internalizing: keeping anger inside — the Bible forbids hating a brother in your heart (Leviticus 19:17-18)",
            "Denial: claiming not to be angry, or using substitute words (frustrated, annoyed, upset)"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Proverbs 12:18",
            "Proverbs 29:11",
            "Leviticus 19:17-18"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "The Biblical Response to Anger",
          "subpoints": [
            "Vengeance belongs to God (Romans 12:19) — the \"put off\" of anger",
            "Love is the \"put on\" (Romans 12) — heap coals of fire, turn the enemy into non-enemy",
            "Repentance: confessing anger as a heart sin before God — not venting about the person, but focusing on your own heart condition",
            "Jay Adams's diagnostic categories (to be covered in a future lesson)",
            "Ephesians 4: replace anger with kindness, tenderness, and compassion"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Romans 12:19",
            "Ephesians 4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Note: This file is labeled \"WLC 118-121\" but its actual content is a Wednesday night study by Pastor Tito Lyro on the topic of anger. It does not contain the WLC 118-121 Sunday school lesson. The lesson appears to be drawn from research Pastor Lyro was conducting for his Master's thesis on anger and its effects in relationships.\n\nThe lesson opens with James 1:19-20 — be swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath — as the central text. Pastor Lyro observes that the wrath of man never produces the righteousness of God, making anger an inherently counterproductive response for the Christian. He then develops seven truths about anger: it is not necessarily sinful (God and Jesus experienced it), but human anger is almost always mixed with sin; it always seems righteous to the one experiencing it because it is always a response to perceived injustice; it is a heart issue rather than something that happens to us; it has a physical component; it is dangerous and disruptive; it is murderous in nature; and those given to anger are perpetually exposed to chaos.\n\nThe lesson also surveys four common but ineffective ways people handle anger — venting, displacement, internalization, and denial — showing that each fails because it treats anger as something external to be managed rather than as a heart condition to be repented of. The biblical alternative is to put off anger and put on love, confessing the sin before God with focus on one's own heart rather than the other party's wrongs, and being restored to peace through repentance and the grace of Christ."
