      "outline": [
        {
          "title": "Introduction: Setting and Context",
          "subpoints": [
            "This is an Adult Sunday School session introducing the 1 Peter series",
            "The previous week covered a biography of Peter — knowing who Peter was helps us understand what he wrote",
            "Peter uses the standard first-century letter greeting (grace to you) but adds \"peace\" — a distinctively Christian opening",
            "Grace and peace go to the elect dispersed in Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Peter 1:1-2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "What Is an Apostle?",
          "subpoints": [
            "The word apostle had a general meaning of \"messenger\" before Jesus gave it a richer, specific meaning",
            "Jesus chose 12 disciples and named them apostles (Luke 6:13)",
            "Listing the twelve apostles with the congregation",
            "After Pentecost, the disciples began functioning as apostles; Matthias replaced Judas; Paul was added last",
            "The title \"the twelve\" became a technical term for the apostolic college even when the number varied",
            "\"Apostle of Jesus Christ\" is a title used exclusively for the New Testament office — no teacher, prophet, evangelist, or elder bears that particular title",
            "The apostles had authority at least equal to Old Testament prophets; they spoke and wrote God's very words",
            "Paul told the Thessalonians to receive his words as the words of God; Peter in 2 Peter regarded Paul's writings as Scripture",
            "Mark's Gospel is likely Peter's account; Luke worked alongside Paul and had access to the other apostles"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Luke 6:13",
            "Acts 1:24-25",
            "1 Thessalonians"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Are There Apostles Today?",
          "subpoints": [
            "The Mormon church claims apostles who speak infallibly; some evangelical churches claim apostles",
            "Short answer: no — the Bible is complete and we no longer need new apostolic revelation",
            "Ephesians 2:19-22: the church is built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, with Christ as cornerstone",
            "A foundation is laid once — we are now in the superstructure-building phase; the foundation need not be re-laid",
            "The apostles' role was to provide revelatory, infallibly authoritative, canonical witness to Christ's finished work (Richard Gaffin)",
            "Since that function is fulfilled, apostles are no longer needed",
            "The requirement to be an apostle (Acts 1): must have been a witness to Jesus's ministry and resurrection — no one can fulfill this today",
            "Paul acknowledged he was the last, born out of time (1 Corinthians 15:7-9)",
            "No new apostles since the completion of the New Testament; but we have the words of the apostles in Scripture — authoritative and powerful"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Ephesians 2:19-22",
            "Acts 1",
            "1 Corinthians 15:7-9"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "In this Adult Sunday School session introducing the 1 Peter series, Pastor Tito Lyro focuses on the phrase \"Peter, an apostle of Jesus Christ\" in the opening verse. The previous week had covered Peter's biography; this lesson considers what the office of apostle actually is. Pastor Lyro takes the congregation through a quiz of the twelve apostles' names, then explains that Jesus gave the word apostle its richer, authoritative meaning in Luke 6:13 — specifically designating twelve disciples as apostles. The title \"apostle of Jesus Christ\" is uniquely attached to this New Testament office: no other church officer bears that designation.\n\nThe central question of the lesson is whether apostles exist today. After noting that some churches — including the LDS church and certain charismatic evangelical churches — claim apostles, Pastor Lyro argues from Ephesians 2:19-22 that apostleship was a foundational, non-continuing office. Paul's metaphor of the church as a building on a foundation of apostles and prophets implies the foundation is historically complete; the church is now in the superstructure-building phase. The apostles' unique function was to provide infallibly authoritative revelatory witness to Christ's finished work (citing Richard Gaffin's theological formulation). Since that function has been fulfilled in the completed canon of Scripture, there is no need for new apostles — and indeed, the requirement of having personally witnessed Jesus's ministry cannot be fulfilled by anyone today.\n\nThe lesson closes with an exhortation to read 1 Peter as authoritative Scripture — the very words of God through an apostle — and to embrace everything it says as God's will for life in Christ."
