      "summary": "In this Sunday evening sermon on 1 Peter 1:3-5, Pastor Tito Lyro focuses more narrowly on the theme of hope introduced in the morning service. The afternoon sermon considers these same verses with fresh eyes, examining what the living hope is, where it comes from, and what it demands of us.\n\nPastor Lyro opens by confronting a common misplaced hope: the hope that if only we were better Christians, our difficulties would resolve. He argues that Peter points to one place only for the Christian to find hope — the resurrection of Jesus Christ. This section also probes whether the congregation thinks of God the way Peter does: as overflowing with graciousness, mercifulness, kindness, and covenant faithfulness. When life knocks you down, is your first thought that God is gracious? Or do you assume he is working against you?\n\nThe heart of the sermon is an exploration of what makes this hope \"living.\" Biblical hope is not a wish or an uncertain desire — it is confident expectation grounded in the certainty of God's promises. It is the faith-category directed toward the future. This hope is life-generating and life-directing, like the front cable of a ski lift pulling the believer toward his final destination: the resurrection and eternal life with Christ. The incorruptible inheritance reserved in heaven — guaranteed by the Holy Spirit as down payment — is the object of this hope.\n\nThe sermon closes with sharp application: is this hope enough for you? Are you clinging to an interim hope — looking for something in the present circumstances to stabilize you? The hope of the resurrection frees believers to throw themselves fully into the present life, knowing that neither defeats nor victories have the final word. The less this life is treated as the ultimate end, the more joyful and useful for the Lord we become."
