      "outline": [
        {
          "title": "Introduction: Why Serving Christ Is Often Joyless",
          "subpoints": [
            "Christians often serve with a sinking feeling rather than joy",
            "Three wrong views rob us of joy: wrong view of Jesus, wrong view of others, wrong view of ourselves",
            "The sermon series resumes 1 Peter next week; this is Part 2 of a two-part series on serving"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Luke 18:9-14"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Wrong View #1: A Wrong View of Jesus",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. Serving Jesus to be good enough for him — the Pharisee error (Luke 18:9-14; Gal 3:1-3)",
            "The Pharisee was not wrong to fast and tithe; his error was believing his goodness qualified him before God",
            "When we try to earn acceptance, we construct a manageable false Jesus — one who can be pleased by our efforts",
            "Jesus's standard: unless your righteousness exceeds that of the Pharisees, you have no place in the kingdom (Matt 5:20)",
            "B. Serving Jesus to get something from him — the prodigal and the elder brother both wanted what the father had, not the father himself (Luke 15:11-32)",
            "Rewards in heaven are gracious, not merited; the parable of the day laborers shows that all workers receive the same denarius (Matt 20:1-16)",
            "C. Serving Jesus to pay him back — sounds humble but is inherently proud; implies the cross was insufficient",
            "Paying off a debt makes our service about ourselves, not him; acts as though God may still collect the debt; makes us equal partners with God"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Luke 18:9-14",
            "Gal 3:1-3",
            "Luke 15:11-32",
            "Matt 20:1-16",
            "Matt 5:20"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Wrong View #2: A Wrong View of Others",
          "subpoints": [
            "Serving Jesus to impress others — fear of man (Matt 6:1-2)",
            "If we give, pray, or fast to be seen by others, their approval is all we will get",
            "Test: would you do this thing if you knew no one other than you and God would ever know?",
            "Serving to be noticed turns Christian service into competition — either miserable (crushed when others do better) or proud (comparing and inflating oneself)",
            "The cycle of self-justification: lying about one's own weaknesses, dragging other Christians down to elevate oneself"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Matt 6:1-2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Wrong View #3: A Wrong View of Ourselves",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. Serving because Jesus needs us — Martha's error (Luke 10:38-42)",
            "If we never listen to Jesus's word because there is too much Christian service to be done, we may think he needs our work more than we need his word",
            "One way to spot this: people-focused but never God-centered; serving people without a God-centered heart",
            "B. Serving as though we can do the work ourselves — service not bathed in prayer that recognizes our inability apart from Christ's grace",
            "We are no one's savior; Jesus is quite capable of doing all he wants to do without us",
            "We are liberated by knowing there is a Savior, a refuge, a rock — and we are not it"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Luke 10:38-42"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Conclusion: Our Relationship with Christ Does Not Depend on How We Serve",
          "subpoints": [
            "Our relationship with him is only dependent on the fact that he has served us and gave himself as a ransom for us",
            "If you are serving out of coercion or joylessness: repent and experience Jesus as your lover, friend, king, older brother, and God as your Father",
            "Experience the free gift of salvation — not as something to be paid back, but received with gratitude"
          ],
          "scripture": []
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches the second installment of a two-part series on serving Christ, structured around three wrong views that commonly rob Christians of joy in Christian service. The sermon text is the parable of the Pharisee and the Tax Collector (Luke 18:9-14), which provides the opening illustration: the Pharisee was not wrong to fast and tithe, but he believed his service qualified him before God. Jesus's verdict — that the tax collector rather than the Pharisee went home justified — throws cold water on every system that makes human effort the basis of acceptance with God.\n\nThe first wrong view of Jesus is examined in three forms: serving to be good enough for him (the Pharisee error), serving to get something from him (the elder-and-younger-brother error from Luke 15), and serving to pay him back (which sounds humble but is actually proud, implying the cross was insufficient). Each of these wrong views produces a false Jesus — one who can be managed and pleased by human performance — and therefore cannot produce genuine joy. The parable of the day laborers (Matthew 20) illustrates that rewards in heaven are gracious, not merited, and that all workers receive the same denarius regardless of when they started.\n\nThe second wrong view — serving to impress others — is diagnosed as fear of man. Jesus's warning in Matthew 6:1-2 is plain: if we give, pray, or fast to be seen by others, their approval is all we will get. This attitude turns Christian service into a competition that produces either misery or pride. The third wrong view — believing Jesus needs us — is illustrated by Martha's complaint about Mary (Luke 10:38-42), and leads to people-focused service without a God-centered heart condition. The sermon closes with the liberating truth: our relationship with Christ depends not on how we serve him but solely on the fact that he has served us."
