      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro delivers the fourth and final installment of a series on joy in serving Christ, presenting three analogies drawn from Scripture to help Christians regain or maintain joy in Christian service. The sermon text is John 15:11-15, in which Jesus declares that he no longer calls his disciples servants but friends, defining friendship by its ultimate expression: laying down one's life.\n\nThe first analogy — friends with the boss — addresses the relational distance many believers feel in their service. When Jesus is viewed as a distant employer, service becomes an attempt to impress him or those close to him. But Jesus demonstrated his friendship by dying for us, and he explains his commands to us as a friend explains his reasoning — not merely issuing orders. Working for a friend produces greater dedication and joy, not less. The second analogy — bride of the king — draws on Ephesians 5:22-27 and the story of Hosea and Gomer. Jesus is not Prince Charming who marries the beautiful Cinderella; he is the husband who seeks out his unfaithful, prostituted wife, walks into her captivity, and buys her back with his own blood. We are Gomer. And he loves his undeserving bride enough to sanctify and cleanse her, presenting her glorious without spot or wrinkle. This transforms service into the glad response of a wife who serves the husband who loves her sacrificially.\n\nThe third analogy — children of the Father — addresses the mistaken belief that God needs our service. The parable of the prodigal son illustrates the point: the son prepared a speech to offer himself as a hired servant, but the father embraced him as a son before he finished speaking. The son was right that he did not deserve to be called his father's son — but that is solely the father's call. Romans 8:15-17 declares that we have received the Spirit of adoption by whom we cry \"Abba, Father.\" The ultimate treasure of Christianity is not blessings, hope, or peace — it is that we get God himself, as our Father."
