      "summary": "Preaching from Colossians 3:1–4, Mr. Isaiah Couch meditates on the first seven words of the chapter — \"If then you were raised with Christ\" — arguing that this single declaration is the foundational bedrock upon which the entire Christian life is built. Rather than beginning with imperatives to mortify sin or to put on the new man, the Apostle Paul first grounds the believer's life in the rock-solid reality of their position in Christ. Just as Genesis 1:1 can be read over without pausing to absorb the staggering claim it makes, so these opening words of Colossians 3 are often passed by too quickly.\n\nThe sermon unfolds three implications of being raised with Christ. First, the believer is united to Christ — chosen from before the foundation of the world, united to Him in His death, resurrection, ascension, and coming return, and destined one day to hear the call of the Bridegroom (Song of Solomon 2:10). Second, the believer is hidden in Christ — no longer exposed in Adam's nakedness before the holy God (Hebrews 4:13), but covered in Christ's righteousness and hidden in the great High Priest who entered the true holy of holies to intercede for His people (Hebrews 9:24). Third, the believer is made to love Christ — not dragged kicking and screaming into heaven, but given a new heart that loves what God loves, and whose hope of seeing Christ as He is produces ongoing purity (1 John 3:1–2).\n\nMr. Couch concludes by urging the congregation to carry this reality throughout the week: \"If then you were raised with Christ\" is not a peripheral truth but the very foundation of the Christian's identity, motivation, and hope."
