      "outline": [
        {
          "title": "Introduction and Lineage",
          "subpoints": [
            "Samuel Miller descended through his great-grandmother from John Alden and Priscilla Mullins of the Mayflower — John Alden was the last surviving signer of the Mayflower Compact (died 1687)",
            "Born October 31, 1769 — Reformation Day — in Dover, Delaware",
            "Eighth of nine children; father was the Reverend John Miller, a Presbyterian minister and Old Side Presbyterian"
          ],
          "scripture": []
        },
        {
          "title": "The Old Side / New Side Context",
          "subpoints": [
            "Presbyterians divided into Old Side and New Side between 1741 and 1758 over subscription to the Westminster Confession and itinerant revivalism",
            "John Miller (father) was Old Side: full subscription to the confession, strong church judicatories, thorough commitment to Westminster standards and Presbyterian polity",
            "This theological formation shaped Samuel Miller throughout his life"
          ],
          "scripture": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Early Life and Education",
          "subpoints": [
            "Samuel's mother was described by him as one of the most pious women he ever knew — raised in a godly home",
            "Historical context: born in 1769 — five years old at the Declaration of Independence (July 4, 1776); the US Constitution completed September 17, 1787",
            "Taught alongside his brothers at home by his father",
            "Entered the University of Pennsylvania in 1788; observed Washington, Hamilton, and Franklin gather to draft the Constitution",
            "Graduated with honors July 31, 1789 at age 19 — salutatorian; delivered a Latin oration arguing against the neglect of female education",
            "Returned to Dover to study theology under his father, a competent Latin and Greek scholar"
          ],
          "scripture": []
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Elder Scott Hollander presents a biographical sketch of Samuel Miller (1769-1850), one of the founding professors of Princeton Theological Seminary and a pivotal figure in the history of American Presbyterianism. The lesson traces Miller's life from his distinguished Mayflower lineage — through his great-grandmother he descended from John Alden and Priscilla Mullins — through his upbringing in a godly Old Side Presbyterian household under his father, the Reverend John Miller of Dover, Delaware.\n\nThe lesson situates Miller within the significant historical events of his era: born in 1769, he was five years old at the Declaration of Independence and a young man when he observed Washington, Hamilton, and Franklin draft the Constitution as a student at the University of Pennsylvania, from which he graduated with honors in 1789 at age nineteen. His salutatorian address, delivered in Latin, argued against the neglect of female education — a remarkably progressive stance for the time. His father's Old Side theological commitments — full subscription to the Westminster Confession, strong church judicatories, and thorough adherence to Presbyterian polity — formed the theological DNA Miller would carry into his long ministry.\n\nMiller became especially famous for his scholarly writing on Baptist history, his influential essay on the office of the ruling elder, and his role as one of the first two professors at Princeton Theological Seminary (alongside Archibald Alexander). The lesson is part of the congregation's \"Reformed Church Biographies\" series during October, which Elder Hollander notes is providentially fitting given the number of significant events in Miller's life that occurred in October."
