      "summary": "Elder Scott Hollander preaches on Leviticus 19:1-18 in a sermon that bridges the congregation's Westminster Larger Catechism Sunday School series on the Ten Commandments and Pastor Lyro's 1 Peter sermon series. The connection is explicit: 1 Peter 1:15-16 quotes Leviticus 19:2 directly — \"Be holy, for I am holy.\" The sermon pursues three goals: to define holiness, to trace the justification for holy living, and to apply the Levitical commandments to the life of the modern church.\n\nHoliness is defined as \"utterly distinct, set apart\" — Yahweh is transcendently set apart from all creatures because he alone is the uncreated, self-existing God. When he calls his people to be holy as he is holy, he calls them to share in his communicable attributes — righteousness, justice, love, mercy, grace, and kindness — and to be set apart from the surrounding nations. The justification for holy living is located in two foundations: the character of Yahweh himself, and the meaning of the covenant name Yahweh. Elder Hollander invests significant time in urging the congregation to become thoughtful readers of Scripture by attending to the distinction between Yahweh (God's personal, covenantal name, carrying all that is true of him in relationship to his people) and Adonai (Lord, a title of sovereignty), noting that the phrase \"I am Yahweh your God\" is repeated fourteen times in the chapter and that this repetition demands theological attention.\n\nThe application section moves through five observations: (1) obedience leads to holiness — beginning with honoring parents and keeping the Sabbath; (2) right worship leads to holiness — the peace offering passage illustrates the regulative principle of worship; (3) love of neighbor leads to holiness — traced through generosity to the poor, honesty in dealings, respect for all image-bearers regardless of their limitations, justice without partiality, and love that includes confronting sin and pursuing reconciliation. The sermon closes with an appeal to Phil 2:12-13: we work out our sanctification because it is God who works in us, and we cannot fulfill these commandments apart from the work of Christ and the Holy Spirit."
