      "summary": "In this installment of the Reformed Church Biographies series, Mr. Isaiah Couch presents the life and legacy of Charles Hodge (1797–1878), the third professor of Princeton Theological Seminary and what Couch calls \"the ocean\" of American Presbyterianism. Organized around the twin themes of doctrine and devotion, the lesson traces Hodge's formation through a widowed but faithful mother, the catechetical ministry of Pastor Ashbell Green, and a spiritually pivotal revival at Princeton in 1814. Hodge's personal life as a husband and father is highlighted — particularly a Christmas 1825 letter in which he pledged with his wife Sarah to educate their eight children \"for heaven\" and to make that the supreme end above all other concerns.\n\nThe central doctrinal focus of the lesson is the \"spirituality of the church\" — a principle Hodge defended when he opposed the 1861 Gardiner Spring Resolution, which would have required churches to pray and pledge loyalty to the federal government. Though personally a Lincoln supporter, Hodge argued from Westminster Confession of Faith 31:4 that the church as an ecclesiastical body has no authority to render judgment on purely civil questions the Bible does not decide. This same principle shaped his nuanced engagement with the slavery controversy: committed to the church's 1818 unanimous condemnation of slavery as a violation of human rights, he nonetheless opposed both proslavery advocates and those demanding immediate church discipline of slaveholders without a clear biblical basis.\n\nThe lesson closes with a portrait of Hodge's piety: a study with two doors (one for his children, one for seminary students), heard singing hymns as he paced, leading family worship from his famous chair, and writing in The Way of Life that true religion is \"a permanent principle of action, spontaneous in its exercises and progressive in its nature.\" At the 1872 celebration of his fifty years of teaching, a student offered what Couch considers the best summary of the man: \"The world shall write upon his monument great, but we his students will write upon it good.\""
