      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from 1 Peter 1:13–21, arguing that the Christian life is characterized by one dominant fear — the fear of the Lord — that displaces all other fears. The sermon has two points organized around one coin with two sides: (1) the single motivation for Christian living is redemption, and (2) from that motivation flow five specific actions Peter commands his readers to take.\n\nOn the motivation side, Peter grounds everything in the knowledge that believers have been redeemed — not with corruptible things like silver or gold, but with the precious blood of Christ, the spotless Passover Lamb. This redemption was planned before the foundation of the world and accomplished in history; it liberates believers from the aimless, purposeless life handed down by their ancestors and from every form of generational bondage to sin. Faith alone appropriates this redemption, placing the believer's faith and hope entirely in God.\n\nFrom this single motivation flow five actions: (1) gird up the loins of your mind — renew your thinking by the Word; (2) be sober — resist all intoxicating influences, including digital distraction; (3) rest your hope fully on the return of Christ — our only true North Star; (4) be holy in all your conduct, because holiness is your new identity in Christ (illustrated with a child trying to put on shorts that no longer fit); (5) conduct yourselves in the fear of the Lord, living as pilgrims and sojourners in a temporary dwelling. The sermon closes with an urgent call to unbelievers to come to faith and an encouragement to believers to hold the things of this life very loosely."
