      "summary": "Preaching from 1 Peter 1:22–25 in the evening service, Pastor Tito Lyro focuses on the foundational reason the New Testament repeatedly commands believers to love one another. The main action of the passage is verse 22's command: \"Love one another fervently with a pure heart.\" Peter supplies two reasons why Christians can and must do this, both rooted in the gospel.\n\nThe first reason is sanctification: as we obey the truth through the Spirit, our souls are purified, and the natural fruit of that obedience is genuine love for the brethren. Hatred or indifference is not merely a moral failing — it is evidence of stunted faith and immaturity. The second reason is the new birth: the same Word of God that is both living (never stops working) and abiding (never goes away) was used by God to regenerate our hearts, and that new, regenerate heart necessarily loves those who also belong to the covenant community. Peter quotes Isaiah 40:6–8 to prove the Word's endurance — flesh is grass and fades, but the Word of the Lord endures forever, and that enduring Word is the very gospel preached to them.\n\nPeter's goal is not merely brotherly love but fervent love — he uses two progressively stronger Greek words for love, signaling that as we grow in grace, love for the brethren must grow with it. The pattern is Christ's own self-sacrifice (John 13:34–35). The sermon closes with an interactive discussion with the congregation identifying concrete expressions of fervent love: prayer, helping carry things into church, hospitality, forgiving, financial help, visiting the housebound, and asking how someone is — and actually waiting to hear the answer."
