      "summary": "Guest speaker Pastor Renan de Olivera, pastor of the Second Presbyterian Church of Odeia, Brazil, preaches from Psalm 133 on why Christian unity is described as both good and pleasant. Preaching through translation, Pastor de Olivera argues that the Psalm's two images — the anointing oil descending from Aaron's head to the hem of his garment, and the dew of Hermon descending upon Mount Zion — illustrate that Christian unity is simultaneously spiritual and material, priestly and providential. The anointing oil represented the entire people of Israel through their one mediator; the dew of Hermon provided material sustenance (grain, animals) that made possible the sacrifices of worship, so that no member of the community could claim to be unimportant to the whole.\n\nPastor de Olivera offers three reasons why unity is good and pleasant: (1) because united in Christ we have one mediator, the Lord Jesus Christ, without whom there is no access to God; (2) because when we maintain love and unity, it shows the Holy Spirit is at work among us and proves that God is in our midst; and (3) because God ordains His blessing only upon the place where His people are gathered and united — even through disagreements, conflicts, and the need for forgiveness, the Lord commands His blessing upon that body. He closes by warning that the sin of breaking the unity of God's people is one of the few sins the Bible says is punished by exclusion from communion, and that the church — as the new Jerusalem — is the place where all the blessings of covenant life now flow to those united in Christ."
