      "summary": "In this Sunday School lesson on James Henley Thornwell, Pastor Tito Lyro situates Thornwell within the broader controversies that shaped American Presbyterianism. The lesson opens with a detailed overview of two defining Presbyterian controversies: the Old Side / New Side split (1706–1758) over confessional subscription and revivalism, and the Old School / New School controversy (1830s–1857) over ordinary means of grace versus the anxious bench and decision theology. All five men in the biographical series were old school confessionalists, and the PCA traces its heritage to this tradition.\n\nThornwell's life is traced from his origins in South Carolina — where his father died before his eighth birthday, leaving his godly mother to raise him — through his brilliant academic career (valedictorian at the University of South Carolina, speech delivered in Latin) and his unexpected conversion through reading the Westminster Confession of Faith, which he purchased for 25 cents and read through the night. The lesson covers his two major theological controversies: his debate with Charles Hodge over the *jure divino* basis for church government (which still divides Presbyterian denominations into two-office and three-office bodies today), and his defense of chattel slavery, which Pastor Tito straightforwardly calls inexcusable and wrong. Thornwell constructed an idealized version of the Christian plantation that failed to reckon with the brutality of antebellum slavery as it was actually practiced. The lesson closes with a call to historical charity without historical dishonesty — we learn from the past without whitewashing it, and we soberly ask what sins of our own time we may be blind to."
