      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from 1 Peter 1:22–2:3, bringing together the threads of the previous week's evening sermon (on verses 22–25) with a new focus on chapter 2:1–3. The sermon's organizing image is taken from verse 2 — drinking deeply from the milk of the Word — but the passage covers three inseparable movements: violently stripping off relationship-destroying sins (2:1), drinking deeply from the Word (2:2), and grounding the whole in the known goodness of the Lord (2:3).\n\nThe sermon opens with John Donne's famous \"No Man Is an Island\" to establish that human beings are created for community, and the 35 unique \"one another\" commands of the New Testament demonstrate that the epistles are essentially instruction manuals for life in covenant community. The main action remains verse 22's command to love fervently, and everything in chapter 2:1–3 serves that command.\n\nThe largest section of the sermon treats the five relationship-destroying sins of verse 1: malice, deceit, hypocrisy, envy, and evil speaking. Evil speaking receives particular development, expanded into seven sub-forms: lying, distracted conversation, self-centered monopolizing, destructive and unsafe speech, harmful body language, refusal to communicate, and bitterness that relitigates the past. All of these must be ripped off with urgency and violence, like stripping clothing, because they cannot coexist with fervent love.\n\nThe means for producing love is drinking deeply from the Word — with the panting desire of a deer at a drought-dried stream or a dog after an hour of running — internalizing its life-giving power so that the living Word has its way with us and produces growth in love for the brethren. The foundation for all of this is the known goodness of God: Peter's \"if\" in verse 3 is actually a confident affirmation (\"since you have tasted that the Lord is gracious\"), grounding the entire Christian life of community in the character of the Lord Himself."
