      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from 1 Peter 2:4–10, a passage dense with Old Testament quotations and allusions — two from Isaiah (28:16 and 8:14), one from Psalm 118:22, and allusions to Exodus 19 and Hosea 1–2. The sermon's governing call is simple: keep coming to Jesus, the living stone. The passage contains no imperatives, only descriptions — because coming to the Rock is what Christians characteristically do; as they keep coming, they are built up.\n\nThe sermon opens with a careful treatment of the church as continuation and expansion of Israel, not its replacement. Peter applies Old Testament Israel passages to largely Gentile churches because by faith they are Abraham's seed and heirs (Galatians 3:29), members of the commonwealth of Israel through the blood of Christ (Ephesians 2:12–13). The Rock promised by God in the midst of Isaiah's woes (Isaiah 28:16) is Jesus Christ. The stone rejected by the builders (Psalm 118:22) is the resurrected Christ — and \"this is the day the Lord has made\" is the day of resurrection. The stone of stumbling (Isaiah 8:14) is the same Christ who is an offense to the disobedient. Not believing in Jesus is not a passive neutral state; it is an active rejection that will result in being crushed under the stone that falls.\n\nThose who do come to the Rock become living stones themselves, built up into a spiritual house and a holy priesthood. Peter applies the identity language of Old Testament Israel — chosen generation, royal priesthood, holy nation, God's own people — to the Gentile churches, and the Hosea allusion in verse 10 captures the miracle: those who were once not a people are now the people of God. As priests with direct access to the Father through Christ our one mediator, believers offer spiritual sacrifices: the giving of themselves (Romans 12:1), financial giving for the gospel (Philippians 4:18), singing praises (Hebrews 13:15), doing good and sharing (Hebrews 13:16), and proclaiming the praises of the one who called them out of darkness."
