      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro resumes the Sunday School series on the Ten Commandments after a Reformation hiatus, presenting the twentieth lesson on the Ninth Commandment using Westminster Larger Catechism questions 143-145. The commandment \"You shall not bear false witness\" is shown to encompass far more than courtroom perjury — it governs every aspect of speech, reputation, and truth-telling in daily life. The catechism identifies twelve positive duties, including preserving the truth, speaking it sincerely and fully, holding a charitable esteem of neighbors, rejoicing in their good name, discouraging gossip and flattery, and keeping all lawful promises. The catechism also lists nineteen prohibited sins — among them equivocation, slander, backbiting, gossip, unjust judgment, and cowardly silence in the face of injustice. A significant portion of the lesson explores situations where the truth may not be owed (such as wartime deception), the Christian duty to speak out on cultural and political matters, and the pastoral reminder that the blood of Christ covers all failures to keep this commandment. The teacher emphasizes that this command ultimately concerns the heart and how we perceive, speak of, and relate to those around us."
