      "summary": "Elder Andrew Hoy continues and completes the final Sunday School lesson of the Westminster Larger Catechism series, carrying over from WLC 149-150 the previous week into a full treatment of WLC 151. After a recap of the previous week's arguments that no one can perfectly keep God's law and that not all sins are equally heinous, he provides additional scriptural support for distinctions within sin: Jesus's words on anger in Matthew 5, the distinction in judgment between Chorazin and Tyre in Matthew 11, Jesus's statement to Pilate about the \"greater sin\" in John 19:11, 1 John 5:16 on sins that lead to death, David's distinction between secret and presumptuous sins in Psalm 19, and Paul's differential response to sins in 1 Corinthians 5. He then unfolds WLC 151's four categories of aggravation that make some sins more heinous than others: (1) the persons offending — the more age, maturity, grace, social standing, privileges, influence, and authority a person has, the more heinous their sin; (2) the parties offended — sins against God are most heinous, then sins against superiors and the weak brethren; (3) the nature and quality of the offense — its explicitness, compounding character, reach, and relation to knowledge; and (4) circumstances of time and place, especially the Lord's Day and times of experienced grace. The lesson closes with a pastoral word for the scrupulously self-examining Christian, reminding them of six gospel truths that undergird the ongoing battle of sanctification."
