      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from Psalm 86, a prayer of David, asking five questions of the Psalm that together build a theology of prayer. Using the illustration of a young boy whispering in his father's ear — and the cover of Sam Storms's book \"Reaching God's Ear\" — he frames the sermon around the question of why and how we should pray. Question one: why should we pray fervently? Because God is good and delights to answer (v. 5, 7), and because He commands prayer and keeps His word (Jeremiah 33:3; 2 Corinthians 12:7-9 showing Paul's thorn answered with \"My grace is sufficient\"). Question two: what is required of those who pray? Poverty of spirit — an awareness of one's destitute condition (v. 1) — and singleness of heart (v. 11). Humility is required; the excuse \"I'm not good enough to pray\" is pride masquerading as humility. Question three: how often ought we to pray? The Psalm contains fifteen petitions; David prays all day long (v. 3). We tend to quit too quickly; we must fight the certainties of discouragement. Question four: what gives us confidence? The character of God Himself — His goodness, His forgiveness, His abundance of mercy and wonderful works. Question five: what about the burden of sin and guilt? For every accusation of the enemy there is a corresponding remedy from the throne of grace. The character of God — His compassion, His patience, His abundant mercy (v. 15) — is stronger than any accusation. We may even pray for the strength to pray, since that too comes from the Lord."
