      "outline": [
        {
          "title": "Context: The Third and Hardest Sphere of Submission",
          "subpoints": [
            "Peter gives three authoritative illustrations of submission in 1 Peter 2:13–3:6, in order from easiest to hardest",
            "Easiest: submit to civil government (furthest removed from daily life)",
            "Second: submit to employers (closer — parallel to first-century master-slave relationship)",
            "Hardest: a wife's submission to her husband — the closest human relationship, where full knowledge of another's failures makes submission most difficult",
            "The temptation when preaching this passage is to qualify it to death — 'death by a thousand qualifications' — but the plain text must be allowed to speak"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Peter 2:13",
            "1 Peter 3:1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "The Task of Submission (v. 1)",
          "subpoints": [
            "The command is direct: 'wives, be submissive to your own husbands'",
            "Peter equates submission with obedience in verse 6 (the example of Sarah)",
            "Submission does not make a person less — every human being, including Jesus Christ, lives in some form of submission",
            "Jesus' submission to the death of the cross accomplished our salvation (Phil. 2:8)",
            "Being filled with the Spirit includes submitting to one another (Eph. 5:18–21)",
            "A wife is called to submit to her own husband — not to every man by virtue of her sex",
            "Both husbands and wives submit to government and church; the wife's special calling is submission to her own husband",
            "This submission is not based on the husband's character or obedience to the Word"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Peter 3:1",
            "Philippians 2:8",
            "Ephesians 5:18-22",
            "Colossians 3:18"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "The Goal of Submission (vv. 1–2)",
          "subpoints": [
            "Primary goal: to glorify God — a wife's daily, selfless submission declares the glory of God as much as the heavens declare it",
            "Secondary goal: to win her husband — the believing wife's greatest tool for drawing her husband to faithful discipleship is her submission",
            "The husband who is not obeying the Word is won without a word — by observing her chaste conduct accompanied by fear",
            "This submission is freely given, active (not passive), and draws its strength from the gospel, not fear of man"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Peter 3:1-2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "The Manner of Submission (vv. 2–4)",
          "subpoints": [
            "'Without a word' does not mean complete silence (3:15 commands all believers to give a reason for their hope); it means without nagging, lecturing, harassing, goading, or haranguing",
            "Elizabeth George: 'Peter is not teaching that wives are never to speak. He is speaking to the issue of preaching, lecturing, harassing, nagging, and answering back'",
            "The husband observes — continually watches — her life fueled by fear of God, and that is attractive even to the unbelieving because God's order is written on the heart of humanity",
            "True beauty is not outward adornment — Peter's point is not a prohibition on jewelry or nice clothes, but a call to trust in the right thing",
            "Whatever you win them with is what you win them to — winning by outward beauty alone wins only to outward beauty",
            "True beauty: 'the hidden person of the heart with the incorruptible beauty of a gentle and quiet spirit'",
            "Gentle spirit: does not insist on its own rights or demand its own way",
            "Quiet spirit: not about volume of voice but about a well-ordered, peace-filled heart — Chuck Swindoll: 'the silent eloquence of a lovely life'",
            "This inward beauty is incorruptible — it increases with age, in sickness, in service"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Peter 3:2-4",
            "Proverbs 31",
            "Proverbs 11:22"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "The Example of Submission (vv. 5–6)",
          "subpoints": [
            "Holy women through the centuries adorned themselves in this way — trusting God and being submissive to their husbands",
            "Sarah is Peter's supreme example: she called Abraham 'lord' (Gen. 18:12)",
            "Sarah was beautiful — twice Abraham told people she was his wife for fear someone would kill him to take her — but Peter highlights her submission as her greatest beauty",
            "Sarah was not perfect, but she was faithful in her submission",
            "Christian wives are Sarah's daughters if they do good and are not afraid of any terror"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Peter 3:5-6",
            "Genesis 18:12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "The Danger of Submission — and the Response to It",
          "subpoints": [
            "Peter acknowledges 'terror' — there are genuinely frightening things that can happen in marriage",
            "In the United States, one in four wives suffers regular physical abuse in marriage — this must be addressed alongside the call to submission",
            "A husband who abuses his wife is a coward behaving as a citizen of hell",
            "Abuse is absolutely unacceptable and is not what submission asks a wife to endure",
            "If a husband has deserted his family through abuse, wife and children must seek refuge",
            "The church and civil government are protecting institutions for the wife",
            "However, not all that is called abuse today are reasons to end a marriage, and not all suffering is a reason to divorce",
            "Chad and Emily Vendixhorn (Gospel-Centered Marriage): 'Often an ungodly husband does not make his wife's life dangerous — he makes it miserable.' Monica, Augustine's mother, honored her volatile husband, made it easy for him to love her, and he came to Christ.",
            "Peter's plan A is the only plan — not because it always produces the desired outcome, but because following God's word always glorifies God, and that is always a win in marriage"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Peter 3:6",
            "1 Corinthians 7:13-14"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro expounds 1 Peter 3:1–6, the third and hardest of Peter's three illustrations of Christian submission. He addresses the command for wives to submit to their own husbands — even unbelieving ones — explaining the task (independent of the husband's character), the goal (to glorify God and win the husband without nagging), the manner (the incorruptible beauty of a gentle and quiet spirit, not outward adornment), and the example of Sarah. He also confronts the reality of abuse directly, affirming that submission never requires a wife to endure physical danger, while also pushing back against the culture's tendency to treat all marital difficulty as grounds for divorce.",
      "soundcloud_url": "https://soundcloud.com/olympiabp/01-21-24-the-faithful-parent-6-parenting-from-conception-pastor-tito-lyro"
