      "summary": "This Sunday School lesson is the seventh installment in the Faithful Parent series and picks up where the previous lesson left off on parenting from conception. Pastor Lyro begins by completing the earlier session's discussion on the sanctity of human life, arguing that because every human bears the image of God from the moment of conception, abortion constitutes murder. He calls the Church to repent of its practical indifference to the unborn and to engage in concrete action—prayer, advocacy, voting, volunteering at pregnancy centers, and sharing the gospel—as spiritual duties rooted in Proverbs 24 and 31.\n\nThe lesson then transitions to five foundational expectations applicable to parenting at every stage. First, the Bible is fully sufficient for parenting; Christian parents need no outside psychological system to raise their children well. Second, discipline is not a substitute for regeneration—every child is born a sinner and must be pointed to Christ, not merely trained into external compliance. Third, Christian child rearing is covenantal: children of at least one believing parent hold a special covenantal status before God, giving parents grounds for prayerful confidence.\n\nFourth, Scripture places final parenting responsibility on the father, who cannot delegate or abdicate this calling. Fatherhood requires servant leadership modeled after Christ—authority exercised through sacrifice and initiative, never passive or coercive. Honoring and supporting the mother, maintaining a faithful marriage, and knowing what is happening in the household are all dimensions of this responsibility. Fifth, young children need boundaries, not independence; parents who establish firm, loving guidance early will face far less grief during the teenage years than those who defer discipline."
