      "summary": "A PM sermon on Luke 19:1-10, the story of Zacchaeus. Set in the prosperous resort city of Jericho, Zacchaeus was not merely a tax collector but the chief tax collector — the boss of all other tax collectors — and explicitly wealthy. Jesus came to Jericho specifically for this man, looked up at the exact sycamore tree where he was perched, and called him by name. The sycamore's low branches made it accessible for a short man, but the point is divine particularity: of all the people in Jericho, Jesus called this one. Verse 8 uses a true-to-fact conditional in Greek, meaning Zacchaeus is admitting extortion while pledging fourfold restitution — immediate evidence of genuine conversion. The sermon draws a contrast with the rich young ruler (Luke 18): one man clung to wealth and walked away from Jesus, the other abandoned it and received salvation — fulfilling Jesus's words that 'the things which are impossible with men are possible with God.' The episode closes with the programmatic statement of Luke's Gospel: 'The Son of Man has come to seek and to save that which was lost.'",
