      "summary": "A Good Friday sermon on Luke 23:26-46. Five movements through the crucifixion narrative: (1) Simon of Cyrene — compelled to carry the cross, his family transformed; Mark identifies his sons Alexander and Rufus by name; Romans 16:13 suggests his wife became a spiritual mother to Paul; (2) the lamenting women — Jesus redirects their mourning toward themselves and their children, prophesying Jerusalem's destruction in 70 AD; (3) the two criminals — one mocking, one repenting with Spirit-opened eyes; the repentant thief saw Christ's innocence, his own guilt, and Christ's kingdom, receiving the promise of Paradise; (4) the three hours of darkness and the cry 'Father, forgive them' — Jesus bore the eternal wrath of God in those hours (2 Corinthians 5:21); the 'them' of his forgiveness includes all who have sinned; his final cry is one of trust, not despair; (5) the veil torn — declaring open access to God's presence, forever, through Christ alone. Closes with W.H. Auden's observation that Jesus fulfills none of our self-designed dreams — which is exactly why he is real and sufficient.",
