      "summary": "A Sunday school lesson by guest speaker Dr. Jonathan Gibson (Associate Professor of Old Testament, Westminster Theological Seminary), focusing solely on Genesis 1:1. Three frameworks are applied: the verse is liturgical (a call to worship, as when Neil Armstrong quoted it from orbit), theological (12 attributes of God are deduced from the single verse: one, spirit, eternal, infinite, unchangeable, self-existent, living, immortal, creator, omnipotent, omniscient, sovereign), and practical (God can help us with anything because he made everything). The Hebrew word bara (to create) is explained: God is its only subject in the entire OT, and in Genesis 1:1, ex nihilo creation is required because before the beginning only God existed. The heavens of verse 1 are the invisible heavens where God dwells; the visible sky is created on Day 2. The lesson closes with Dr. Gibson's testimony: when his daughter Laela was stillborn, Genesis 1 was a source of profound comfort — the sovereign creator who controls light and darkness is trustworthy even in grief. His children's book *The Moon Is Always Round* grew from a conversation with his young son Ben about God's unchanging goodness.",
