      "summary": "In this Sunday evening sermon from Luke 7:11-17, Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from the account of Jesus raising the widow of Nain's son. He frames the entire passage with the claim that the life-giving word of Christ is both a message of hope for a world of despair and a message of power for a world that is dying. Working through the passage carefully, he shows how two crowds converge at the city gate: a lively crowd following Jesus and a grieving crowd following a coffin. The contrast illustrates the fundamental truth that wherever Christ is absent there is despair, and wherever He is present there is hope, regardless of circumstances.\n\nHope shines through in three distinct ways. First, through His compassion — the Greek word Luke uses is rooted in the word for \"bowels,\" indicating feelings from deep inside. Jesus does not merely offer intellectual sympathy; He feels deeply for suffering people, and He continues to do so now as our sympathetic High Priest in the heavenly places. Second, through His grace — the widow did nothing to merit the miracle; there was no faith, no expectation, no preparation on her part. This is the heart of the gospel: even when we were dead in our transgressions, God made us alive together with Christ. Third, through His word — Christ spoke to the dead young man and he arose. That same powerful, living, active word is in the Scriptures we hold today, able to raise dead sinners to new life.\n\nThe second half of the sermon focuses on power: the power of the word of Christ demonstrated in dramatic fashion as the dead man sat up and began to speak. Pastor Lyro draws out the ripple effects — on the man himself (spiritual life leaves no doubt), on the mother (fellowship restored, joy returned), on the observers (fear and praise), and on the entire region. He illustrates with the story of William Carey and his paralyzed sister Polly whose prayers fueled the modern missionary movement, and closes with Robert Louis Stevenson's childhood image of watching a lamplighter \"cut holes in the darkness\" — a picture of what the word of Christ does through His people."
