      "summary": "In this first of two sermons on 1 Peter 3:8-22, Pastor Tito Lyro sets the interpretive table for one of the most challenging passages in the New Testament. The passage contains two statements that tend to distract from the main point: Peter's claim that \"baptism now saves\" and his reference to Christ preaching to spirits in prison. Before engaging these controversial texts, Pastor Lyro lays out four hermeneutical principles: (1) every passage in 1 Peter must relate to the book's purpose — encouraging suffering Christians to trust God and continue doing good; (2) Scripture does not contradict itself, so Peter's baptismal statement cannot mean that water justifies; (3) the words \"save\" and \"salvation\" have a wide semantic range in Scripture, spanning from regeneration to glorification; and (4) context is king, and the broader context of the passage is God's vindication of those who suffer unjustly.\n\nThe central theme of the passage — and of 1 Peter as a whole — is that God vindicates the righteous. The Psalms are full of saints crying out for divine vindication against unjust persecution, and Peter, Paul, and Jesus all teach the same response: do not avenge yourself; bless those who curse you; God will repay. The single motivating reason a Christian can choose to bless enemies rather than retaliate is the promise that God will vindicate. This is grounded in Psalm 34:12-16, which Peter quotes in verses 10-12: God's eyes are fixed on the righteous (He is our ever-watchful parent), His ears are open to their prayers, and His face is set against the wicked in certain judgment.\n\nThe sermon covers verses 8-12 and closes with a preview: next week Peter will provide three concrete examples of God's faithful vindication — Christ vindicated in the resurrection, Noah vindicated through the flood, and the Christian vindicated through baptism."
