      "summary": "In this second sermon on 1 Peter 3:18-22, Pastor Tito Lyro unfolds the three examples that Peter provides to prove that God vindicates those who suffer unjustly for righteousness' sake. After reviewing last week's four interpretive principles, he focuses on the main point that the two controversial statements — \"baptism now saves\" and \"Christ preached to spirits in prison\" — are not the point of the passage. They are examples within a broader argument: because God has always vindicated the faithful who suffer unjustly, Christians today can entrust themselves to God and continue doing good.\n\nThe first example is Christ's own vindication in the resurrection. Christ suffered unjustly — the just for the unjust — entrusting Himself to the Father who judges righteously. God vindicated Him by raising Him from the dead by the power of the Holy Spirit, affirming that all Christ accomplished on the cross was accepted and effective. The cross seemed a place of defeat, yet through it all principalities and powers were subjected to Christ.\n\nThe second example is Noah's vindication through the waters of the flood. Peter calls Noah a preacher of righteousness who endured 120 years of suffering at the hands of history's most wicked generation. During that time, Christ preached through the Holy Spirit through Noah to the people, giving them ample opportunity to repent — which they refused. Those spirits are now in prison (hell), not because God was mean but because they rejected the gracious offer of repentance. Noah faithfully trusted God and built the ark; the flood came and vindicated him completely. If God sustained Noah through that extreme situation, He can sustain us in our comparatively lesser suffering.\n\nThe third example is the Christian's own vindication through baptism. Baptism in the New Testament was the public declaration that a person now belonged to Christ, which immediately brought suffering. It is not a ritual washing but an appeal to God — a pledge that this person belongs to Christ and suffers unjustly. Just as Noah was vindicated through water, the Christian is vindicated through the waters of baptism. Christ, now seated at the right hand of God with all authorities and powers subject to Him, sees the baptized believer and will vindicate them if not in this life then in the life to come."
