      "summary": "In this sermon from 1 Peter 4:1-6, Pastor Tito Lyro focuses on the striking claim that it is better to suffer than to sin — a truth that cuts directly against the cultural idol of comfort and ease. Peter returns here to verse 17's teaching, now supported by the three examples of vindication just given (Christ, Noah, baptism), and unfolds three reasons why the Christian should choose suffering over sin.\n\nFirst, Christ equips us to obey God. Choosing to suffer for righteousness means you have already chosen not to sin, and that moral choice — exercised repeatedly — strengthens the pattern of obedience. The phrase \"has ceased from sin\" does not mean sinless perfection (Scripture elsewhere rules that out), but a decisive break with sin's controlling power. Christ serves as both example and source: not only did He Himself suffer rather than sin, choosing to entrust Himself to the Father, but believers are united to Him in His death and resurrection. As Romans 6 teaches, they have been baptized into His death and raised to walk in newness of life — which means the power to choose obedience over sin is available through that union.\n\nSecond, the church has already wasted enough time living like the surrounding culture. Peter lists six characteristics of Gentile life — lewdness, lusts, drunkenness, revelries, drinking parties, and abominable idolatries — all marked by lack of self-control and self-centeredness. But these people are no longer Gentiles. In Christ they are a new creation, the Israel of God, and what formerly characterized them need not characterize them any longer. The world will find this strange and will speak evil of the faithful Christian — but this is precisely what faithfulness looks like.\n\nThird, God will judge. The unbelievers who blaspheme the faithful for their godly conduct will give account to the Judge of the living and the dead. This brings the passage back to the overarching theme of 1 Peter: entrust yourself to God, He will vindicate you. The sermon closes with a direct application to relationships with unbelievers, particularly romantic relationships, arguing that a believer living faithfully will inevitably experience conflict in such relationships — and if there is none, that is itself a reason to examine one's own faithfulness."
