      "summary": "In this Sunday evening sermon on John 15:18-27, Pastor Tito Lyro addresses the dark side of union with Christ: the inevitability of the world's hatred. In the upper room discourse, the Lord has been warmly describing the blessings of the Christian life — fruitfulness, brotherly love, the Holy Spirit's empowering. But now the tone shifts as Jesus prepares His disciples for the world's hostility. Union with Christ creates enemies, and this is not a peripheral feature of the Christian life but a central one.\n\nPastor Lyro traces the history of Christian persecution — from the crucifixion of Christ by the very religious establishment that should have recognized Him, to the martyrdom of Stephen, to synods of Christians demanding the execution of John Hus and Jerome, to William Tyndale burned for translating the Bible, to contemporary Christians having their children taken for not affirming gender ideology. The world has always hated the church, and when the world gets into the church, it is the church herself that becomes the persecutor. The sun, he argues, is setting on Christianity's favored position in Western culture — the fashionable elites of our day now consider it a virtue to destroy Christianity.\n\nThe theological root of the world's hatred is not sociological — it is theological. The world hates Christians not primarily because of what Christians do, but because of who Christians are united to. With the power of their sinful nature, the world \"can smell Christ on us.\" It is our visible association with Jesus that is the real, lasting obstacle. Verses 22-23 make this explicit: he who hates Christ hates the Father also — there is an anti-God spirit at the root of all opposition to the church.\n\nThe sermon closes with a clarifying warning and a word of freedom. Christians cannot have both Christ's love and the world's approval — those two are mutually exclusive. This is actually liberating, because it releases believers from the quid-pro-quo mentality and frees them to serve without worrying about receiving anything back. The reward is not from the world but from Christ Himself: \"Great is your reward in heaven.\""
