      "summary": "In this sermon on 1 Peter 4:7-11, Pastor Tito Lyro unfolds Peter's vision for the God-glorifying, other-serving life of the Christian community. He frames the passage with its opening indicative and its closing purpose clause: the basis for everything is that \"the end of all things is at hand\" — Christ is returning — and the goal of everything is that \"in all things God may be glorified through Jesus Christ.\" Between these two poles, Peter describes what the Christian life looks like: prayer, love, hospitality, and the exercise of spiritual gifts. These are not optional extras but the very texture of Christian living.\n\nEschatology, Pastor Lyro argues, must produce godliness now. A study of last things that has no effect on how one lives today misses the purpose for which God gave us prophetic Scripture. Because Christ is coming, we live for His glory — in eating, drinking, parenting, working, thinking, and relating. If something cannot be done for the glory of God, it must not be done at all. And crucially, God does not demand from us what He does not provide — He lavishly supplies the grace to serve, which means the first call is to faithful prayer, drawing on the strength He freely gives.\n\nThe four practices Peter commends are all others-oriented. Prayer brings each other before God with a clear, sober mind. Fervent love covers a multitude of sins and keeps a fellowship from becoming a nest of suspicion and conflict. Hospitality is not mere entertaining but a gospel investment — welcoming and supporting those who carry the word. And spiritual gifts — every one of which is a grace-gift, not a wage — are to be managed for God's glory and the building up of others. The two examples Peter gives (speaking and serving) reflect the two offices Christ has given His church: the elder and the deacon. The sermon closes with a pointed challenge: you cannot live a godly life and be self-centered at the same time."
