      "summary": "In this Sunday evening sermon on 1 Peter 4:10-11, Pastor Tito Lyro focuses on the concept of gift-based ministry — the principle that God has given every member of the church a grace-gift to be used in serving others. He builds on the morning sermon's treatment of 1 Peter 4:7-11, noting that all Christians are made for community, and the 38-plus \"one another\" commands in Scripture make this undeniable. The basis is eschatological: everything of this age will pass away, but the people of God are citizens of the age to come, so we invest in each other.\n\nEvery Christian has received a gift (charisma — a word that contains charis, \"grace\"), which means these gifts are not earned but given. They belong not to the recipient but to God, to be managed and returned like the talents in the parable. This eliminates all pride. How does one discover one's gifts? Ask what you love doing and can do for God's glory; ask what the people around you affirm you are good at. Romans 12:3-8 and 1 Corinthians 12:4-11 both give non-exhaustive lists: prophecy, ministry, teaching, exhortation, giving, leading, mercy, and more. Every Christian shares in all these gifts to some degree, with some having a greater aptitude in particular areas. God has great creative diversity in equipping His church.\n\nPastor Lyro then applies Peter's two examples. The gift of speaking must be exercised as the oracles of God — only what Scripture teaches, with a life consistent with those teachings, submitted to the authority of the elders. The gift of serving must be exercised by the ability God supplies, not by willpower alone — otherwise burnout and bitterness follow. The sermon closes with Ephesians 4:7-16: when Christ ascended He distributed gifts through the teaching offices of the church, so that the whole body would be joined and knit together, every part doing its share, causing the church to grow up in all things into Christ. A healthy church is one where every part is exercising its gifts for the fullness of their Head."
