      "summary": "This is Part 8 of the Person and Work of Christ Sunday school series, covering Westminster Larger Catechism Questions 52-54 on Christ's estate of exaltation. Mr. Isaiah Couch begins by reviewing the Chalcedonian definition — the two natures of Christ undergo no confusion, change, division, or separation — and emphasizing that in both humiliation and exaltation, Christ is the true and glorious God-man. He then works through the first three of four parts of Christ's exaltation. In His resurrection, Christ's body did not see decay, He raised Himself by His own power, and declared Himself the Son of God who had satisfied divine justice and conquered death — all as the public person and federal head of His church. In His ascension, Christ visibly went up bodily into the highest heavens forty days after the resurrection as our representative head and the true and better Adam, raising our affections to heaven and pledging our own future bodily ascension. In His session at the right hand of the Father, Christ is not idly waiting but actively exercising His threefold office of prophet, priest, and king — gathering and defending the church, furnishing it with ministers and gifts, and making perpetual intercession. The lesson draws particular comfort from Stephen's vision of Christ standing at God's right hand just before Stephen was stoned, illustrating that Christ is presently our surety and advocate in every affliction. The fourth part of exaltation — the return to judge the world — is reserved for a future lesson."
