      "summary": "Continuing in 1 Peter 4, Pastor Tito Lyro picks up verses 17-18 which had been set aside in the morning sermon. He argues that Peter is describing not clock-time but a season (kairos) in which judgment is moving outward from the house of God into the world. Drawing on the Greek text and the translation choices of various English versions, Lyro clarifies that judgment here is not primarily condemnation but a process of evaluation — one that can result in purification for the believer in Christ, and condemnation for those outside of Christ. The righteous are saved through the hard times of this age, while the ungodly's reckoning is deferred to the last day — an idea Lyro grounds in Peter's quotation of Proverbs 11:31 and Paul's exhortation in Acts 14. The sermon concludes with a passionate call to evangelism: since hell is real and condemnation is final, the church must blow the trumpet clearly, proclaiming the gospel to those around them and entrusting the results to God."
