      "summary": "Preaching from 1 Peter 4:12-19, Pastor Tito Lyro exhorts the congregation to keep trusting the Lord through suffering, drawing the memorable image from Finding Nemo of Dorie urging Marlin to just keep swimming. He argues that Peter gives two complementary reasons to persevere: first, suffering borne faithfully for Christ is a sign of the glory to come, because it proves the genuineness of faith and brings an unusual fullness of the Spirit's presence; and second, such suffering is also a sign of the judgment to come — the church is in a season of purifying judgment, while unbelievers accumulate their judgment for the last day. Lyro is especially pointed in calling the church not to compromise the gospel merely to avoid persecution, noting that the cross itself is the most offensive element of Christianity. The sermon closes with verse 19, calling believers to entrust their souls to God as a faithful Creator and to press on in doing good — loving God and loving others — while leaving ultimate vindication in his hands."
