      "summary": "Taking a break from the 1 Peter series, Pastor Lyro addresses the reality of God's presence with his people in suffering. Beginning with the accounts of Gethsemane and the Crucifixion (Mark 14–15), he establishes that God is not merely theoretically aware of human suffering but has entered into it experientially through the person of Jesus Christ. Drawing from Hebrews 4:14–16, he shows that because Christ was tempted and suffered in every way as we are — yet without sin — he is uniquely qualified as our Great High Priest to sympathize with our weaknesses and provide mercy and grace at exactly the right time. The sermon underscores that our suffering will never match Christ's, who bore the very wrath of God at Calvary, and because he endured that dark hour, we have his promise that we will never be forsaken. Rather than seeking answers to the \"why\" of suffering, believers are called to fix their eyes on the cross and the God of the cross, who is both sovereign over suffering and present within it. The congregation is encouraged to cultivate a love for the cross before trials arrive, so that when suffering comes — cancer, loss, unemployment, grief — they are already prepared to be sustained by Christ, who always lives to make intercession for them."
