      "summary": "In this companion sermon to the morning message, Pastor Lyro turns from God's response to our suffering to our biblical response to trials. He begins by anchoring the believer's primary obligation in affliction: to please Christ at all times, knowing that every response — whether in life or death — will be reviewed at the judgment seat of Christ (2 Corinthians 5:9-10). He surveys fifteen reasons Scripture gives for why God allows trials in his people's lives, ranging from unconfessed sin and divine discipline to opportunities to witness, to glorifying God, and to driving believers back to God's Word. Throughout, he cautions against the pastoral error of Job's friends — assuming unconfessed sin is the only explanation — while affirming that God always has purposeful reasons for suffering, even when they remain hidden. The sermon then surveys the rich resources God supplies: his sovereign providential care, Christ's ongoing intercession, the indwelling Spirit, sufficient grace, the community of the church, and the perspective of eternity. Pastor Lyro concludes that God never merely commands believers to respond rightly in suffering and leaves them to manage alone — through Christ he furnishes everything needed to suffer well, for his glory."
