      "summary": "In the sixth lesson of the Core of the Reformed Faith adult Sunday School series, Pastor Lyro teaches that the Reformed faith is a theology for all of life, demolishing the false division between the sacred and secular. He opens with Abraham Kuyper's famous declaration — there is not a square inch in all of creation over which Christ does not cry \"Mine!\" — and grounds it in 1 Corinthians 10:31, Romans 11:36, and Revelation 1:8. He traces the historical roots of the secular/sacred split to pre-Reformation scholastic theology (particularly Thomas Aquinas's elevation of the contemplative life over ordinary work), shows how the Reformation overturned it through justification by faith alone and the doctrine of the priesthood of all believers, and draws on Luther and the Puritans to demonstrate that every lawful vocation is a calling from God. The bulk of the lesson unpacks 1 Corinthians 10:31 — whatever you eat or drink or whatever you do, do all for the glory of God — using eating and drinking as the most mundane examples to show that the principle applies to everything. He teaches that gluttony is an idolatrous relationship with food that can manifest in overeating, hoarding, binge-purge cycles, or anorexia, while drunkenness and recreational drug use are shown to be sins of mind-alteration that must be displaced by being filled with the Spirit (Ephesians 5:18). The session closes affirming that all of life lived before the face of God — from sewage repair to Sunday worship — is sacred."
