      "summary": "As the companion sermon to the morning message on humility, Pastor Lyro turns to pride itself, arguing that every relational breakdown — both with God and with other people — is ultimately rooted in pride. He opens with the disciples' dispute about which of them is greatest (Luke 9:46-48) and establishes the sermon's thesis: every last relational struggle we have traces to pride. He draws on the Puritan Richard Rooker's observation that pride clings so fast to the human heart that when you peel back all other sins, pride is the last and hardest to remove. The sermon surveys the Bible's strong verdict on pride — it appears in Jesus's list of heart-defilements in Matthew 15, is the first item God hates in Proverbs 6, and is called an abomination in Proverbs 16:5 — and makes the sharp pastoral point that the popular saying \"God hates the sin but loves the sinner\" is false because sin exists only in the sinner. The main body of the sermon is a diagnostic list of 32 manifestations of pride, presented as a practical tool for self-examination. Manifestations catalogued include complaining against God, ingratitude (Hezekiah in 2 Chronicles 32), anger, seeing oneself as better than others, an inflated view of one's own importance, and many more. Pride is shown to encompass not only arrogance and self-worship but also self-pity and self-loathing — so-called \"low self-esteem\" is typically not the absence of pride but another expression of it."
