      "summary": "Preached by Elder Nick Anderson from the often-overlooked book of Nahum, this sermon takes Nahum 1:7-8 as its hinge text to explore the goodness and severity of God in tandem. The historical background of the sermon: Nahum prophesied against Nineveh, the capital of the brutal Assyrian Empire, sometime between 663 and 612 BC. Unlike Jonah's preaching a century earlier (when Nineveh repented), this time Nineveh did not repent, and Nahum's prophecies of destruction were precisely fulfilled when the city fell in 612 BC. The sermon is anchored in two verses: verse 7 (\"The Lord is good, a stronghold in the day of trouble, and he knows those who trust in him\") and verse 8 (\"But with an overflowing flood he will make an utter end of its place\"). The first movement explores God's goodness as loving, kind, generous, and inclined toward his creatures — grounding this in a battery of Psalms. God's goodness is expressed both in his mercy toward his people and in his justice against his enemies. God especially knows and cares for his own people — salvation rests not on our works but on his certain knowledge of those who are his (2 Timothy 2:19). The second movement describes God's severity: his wrath is terrifying and total, and a comparison with the Mount St. Helens eruption (impressive as it was) illustrates how infinitely beyond human disaster God's judgment will be. The sermon closes by drawing both together: God's patience is a gift — he is slow to anger, but not forever patient — and for the believer this is a great comfort, while the unbeliever is urged to repent today."
