      "summary": "In the third sermon of the Fruit of the Spirit series, Pastor Tito Lyro takes 1 Corinthians 13 as the most thorough biblical definition of love and shows why love is listed first among the fruit of the Spirit in Galatians 5:22: it encompasses all the other fruit. He grounds the entire discussion in the gospel, arguing that our ability to love is rooted in God's love for us — we love because he first loved us. He traces the nature of God's love as demonstrated in the giving of his Son and in the adoption of undeserving sinners as his children, and shows that love for God is demonstrated by obedience to his Word while love for God compels love for the brethren. He then explains why love is permanent and greatest among faith, hope, and love: faith will become sight and hope will be fulfilled at Christ's return, but love will continue for all eternity. Working through the fourteen characteristics of love in 1 Corinthians 13:4-8, he shows both what love is — patient, kind, persevering — and what it is not: envious, proud, rude, self-seeking, easily provoked, record-keeping, or rejoicing in sin. The sermon closes with the insistence that love is not a technique but the fruit of a deep encounter with the gospel: only the cross of Christ is great enough to drive impatience, envy, pride, and grudge-keeping out of a human heart."
