      "summary": "Mr. Isaiah Couch presents the life of Robert Moffat (1795-1883), a Scottish missionary who labored 54 years in southern Africa under the London Missionary Society. Using Ecclesiastes 3:1 as a framework, the lesson traces five seasons in Moffat's life: his birth and early calling nurtured by his mother's love for missions; his years of planting the gospel among the Namaqualand and Bechuana peoples, including the conversion of the feared Chief Afrikaner; a long season of mourning marked by tribal wars, sickness, and the deaths of five children; a season of dancing as revival broke out at Kuruman and Moffat completed the translation of the entire Bible into the Bechuana language; and his final years in England, widowed yet faithful, receiving honors before his death in 1883. Throughout, the lesson emphasizes Moffat's unwavering trust in the sovereignty of God and his burning desire to serve as \"the King's Gardener\" in Africa."
