      "summary": "In the fifth sermon of the Fruit of the Spirit series, Pastor Tito Lyro examines the fruit of peace in Galatians 5, beginning by noting that peace eludes the world and often the church because people are not walking in the Spirit. He opens by briefly reviewing love and joy before turning to a thorough biblical definition of peace: not merely the absence of conflict (the Western idea), but the biblical concept of shalom — wholeness, completeness, satisfaction, and covenant well-being. He traces peace through the whole Bible as one of God's greatest blessings, noting that God calls himself \"the God of peace,\" that the covenant of salvation is a \"covenant of peace,\" and that the Son is the \"Prince of Peace.\" He shows that peace is given by the entire Trinity: by the Father in creation, through the Son in redemption, and by the Spirit as a fruit. The sermon then develops three dimensions of peace — with God (the most important, only possible through Christ), with others (genuine harmony and unity as a result of loving one's neighbor), and internal peace (wholeness and contentment, the least important of the three). Pastor Lyro powerfully ties Jesus' reputation in the world to the unity of his people, drawing on John 17:20-23, and closes with two vivid word pictures from Paul and John Owen: peace as the referee that blows the whistle on anything out of line, and peace as the rope that binds the church's diverse members into one bundle that Christ can carry home."
