      "summary": "In the evening sermon, Pastor Tito Lyro takes two verses from Ephesians 4:31-32 to show that kindness is better than bitterness, and that the two are a direct put-off/put-on pair in Paul's argument. He starts by establishing the power of words as a window into the heart (Proverbs 18:21; James 3:5-6) and then works through the five sins of verse 31 — bitterness, wrath, anger, clamoring, and evil speaking/slander — noting that Paul uses the word \"every\" before each, calling for repentance not in the abstract but regarding specific bitterness toward specific people. The root cause for all five is identified as a heart that has not allowed God's forgiveness to govern its behavior. Drawing on the parable of the unforgiving servant, Pastor Lyro argues that since God has forgiven us a debt infinitely larger than any debt owed to us, we have no grounds for withholding forgiveness. The put-on side — kindness, compassion, forgiveness — is then traced to the character of God: kindness is the opposite of malice and seeks the good of others, compassion requires actually seeing and knowing people, and forgiveness flows naturally from one who has tasted forgiveness themselves. The sermon closes with the critical point that sanctification is not a move away from the gospel but a deeper immersion into it — the same grace that justified us continues to transform us."
