      "summary": "Mr. Steve Thomas presents the life of William Chalmers Burns (1815-1868), Scotland's great evangelist and the first Scottish Presbyterian missionary to China, framing his life around two themes: taking up one's cross completely, and serving faithfully in whatever role God assigns — whether celebrated or obscure. Burns grew up in a godly home in Kilsyth, Scotland, trained for law under worldly ambition, but was converted through his father's overheard prayer, a cholera epidemic, and letters from his sisters. After licensure he filled Robert Murray M'Cheyne's pulpit at Dundee and sparked widespread revival in Scotland and Northern England, including the famous open-air sermon in the rain at Kilsyth with its climactic cry, 'No cross, no crown.' For nine years Burns labored as an evangelist at home, chafing against his unfulfilled missionary call, before finally sailing for China in 1847 where he would spend the rest of his life. In China he moved into the Chinese Quarter, traveled by foot and riverboat, was repeatedly robbed, met Hudson Taylor (from whom he learned to adopt Chinese dress), and donated his entire salary back to the mission committee to fund more workers. He died in Manchuria on 4 April 1868 — exactly 53 years to the day after his birth — with the words of the Lord's Prayer on his lips. Mr. Thomas draws the lesson that Christians must be willing to take up their crosses fully, serve where God places them without seeking recognition, and be content to sow where another will reap."
