            "Historical definitions reviewed: Plato (power under control — the tamed horse); James Boice; John Stott; Herman Ridderbos (tolerance); William Barclay (best): submissive to the will of God, teachable, not too proud to learn, and most often — considerate of others",
            "Gentleness and humility go hand in hand; both were cuss words in Paul's day — signs of weakness; yet gentleness is a uniquely Christian virtue absent from pagan lists of virtues",
