      "summary": "This adult Sunday School lesson in the \"Thinking Biblically About Life\" series examines adoption from Scripture, aiming to lay theological groundwork for ministering to adoptive families and adoptees. Pastor Lyro defines adoption (following Steven Doe) as bringing one not born within a family to full equality with biological children, establishing a new legal relationship and terminating existing ones. He surveys adoption in the ancient world — present in Hammurabi's Code but conspicuously lacking specific legislation in the Old Testament, where God instead provided for the fatherless through other laws. Twelve Old Testament examples of adoption are identified; three parallel modern practice: Eliezer adopted by Abraham, Moses adopted by Pharaoh's daughter (named, trained, and fully incorporated as a household member — renouncing these rights in Hebrews 11 proves their legal reality), and Esther adopted by Mordecai. Three Old Testament theological foundations for adoption are drawn out: Israel adopted by Yahweh on the basis of pure grace alone; the Davidic king adopted as God's son (with discipline as a sign of genuine sonship); and the eternal Son adopted in the language of Psalm 2:7 at the Incarnation, which elevates adoption to the highest relational category. New Testament theology in Paul's five uses of huiothesia shows that biblical adoption focuses entirely on the benefit of the one adopted — not the needs of the adopter. Five implications follow: adoption is legitimate and honorable, adopted children are never second-class, love must be unconditional and gracious (as God's is toward us), and adopted children are full heirs of the covenant."
