      "summary": "Pastor John Battle, preaching in Pastor Lyro's absence, continues his series in 1 Thessalonians with 1 Thessalonians 2:17-3:3. The sermon takes a simple but powerful three-point outline: Paul had a plan, Satan had a plan, and God has a plan. Paul's plan — to return to the beloved church he had been driven out of after only three weeks — was thwarted on at least two occasions. Yet Paul's heart was always with them; they were his glory and his joy. Satan's plan was to use evil men and circumstances to hinder the gospel and make the lives of believers miserable, driven by his ultimate aim to exalt himself and defeat God. The sermon develops the biblical portrait of Satan as the one who currently holds controlling influence over the kingdoms of this world, warning against putting trust in political leaders. Yet the central and comforting point is that God has a plan — and it always prevails. Paul's inability to return resulted in Timothy being sent, the Thessalonians receiving what they needed, Timothy developing as a leader, and the church receiving 1 and 2 Thessalonians. David's sin with Bathsheba appeared a great victory for Satan, but God used it to produce Psalms 32 and 51, which became foundational examples of justification by faith. The cross itself — the seeming ultimate triumph of evil — was according to the determined counsel and purpose of God. Our confidence is not in ourselves but in the Lord who overrules all."
