      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro concludes the fruit-of-the-Spirit series (apart from a final wrap-up sermon) by examining self-control from Luke 4:1-13 and Luke 22:39-46. He defines self-control as the inner power to withhold present desire with the knowledge of a guaranteed better future — emphasizing that Scripture never commands us to control our circumstances, only ourselves. The root of self-control is a heart changed by the Spirit of God, while its opposite is not mere lack of discipline but selfish greed. The sermon centers on the self-control of Jesus Christ: in the wilderness, He refused the devil's offer of what God had promised Him later; in Gethsemane, He submitted His will to the Father even at the cost of the cross. Because of Jesus's self-control there is a cross, a resurrection, and hope for sinners. Believers receive the same capacity through union with Christ and walking in the Spirit, enabling them to resist temptation and grow in discipline as they mature in sanctification."
