      "summary": "This is the second and final part of the \"Thinking Biblically About Life\" series lesson on adoption. Pastor Tito Lyro begins by revisiting the five implications of the theology of adoption established in the previous session: that adoption is legitimate and God-honoring; that adopted children are never second-class; that adoption is a high calling and honor; that adoptive parents must adjust expectations by reflecting on their own adoption in Christ; and that adopted children are fully subject to parental discipline as an expression of love. He then addresses how to minister practically to families who have adopted, emphasizing the importance of knowing people by asking good questions and of highlighting the personal, permanent, privileged nature of our relationship with God as a model for adoptive parent-child relationships. The final section addresses the most common adoptive-type situation in modern culture — step-parenting when one or both spouses bring children into a marriage — covering pre-marital counseling requirements, unifying parenting approaches, setting proper expectations, and keeping the primacy of the marriage relationship."
