      "summary": "In this first installment of the series Jesus Christ—The Final Revelation, Pastor Lyro opens with Hebrews 1:1-4 as an introduction to the forthcoming series on the book of Hosea. The sermon establishes how the Old Testament should be read—always through the lens of the cross and the finality of Christ's revelation. The author of Hebrews wrote to Jewish Christians tempted to abandon Christ and return to Judaism, arguing that Christ is the ultimate 'better,' the final and complete word from God.\n\nPastor Lyro develops the central claim of verses 1-2: God spoke in partial and progressive ways through the Old Testament prophets, but has now spoken finally and fully through His Son. This is not merely a quantitative upgrade in revelation but the eschatological arrival of God's complete self-disclosure. Christ is the goal of all the Old Testament; once we have Christ, we have God's highest word. There is no going back to the incomplete and no going forward to something greater.\n\nThree pastoral applications are drawn. First, in a pluralistic age that rejects absolute truth, believers must hold that Christ is the only truth to which all revelation has pointed. Second, Islam's view that Muhammad surpassed Christ as a revealer of God is a direct contradiction of Hebrews 1. Third, Mormonism's additional revelations and testaments undermine the finality of Christ's revelation; such extra-biblical revelations notoriously contradict each other and are not trustworthy.\n\nThe sermon closes with the warning of Hebrews 2:1: since we have been given more revelation than any saint in the Old Testament—more than Moses, David, or Elijah—we are under a greater obligation to take heed. The response called for is careful attention to God's Word in every form: personal reading, corporate worship, meditating, praying, and living out the Scriptures."
