      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro begins a new multi-part sermon series through the book of Hosea with this introductory message, reading all of Hosea chapter 1. He situates the book within the canon as the first and longest of the twelve minor prophets, noting that they are 'minor' only in terms of size, not importance. He establishes the historical context of Hosea's ministry in the 8th century BC, when the Northern Kingdom was at the height of prosperity under Jeroboam II — a prosperity that paradoxically fueled Israel's spiritual decline and idolatry.\n\nPastor Lyro addresses the fundamental question of why God speaks through prophecy at all, organizing his answer around who Hosea was, when he prophesied, how (through verifiable, analyzable words pointing to the ultimate Word of John 1:1), and why. The 'why' is answered in three ways: because God loves his people (Hosea 11:8-10), because God is faithful to his covenant (Hosea 11:12), and because Israel turned her back on her God (Hosea 6:4). The prophecy is characterized as one of 'weal and woe' — alternating between covenant curses for disobedience and promises of restoration.\n\nThe central metaphor of the book — God's marriage to Israel, illustrated by Hosea's marriage to the unfaithful Gomer — is introduced as the structural key to understanding the whole prophecy. Israel is the unfaithful wife who worships false gods; God is the faithful husband who continues to provide for and pursue her even while she credits Baal with the blessings of creation.\n\nThe message closes with three applications: prosperity tends to weaken commitment to God (Proverbs 30:8-9); we are no different from Israel in our tendency to wander; and God will never ultimately abandon his people, disciplining them to bring them back to himself."
