      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro teaches the second lesson in the five-part Deconstruction of Christianity series. The lesson is organized around two themes: the 'rerun' (deconstruction is nothing new) and the 'fallout' (its real and devastating consequences). He opens with Ecclesiastes 1:9-10 — 'there is nothing new under the sun' — to frame the argument that deconstruction, however new the terminology, is an ancient phenomenon rooted in the Fall itself.\n\nPastor Lyro reviews the definition from the previous lesson and walks through Genesis 3 in detail, showing that Satan's approach to Eve is the template for all deconstruction: first, question God's word ('Has God indeed said...?'), then deny God's word ('You will not surely die'), and finally, deconstruct God himself by claiming he is withholding good, is untruthful, and is insufficient. Modern deconstruction, he argues, repeats these same moves in contemporary cultural idiom. He also draws on Jeremiah 19, where Israel adopted the religious practices of surrounding pagan nations and even sacrificed children in the Temple, as a historical example of allowing culture to override Scripture.\n\nThe second half of the lesson addresses the fallout from deconstruction. Despite the celebratory narratives promoted on social media, deconstruction causes real harm: to the person deconstructing (loss of faith, community, and direction), to their families, and to the broader church. Pastor Lyro draws a parallel with the transgender movement — both project stories of liberation and happiness, while actual participants often experience profound pain. He specifically identifies deconstruction coaches as dangerous, citing Jesus's warning about millstones for those who cause little ones to stumble, and noting that Abraham Piper (son of John Piper) now actively evangelizes for deconstruction. The church's responsibility is not merely to accompany people on their 'journey' but to tell them where they need to end up."
