      "summary": "This sermon was preached in response to the death of a beloved church member, Mrs. Bond. Pastor Lyro sets aside his planned text and turns to Psalm 23 to address the congregation's grief and to provide a biblical framework for facing death with courage. He begins by observing that American culture has sterilized death—people rarely die at home, the dying are heavily medicated, and the subject is avoided. But ignoring death does not stop it. Mortality remains at 100%, and the death of a loved one is a natural occasion to consider our own.\n\nThe pastor argues that death is neither natural nor a friend. We were created to live forever with God; death intruded through the fall. When Adam and Eve sinned, God's word proved true: they died. Death is called in Scripture 'the last enemy,' and it took the resurrection of Jesus Christ to conquer it. The sermon catalogs the many ways the shadow of death falls over our lives before the final moment: loss of health, of loved ones, of youth, of independence, of meaning. Each is a foretaste of death and a reminder that only what is done for Christ will last.\n\nThe heart of the sermon is the comfort of Psalm 23:4: 'You are with me.' Jesus faced death fully—not as a stoic, but crying out Psalm 22 from the cross. Yet he committed his spirit into the Father's hands. That same Jesus, risen and alive, walks with every believer through the valley of the shadow of death. Drawing on Hebrews 2 and 12, Pastor Lyro shows that Christ endured the cross for the joy set before him—a joy that includes his people. Bunyan's Pilgrim's Progress illustrates the same truth: as Christian and Hopeful enter the river of death and look to Christ, they find ground to stand on.\n\nThe sermon closes with an invitation to 'practice dying' by living now for what is eternal—to love God and love others, because only these are imperishable. Accepting the reality of death, staring through it to Christ on the other side, is the only way to face our last enemy with true and honest courage."
