      "summary": "Elder Nick Anderson teaches the third lesson in the Deconstruction of Christianity series, focusing on the crises that trigger deconstruction and the deeper issue of objective truth. He begins by observing that virtually every deconstruction story begins with some kind of crisis, and uses the image of an earthquake: some buildings collapse while others remain standing, depending on their foundations.\n\nHe surveys six categories of crisis that people commonly cite: suffering (Job, Joseph, and David all suffered greatly yet their faith was strengthened); doubts (the difference between bringing doubts to God vs. conducting an 'exit interview' from faith); political crises (particularly Trump-related disillusionment); purity culture's lack of grace in the 1990s-2000s; crises of the Bible (claims of contradiction and immorality, using Jephthah's vow as an example of cherry-picking out of context); and abuse (real abuse happens, but fallen people are the problem, not Christianity). He argues that none of these constitute good reasons to reject the faith, and that deconstruction is not inevitable even for those who go through them.\n\nThe lesson then pivots to what Anderson identifies as the fundamental flaw in deconstruction: the detachment from objective truth. Drawing on Francis Schaeffer's 'two-story' illustration, he argues that the modern mind has relegated religion to the 'upper story' of subjective preference, when in fact Christianity makes objective truth claims. Jesus did not say 'a truth' or 'my truth'; he said 'I am the way, the truth, and the life.' The resurrection is either true or it is not — and if not, 'your faith is also empty' (1 Corinthians 15:14).\n\nAnderson closes by distinguishing Reformation from deconstruction: the 're' means returning to the original standard of Scripture, while the 'd' means doing the opposite — abandoning it. The Westminster Confession's chapter on Scripture is cited to affirm that the Bible's divine authority is ultimately confirmed by 'the inward work of the Holy Spirit, bearing witness by and with the word in our hearts.'"
