      "outline": [
        {
          "title": "The Children of Hosea and Gomer: Real People, Symbolic Names",
          "subpoints": [
            "In Old Testament culture, names carried deep meaning — not so in our day",
            "Last week: Jezreel (child 1) pointed forward to God's judgment on the house of Jehu",
            "Lo-Ruhamah and Lo-Ammi may not be Hosea's biological children — the text omits 'bore him a son' for both",
            "These may be children born of Gomer's unfaithfulness — yet Hosea cares for them, illustrating God's care for spiritual children of harlotry"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Hosea 1:2-9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Lo-Ruhamah: Idolatry Rejects God's Mercy",
          "subpoints": [
            "Lo-Ruhamah means 'no mercy' or 'no compassion' — specifically the absence of forgiveness",
            "God has been calling Israel to repentance for centuries through the prophets (Elijah, Elisha, the prophetic office itself)",
            "God does not cease to be merciful; it is that Israel's sins have put them outside the reach of God's mercy",
            "The people of Israel do not want God's mercy — so God withdraws it; the worst judgment is granting what unconverted hearts want"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Hosea 1:6",
            "Romans 1:28"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "God Gives Over to the Consequences of Sin",
          "subpoints": [
            "Romans 1:28 — 'God gave them over to a debased mind' for those who exchange God's truth for the lie",
            "God allowing sin's consequences is intended to draw people back to him (prodigal son: 'he came to himself')",
            "Jesus over Jerusalem: 'how often I wanted to gather you... but you were not willing' (Matthew 23:37-38)",
            "Church discipline: handing over to Satan with hope that the soul will be saved — consequences run their course",
            "Final fulfillment: the Assyrian exile of the Northern Kingdom in 722 BC (2 Kings 17:6)"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Romans 1:28",
            "Matthew 23:37-38",
            "Luke 15:17",
            "2 Kings 17:6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Lo-Ammi: Sin Separates from God",
          "subpoints": [
            "Lo-Ammi means 'not my people' — and the text's comment 'I will not be your God' can be translated 'I am not your God'",
            "God originally introduced himself to Israel in Egypt as Yahweh / 'I AM' (Exodus 3)",
            "Here, God says 'I am not' your God — the Northern Kingdom has abandoned Yahweh, so Yahweh officially divorces them",
            "Timeline: Lo-Ammi is conceived six years into Hosea's marriage, showing God's long-suffering before judgment"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Hosea 1:8-9",
            "Exodus 33"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "A Ray of Hope: God Preserves a Remnant",
          "subpoints": [
            "Even in judgment there is grace — verse 7: 'yet I will have mercy on the house of Judah'",
            "God will save Judah supernaturally — not by bow, sword, horses, or horsemen",
            "Fulfillment: the angel of the Lord kills 185,000 Assyrian soldiers overnight (2 Kings 19) while Jerusalem sleeps",
            "Why Judah and not Israel? 'I will be gracious to whom I will be gracious' (Exodus 33) — mercy is not justice; God does what God does",
            "Paul quotes the same passage in Romans 9: it is not of him who wills or runs, but of God who shows mercy"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Hosea 1:7",
            "2 Kings 19",
            "Exodus 33",
            "Romans 9:15-18"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches the second sermon in the Hosea series, working through Hosea 1:2-9 and focusing on the names of Hosea's three children. He begins by observing that while modern parents rarely think about the meaning of names, Old Testament names carried deep significance — and the names of Hosea's children are prophetic object lessons for Israel.\n\nThe previous week covered Jezreel (the first child), whose name pointed to God's impending judgment on the house of Jehu. This sermon focuses on Lo-Ruhamah ('no mercy') and Lo-Ammi ('not my people'). Pastor Lyro notes that some scholars believe Lo-Ruhamah and Lo-Ammi may not be Hosea's biological children, since the text omits the phrase 'bore him a son' that was used for Jezreel — suggesting these children may have been born of Gomer's unfaithfulness. Yet Hosea cares for them regardless, which itself illustrates God's faithfulness to a people who have been spiritually unfaithful.\n\nThe name Lo-Ruhamah (no mercy) does not mean God has ceased to be merciful by nature. Rather, Israel's persistent idolatry has put them outside the sphere of God's mercy, because they do not want it. Drawing on Romans 1:28 and the parable of the prodigal son, Pastor Lyro argues that God 'giving over' people to the consequences of their sin is itself an act aimed at restoration — forcing them to experience the reality of a life without God until they 'come to themselves.' The final historical fulfillment was the Assyrian exile of the Northern Kingdom in 722 BC (2 Kings 17:6).\n\nLo-Ammi ('not my people') carries even deeper theological weight. God originally introduced himself to Israel as 'I AM' (Yahweh, Exodus 3). Here the text declares 'I am not your God' — Yahweh formally 'divorces' the Northern Kingdom that has abandoned him. But even in this darkness, verse 7 offers a ray of hope: God will have mercy on Judah and save them supernaturally — not by military might but by divine intervention, fulfilled when the angel of the Lord killed 185,000 Assyrian soldiers in one night. Paul quotes the same passage in Romans 9 — 'I will be gracious to whom I will be gracious' — to affirm that mercy is God's sovereign prerogative, not a response to human merit."
