      "summary": "The afternoon companion to the morning sermon on 1 Corinthians 1:18-31, this message applies 'the word of the cross' to daily life. Where the morning covered the explanation (the cross separates, inverts wisdom, and turns nobodies into sons), the afternoon covers the application: the cross must be at the core of all of life, not the periphery. Three dangers of drifting from cross-centeredness are examined: subjectivism (measuring God by feelings), legalism (earning acceptance by performance), and navel-gazing (excessive focus on one's own sin rather than God's grace). The remedy for all three is the same: return to the cross, where God's character, our justification, and our ongoing sanctification are fully on display.",
