      "summary": "This sermon covers the first half of Hosea 2 (verses 2-13) as part of an ongoing series through the book of Hosea. Pastor Lyro establishes the main thesis: God pursues His people by all means available to Him until they are safely under His care. Using the prophet Hosea's marriage to the unfaithful Gomer as a picture of God's relationship with Israel, the sermon traces two moves God makes. First, He earnestly calls His people to repentance, as seen in Hosea's plea to his children to urge Gomer to put away her harlotry — a call Pastor Lyro grounds in the biblical definition of repentance from the Westminster Shorter Catechism as a radical turning from sin all the way to its root. Second, God uses His sovereign authority over all things to address His people's sins where they are. Israel attributed to the Baals the provision of grain, wool, oil, and drink — not realizing that God had given those things all along. So God withdraws those provisions and lets Israel pursue her lovers fruitlessly, until she comes to her senses and resolves to return to her first husband. The sermon warns against the modern equivalents of Israel's idolatry — turning to worldly means for what only God provides — and closes with a call to embrace Christ's easy yoke rather than the crushing weight of our own idols."
