      "summary": "This is an early lesson in the Sunday school series \"The Word — God's Infallible Means of Grace,\" covering Westminster Larger Catechism Question 3: \"What is the Word of God?\" Elder Nick Anderson works through six questions about Scripture. He establishes that the Scriptures are holy and unlike any other book, then presents three preliminary observations: not everything God has said is recorded in Scripture; not everything in Scripture is equally clear; and most doctrines must be deduced by good and necessary consequence from multiple passages. He then unpacks four properties that make Scripture the only rule for faith and obedience — authority, infallibility, its role as the supreme court of appeal for all controversies, and the rejection of competing standards like Catholic tradition or even respected theologians. He explains the canon — what it is, how it was received (not created) by the church using a threefold test of apostolicity, wide reception, and doctrinal soundness — and defends the Protestant rejection of the Apocrypha on grounds of its absence from the Jewish canon, its historical inaccuracies, its doctrinal problems, and its lack of apostolic endorsement. The lesson closes with a challenge: do we actually treat the whole Bible — especially the Old Testament, which comprises 80% of Scripture — as the Word of God written for our learning, sanctification, and hope?"
