      "summary": "Elder Scott Hollander presents the fourth lesson in a 10-part Sunday school series on the Word of God as God’s infallible means of grace. The lesson is structured around Westminster Confession of Faith 1.6, which affirms that the whole counsel of God—everything necessary for his glory and man’s salvation, faith, and life—is either expressly set down in Scripture or may be deduced from it by good and necessary consequence, and that nothing is to be added to it.\n\nHollander supports this claim with a cluster of key proof texts: 2 Peter 1:3 (God has given us all things pertaining to life and godliness), 2 Timothy 3:16-17 (all Scripture is profitable and thoroughly equips the man of God), John’s purpose statements in his Gospel and first epistle, Deuteronomy 4:2, and the solemn warning in Revelation 22:18-19 against adding to or taking from God’s Word. He uses the Larger Catechism Q5 to frame the summary: Scripture principally teaches what man is to believe concerning God and what duty God requires of man.\n\nThe bulk of the lesson is a sweeping survey of the entire biblical canon, working through the Tanakh structure (Torah, Nevi’im, Ketuvim) and then the New Testament. In the Old Testament, Hollander highlights creation theology, covenant theology, typology of Christ, and the revelation of God’s attributes throughout each section. He draws on Dr. Johnny Gibson’s observation that Genesis 1:1 alone teaches at least 12 things about God, and traces how each section of the Old Testament reveals both what to believe about God and what he requires of his people.\n\nMoving to the New Testament, he surveys the Gospels as the fulfillment of Old Testament promises, Acts as the proclamation of the whole counsel of God (citing Paul in Acts 20:27), the rich theological content of the epistles, and Revelation as the consummation of all things under Christ’s sovereign rule. The lesson closes with the confident assertion that the entire canon—from Genesis to Revelation—is complete, sufficient, and nothing needs to be added."
