      "summary": "Mr. Alex Lyro delivers the sixth lesson in the Sunday school series on the Word of God as God’s infallible means of grace, focusing on how the Holy Spirit uses the Word to sanctify believers. The lesson opens with John 6:37-44, in which Jesus declares that the Father draws all those he gives to the Son—establishing that salvation is entirely of God, not of human merit or effort.\n\nThe first major section reviews the order of salvation (ordo salutis), tracing the sequence from election through glorification. Alex distinguishes monergistic acts (election, calling, regeneration, adoption, glorification—in which God alone acts) from synergistic acts (faith, repentance—in which God and man cooperate, though even here the entirely is the result of God’s grace). He notes that regeneration, faith, and justification, while logically sequential, occur simultaneously chronologically.\n\nThe heart of the lesson is an exposition of Westminster Larger Catechism Q155 (“How is the Word made effectual to salvation?”), guided by J.G. Voss’s commentary. The key insight: the Word has no inherent saving power apart from the Spirit, but the Spirit ordinarily works through the Word. The Spirit’s sovereignty in salvation is no excuse for spiritual laziness—we are called to work out our salvation with fear and trembling precisely because God is working in us.\n\nFour biblical case studies illustrate the Spirit’s work through the Word: King Josiah’s conviction upon hearing the law (2 Chronicles 34), the six-hour public reading in Nehemiah 8, Philip and the Ethiopian eunuch in Acts 8, and the valley of dry bones in Ezekiel 37—the paradigm for all gospel preaching, where the Spirit brings life through the preached Word. The lesson concludes with practical applications from Westminster Shorter Catechism Q90: read ahead, pray for the preacher, receive preaching as God’s Word, and listen with attentiveness and faith."
