      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro continues the Hosea series with a sermon on chapter 4, titled “An All Time Low.” He establishes that Hosea 4 marks the absolute nadir of Israel’s spiritual condition in the book—the point at which God removes all metaphorical distance and speaks directly to his people. The critical interpretive move of the sermon is to read the passage not as a description of a foreign nation’s moral failure, but as a charge against the visible church.\n\nGod’s indictment in verses 1-3 is threefold: no truth (covenant reliability), no mercy (steadfast love, hesed), and no knowledge of God. This last charge is the thread that runs through the entire book, reaching its famous summary in Hosea 6:6. Pastor Lyro argues that the evidence God cites in verse 2 (swearing, lying, murder, theft, adultery) shows these charges are not empty accusations but documented facts. Even creation itself mourns as a result (v. 3; cf. Romans 8).\n\nThe bulk of the sermon investigates the question: How did Israel reach this all-time low? Two historical events are identified as the primary causes. First, Solomon’s backsliding (1 Kings 11): his marriage to 700 pagan wives turned his heart to other gods despite two divine appearances warning him not to do this. His sin affected the entire nation for generations. Second, Jeroboam’s idolatry (1 Kings 12): when he made two golden calves and restructured Israelite worship for his own political purposes, he intuitively understood that true worship of God empowers God’s people to resist human authority. He replaced it with convenient, people-pleasing counterfeits.\n\nThe sermon closes by drawing three lessons for the contemporary church from Israel’s history (1 Corinthians 10:11): look to the testimony of faithful saints, remove whatever keeps you from following the Lord, and above all look to Jesus—who endured the cross for the joy of saving a people whose hearts are no different from Israel’s in Hosea 4."
