      "summary": "Mr. Isaiah Couch delivers the eighth lesson in the Sunday school series on the Word of God as God’s infallible means of grace, focusing on the Westminster Larger Catechism Q157: “How is the Word of God to be read?” He begins by inviting the congregation to linger over the question itself before jumping to the answer, observing that the posture the question implies—reverence, dependence on the Spirit, desire to obey—is as instructive as the five-point answer.\n\nThe catechism’s answer identifies five ways the Word is to be read: with high and reverent esteem; with firm persuasion that it is the very Word of God; with desire to know, believe, and obey; with diligence and attention to its matter and scope; and with meditation, application, self-denial, and prayer. Isaiah Couch works through each of these in turn, grounding each point in Scripture and drawing heavily on the 17th-century Dutch theologian Wilhelmus a Brakel, whose systematic theology provides both the doctrinal structure and the pastoral warmth of the lesson.\n\nKey biblical illustrations include the covenant ratification in Exodus 24 (Israel’s reverent posture before the Word), the six-hour public reading in Nehemiah 8, Ezekiel eating the scroll (the Word as honey and sweetness), Thomas Watson’s imagery of the Bible as Christ’s last will and testament, and the Bereans of Acts 17 as the model of diligence. Calvin is quoted on the inseparability of the Word and the Spirit. The lesson concludes with the comprehensive vision of Deuteronomy 6 and Colossians 3:16-17—the Word is to be ever before us and ever within us: taught to our children, bound on our hands, written on our doorposts, dwelling richly in our hearts."
