      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro teaches the seventh lesson in the Sunday school series on the Word of God as God’s infallible means of grace. This lesson addresses a topic he acknowledges many modern Protestants have not thought carefully about: the biblical principle that the public reading and preaching of Scripture in the stated worship services of the church should be done only by men who have been duly appointed and called by the church to do so.\n\nThe lesson is structured around two questions from the Westminster Larger Catechism. Q156 asks whether the Word is to be read by all: while all Christians are bound to read it privately and in their families, not all are permitted to read it publicly to the congregation. Pastor Lyro argues this is because public reading of Scripture is an element of worship—it appears in the church’s order of service alongside the sermon, prayers, and sacraments—and only those recognized by the elders should lead the congregation in worship. The biblical pattern is clear: Moses gave the law to the Levites and elders for public reading (Deuteronomy 31), Ezra the Levitical priest led the great reading in Nehemiah 8, and Paul commands Timothy as a minister to devote himself to the public reading of Scripture (1 Timothy 4:13).\n\nQ158 asks by whom the Word is to be preached: only by those sufficiently gifted and duly approved and called to that office. This requires meeting the qualifications of Titus 1, being gifted and examined, and being sent by the church (Romans 10:14-15; Acts 13:1-3). Even Jesus himself did not begin his high-priestly ministry without divine commissioning (Hebrews 5:4-5). Pastor Lyro defends the importance of a trained clergy, citing J.G. Voss’s comparison between ministers and physicians: the state rightly requires thorough training for those whose decisions affect physical life; how much more necessary is rigorous training for those whose work may affect the eternal destiny of souls. He describes in detail the ordination process of the Bible Presbyterian Church, from taking a candidate under care through licensure to ordination."
