      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro continues the Hosea series with part one of a two-part sermon on Hosea 4:1-10 (the companion sermon on 4:10-19 was delivered the same day in the afternoon service). The sermon reads Hosea 4 alongside 1 Peter 5:1-4 to draw out both the diagnosis of Israel’s spiritual condition and the prescription for the church today.\n\nThe charges God brings against Israel in verses 1-3—no truth, no mercy (hesed), no knowledge of God—are examined carefully. The knowledge of God is identified as the master charge, the thread that runs from Hosea 4 to the book’s famous summary in 6:6. Two important theological observations are drawn from the sin catalog: first, Scripture collapses our tendency to rank sins as serious or light—an inconsiderate word and a curse, hatred and murder, lust and adultery are the same in kind before God; second, some sins are so heinous and persistent that they reach a point of no remedy (Jeremiah 7:16; 1 John 5:16). The land itself suffers for human sin (v. 3; Romans 8; Leviticus 18).\n\nThe most striking move of the sermon is identifying the guilty party: while all Israel is accountable, God’s primary charge (vv. 4-6) falls on the priests. Unlike pagan religions where priests guarded secret knowledge, Israelite priests were appointed to make God’s knowledge maximally public and accessible. Instead, they rejected knowledge and so God rejected them. The vicious cycle of verses 7-10 follows: degenerate priests lower themselves to please the people; the people sink to the level of their priests; and degenerate preachers ultimately give the people the itching-ear teaching Paul predicts in 2 Timothy 4:3-4.\n\nThe sermon turns to 1 Peter 5:1-4 for the positive vision: a faithful shepherd who tends the flock of God (not his own flock), serves willingly and not for gain, leads by example rather than lordship, and imitates the Good Shepherd who laid down his life for the sheep. The closing exhortation is addressed to the congregation: you are to be shepherded; do not resist it. When the time comes to choose a pastor, choose one who will shepherd you according to God’s Word."
