      "summary": "A morning sermon on Hosea 5:15-6:6, examining why God rejects Israel's apparent repentance. Four movements are traced. First, God's retreat (5:15): for 800 years Israel has treated God as a means to pleasure and comfort — a form of hedonism; God will withdraw himself from those who use him rather than love him (Romans 11:36; Psalm 16:11). Second, Israel's false repentance (6:1-3): the words sound repentant but lack the one condition God specified — acknowledgement of sin; the language is entirely therapeutic and self-focused; this mirrors what sociologist Christian Smith called 'moralistic therapeutic deism,' the dominant theology of evangelical adolescents (and many adults) today. Third, God's review (6:4-5): Israel's faithfulness is like the morning dew — present briefly, then gone; God has sent prophets for 800 years with this same message; the repentance of 6:1-3 is mere regret at being caught (worldly sorrow), not repentance over sin against God (2 Corinthians 7:10). Fourth, God's reason (6:6): 'I desire mercy and not sacrifice, and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings.' God has always been about the heart — Old and New Testaments consistently teach this. Jesus quotes this verse twice in Matthew (9:13; 12:7). Without covenant faithfulness flowing from a heart changed by the Spirit, all outward religion is nothing."
