      "summary": "Preaching from Luke 6:36-45, Pastor Lyro addresses Jesus' teaching on the relationship between mercy, judgment, and the condition of the heart. The sermon opens with a humorous illustration of how we all excuse our own faults and magnify the faults of others, then moves to the text's central command: be merciful, just as your Father is merciful.\n\nThe first section explores what showing mercy requires. To show mercy to others, we must first know the merciful God ourselves—Ephesians 2 reminds us that God, who is rich in mercy, acted toward us even when we were dead in trespasses and sins. In this context, showing mercy means three things: not judging others with a condemning spirit that presumes to know their motives, forgiving those who have sinned against us, and being generous toward those in need. Forgiveness is treated with particular care: it is not optional, it is not a feeling, and it may be withheld verbally until the other person repents, but it must always be present in the heart.\n\nThe sermon's second movement addresses self-examination. Jesus' image of the log and the speck illustrates our universal tendency to exaggerate others' sins while ignoring our own glaring failures. The prerequisite to helping someone else with their sin is dealing first with our own. Hebrews 4:12 shows that the Word of God is the instrument God uses to open us to see the true condition of our hearts.\n\nThe final section applies Jesus' teaching on trees and fruit. The fruit Jesus refers to is primarily our words—what comes out of our mouths reveals what is in our hearts. Angry, blaming, bitter words reveal a heart that is not right before God. The only solution is regeneration: a good heart is a born-again heart. And the content of that good heart—the treasure it stores up—comes from meditating on God's great mercy in Christ, so that mercy overflows toward others."
