      "summary": "Preached by Pastor Dennis Leaman at age 85, this sermon on Acts 9:1-17 draws three theological points from the account of Saul's conversion on the road to Damascus. Pastor Leaman begins with vivid color—recalling the intensity of Saul's hatred for the early Christians, explaining the meaning of the 'goads' (sharp spikes on a cart that cause pain when kicked), and connecting the dramatic reversal of Saul's conversion to the sovereignty of God.\n\nThe first point is the total sovereignty of God in salvation. Saul was not a likely convert—he was breathing murderous threats, actively arranging persecutions, and consenting to Stephen's death. Yet God sovereignly stopped him in his tracks. Pastor Leaman works through John's Gospel—the most Reformed in the New Testament, in his estimation—to show that salvation is entirely God's initiative: those given to the Son will come, the sheep know his voice, the Father chose a people from the world. Nebuchadnezzar's humbling in Daniel 4 provides a dramatic illustration: God rules in the kingdoms of men and none can restrain his hand.\n\nThe second point is repentance. No salvation without repentance. The contrast between Judas and Peter illuminates the point: both were sorrowful, but Peter ran to Christ, while Judas ran to despair. Saul's response—'Lord, what do you want me to do?'—is the paradigm of genuine repentance: no exceptions, no conditions, complete submission.\n\nThe third and most developed point is union with Christ. Jesus asks Saul, 'Why are you persecuting me?'—not 'them.' This shows that the church is so united to Christ that to persecute believers is to persecute Christ himself. Pastor Leaman connects this to the doctrine of imputation, arguing against the view that union alone makes imputation redundant. Adam's sin is imputed to us (Romans 5); Christ's righteousness is imputed to us (Romans 3-4). This doctrine has profound practical implications: how we treat our spouses, children, and fellow church members is how we treat Jesus."
