      "summary": "In this Sunday evening sermon on Luke 7:1-10, Pastor Tito Lyro examines the Roman centurion whose servant Jesus healed as a model of effective Christian service. The centurion is notable because he is one of only two individuals whom Jesus commends for \"great faith,\" and he demonstrates this faith while remaining fully in his secular vocation — illustrating the Reformation principle that every lawful calling is sacred and can be performed before the face of God.\n\nPastor Lyro identifies three essential qualities for effective Christian service drawn from the centurion's example. First, an effective servant must hold an exalted view of Jesus, trusting that Christ's mere word is sufficient to accomplish what is impossible for man. The centurion understood spiritual authority and believed that salvation belongs to the Savior, not the sinner. Second, an effective servant must maintain a lowly view of himself — not a paralyzing false humility, but a grace-driven acknowledgment of personal insufficiency coupled with confidence in Christ's all-sufficiency.\n\nThird, the centurion's compassion for his dying slave — a person whom first-century society regarded as property — demonstrates the caring view of others that marks effective service. Pastor Lyro argues that no person is beyond the reach of God's regenerating power, and servants of Christ must never write off the apparently hopeless. The sermon closes with the reminder from missionary Hudson Taylor: \"All God's giants have been weak men who did great things for God, because they reckoned on God being with them.\""
