      "summary": "Preaching from Hosea 9, Pastor Lyro works through one of the darkest chapters in the book, refusing to skip the difficult material on the grounds of 2 Timothy 3:16: every passage of Scripture is God-breathed and profitable. The chapter was delivered by Hosea after 733 BC, when Assyria had already begun occupying Israel's territory. The people rejoice in their sins, call the prophet a fool, and remain willfully blind to the impending catastrophe.\n\nPastor Lyro identifies four divine curses that are coming upon Israel: lack of provision, becoming strangers in a foreign land, not being accepted by God in worship, and barrenness. But the sermon's governing argument is that in each curse, believers in Christ today can see its opposite blessing—because through the Lord Jesus Christ we receive what Israel forfeited through unfaithfulness.\n\nThe curse of lack of provision contrasts with 2 Corinthians 9:8 and Romans 8:31-32: God makes all grace abound toward us and freely gives us all things with Christ. The curse of becoming strangers in a foreign land contrasts with Ephesians 2:11-22: those who were once far off are brought near by the blood of Christ and made citizens of the commonwealth of Israel, heirs of the covenants of promise. The curse of not being accepted by God contrasts with Ephesians 1: we have been accepted in the Beloved, and since Christ is always accepted, we will always be accepted. The curse of barrenness contrasts with Psalm 127: children are a heritage from the Lord, a sign of the continuation of God's work on earth.\n\nThe sermon closes with a Joshua 24 appeal: having surveyed the curses that come from unfaithfulness and the blessings that come from faith in Christ, the congregation is invited to make its choice. As for me and my house, we will serve the Lord."
