      "summary": "Preached on March 1, 2026, this sermon on 2 Corinthians 3:7-18 addresses two perennial dangers in the church — antinomianism (the idea that God doesn't care what we do as long as we believe) and legalism (the idea that our relationship with God depends on how well we keep His law). Paul deals with both by comparing the glory of the old covenant with the surpassing glory of the new covenant, demonstrating that the new covenant brings life, righteousness, clarity, and transformation by the Spirit.\n\nThe sermon traces five dimensions of glory through the passage. First, the old covenant was genuinely glorious — God Himself instituted it, and His glory was literally visible on Moses' face after he descended from Sinai. But it was a ministry of death and condemnation, not because the law was evil, but because sinful people broke it. Moses' face fading symbolized the temporary nature of the Mosaic administration, which was always designed to point forward to something greater. Second, the new covenant surpasses and permanently replaces the old: it is a ministry of the Spirit, brings righteousness rather than condemnation (Christ's righteousness declared ours), and remains forever — unlike the fading glory of Moses' face. Third, the new covenant's glory is openly revealed: new covenant ministers proclaim with great boldness, because the hope of glory produces confidence. The veil that lay over Israel's hearts is taken away in Christ — unbelief is not an intellectual problem but a willful suppression of truth. Fourth, where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty — freedom from condemnation, spiritual blindness, and the dominion of sin — for the Spirit applies the finished work of Christ to the believer. Fifth, believers with unveiled faces behold the glory of the Lord in Scripture and are progressively transformed into Christ's image, from glory to greater glory, by the Spirit of God.\n\nThe sermon closes with four practical applications: rest in Christ alone for justification; rejoice in the privilege of new covenant access to God; pursue holiness by beholding Christ in Scripture; and proclaim the gospel with boldness."
