      "outline": [
        {
          "point": "I. The Treasure We Carry (v. 7)",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. The treasure is 'the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ' — the gospel with its full personal and existential weight",
            "B. The Dead Sea Scrolls as an illustration: priceless contents in common clay jars",
            "C. God intentionally places priceless treasure into weak, fragile, replaceable vessels — primarily ministers of the gospel",
            "D. God's purpose: that no one mistakes where the power comes from — the excellence of the power is of God, not of us"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "II. The Pressure We Endure (vv. 8-9)",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. Four paradoxes of Christian ministry: hard pressed but not crushed; perplexed but not in despair; persecuted but not forsaken; struck down but not destroyed",
            "B. Paul's catalogue of sufferings: five times 39 lashes, three times beaten with rods, once stoned, three shipwrecks, imprisoned (2 Corinthians 11:22-33)",
            "C. God does not remove suffering but preserves his servants through it — sustaining grace in the midst of pressure",
            "D. 2 Corinthians 12:9: 'My grace is sufficient for you, for My strength is made perfect in weakness'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "III. The Life We Display (vv. 10-12)",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. 'Always carrying about in the body the dying of the Lord Jesus, that the life of Jesus also may be manifested in our body'",
            "B. Paul follows the example of Christ who laid down his life for the sheep — he lays down his life for the Corinthians",
            "C. Death works in Paul (suffering) so that life works in the Corinthians (the gospel reaches them)",
            "D. The resurrection as motivation: Paul endures because he knows there is a bodily life to come"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "IV. Four Ways to Live as Jars of Clay",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. Embrace your weakness instead of hiding it — our weakness is the stage on which God's power is displayed",
            "B. In difficulty, remember: hard pressed does not mean crushed; God promises sustaining grace",
            "C. Die to self in the small daily moments — choosing patience over anger, serving instead of demanding, forgiving instead of grudging",
            "D. Intentionally pour the treasure of the gospel into others — discipling, evangelizing, encouraging, teaching children"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Preaching from 2 Corinthians 4:1-12 on March 15, 2026, Pastor Lyro opens with the image of the Dead Sea Scrolls — priceless biblical manuscripts discovered in common clay jars in a cave near the Dead Sea in 1947, where they had been hidden for 2,000 years. The jar itself was worthless; the contents were priceless. This is exactly Paul's picture in verse 7: the gospel — 'the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ' — is placed by God in the fragile, ordinary, easily-broken vessels of human beings, especially ministers of the gospel. God's intentional purpose is that no one mistakes the source of the power: the excellence belongs entirely to God.\n\nThe sermon traces three movements in the passage: the treasure we carry (v. 7), the pressure we endure (vv. 8-9), and the life we display (vv. 10-12). The four paradoxes of verses 8-9 — hard pressed but not crushed, perplexed but not in despair, persecuted but not forsaken, struck down but not destroyed — are set against the backdrop of Paul's extraordinary personal suffering catalogued in 2 Corinthians 11. The pressure is real; but so is God's preserving grace. The sermon closes with four practical applications: embrace weakness instead of hiding it; trust God's sustaining grace in difficulty; die to self in ordinary daily moments; and intentionally pour the treasure of the gospel into others through evangelism, discipleship, and the teaching of children. The contrast between a dying star (supernova) that pours its energy into the universe and a black hole that implodes into itself serves as the closing illustration of the two kinds of lives available to those who carry Christ."
