      "summary": "This sermon opens a two-series parallel study through 2 John and 3 John. Pastor Tito Lyro introduces both letters and then preaches on 2 John 1-3. He establishes that both letters are the most formally letter-like epistles in the New Testament and that their shared author is the Apostle John, who calls himself 'the elder' to invoke ecclesiastical authority, not merely age. He explores the question of the 'elect lady' as recipient, arguing that the term refers to a local church (most likely Ephesus) rather than an individual woman, as internal evidence such as plural pronouns and church-level instructions supports this reading.\n\nThe sermon's theological center is the inseparability of truth and love. Drawing on Paul's definition in 1 Corinthians 13, Pastor Lyro argues that love divorced from truth is not love at all, and that cultural pressure to define love as uncritical affirmation of any desire is incompatible with biblical love. A simple logical test flows from verse 1: those who know the truth love the local church, and those who do not love the local church give evidence that they do not know the truth.\n\nPastor Lyro expands on why loving the local church is essential: Christ died for it, all the means of grace are found in it, members covenant with one another in it, and it is the primary gospel witness. John's greeting—'Grace, mercy and peace will be with you'—is presented as a declaration and promise that follows from loving in truth. The sermon closes with a pointed application: you cannot love Christ the Head while remaining indifferent to the body, and true love in truth manifests as both doctrinal commitment and tender care for God's people."
