      "summary": "This sermon focuses on 2 Corinthians 4:13-15, situating it within Paul and Timothy's context of suffering for the gospel. Though they are hard pressed, perplexed, persecuted, and struck down, they do not lose heart—and the text explains why. Pastor Tito Lyro develops two interlocking points: first, that faith speaks (because Paul and the psalmist before him spoke because they believed), and second, that because faith speaks, grace and thanksgiving to the glory of God increase.\n\nThe first point draws on Paul's quotation of Psalm 116:10—'I believed, therefore I spoke'—to show that speaking has always followed believing among God's people. The logic of Romans 10 reinforces this: hearing implies speaking, and the whole chain of salvation depends on people proclaiming what they believe. A Christian's words of gospel, encouragement, or rebuke will be filled with grace precisely because they flow from faith in Christ. To remain silent about the gospel is to act like a secret believer—a category Scripture does not recognize.\n\nThe second point locates Paul's ministerial motivation in the resurrection of the righteous at Christ's return. Paul can endure because he knows that the same God who raised Jesus will also raise him and present him alongside the Corinthians to the Father. This future certainty makes present conflicts and sufferings relatively trivial, and Paul urges the Corinthians to begin now the reconciliation they will enjoy forever. The passage closes with five applications: believe in Jesus, speak of Jesus, meditate on the glory of the resurrection, pursue reconciliation with fellow believers, and cultivate the thankfulness that flows from experiencing the grace of God."
