      "summary": "This sermon inaugurates a 26-part series through 2 Corinthians under the theme 'Power Perfected in Weakness.' Pastor Tito Lyro confines himself to just the first two verses, using them as the theological foundation for the entire letter and series. He establishes that when we read 2 Corinthians we are not reading ancient correspondence but hearing the living Christ address His church—a church that was real and deeply flawed, representing all of us.\n\nThe sermon works through four main elements of the greeting. First, Paul's apostleship: it rests not on his own success or charisma but on the will of God, making it both authoritative and humble. Second, the partnership with Timothy: their relationship models spiritual fatherhood, shared ministry, and ministerial humility. Paul outranks Timothy by every measure yet calls him 'our brother,' illustrating that authority in Christ's church never negates fraternity. Third, the identity of the church: addressed as 'the church of God in Corinth with all the saints in all Achaia,' the Corinthians are placed within a regional network of churches and reminded they belong not to themselves but to God. The saints in Corinth were not alone—and neither are we. Fourth, the greeting of grace and peace: not a mere formality but a theological doorway asserting that grace always precedes peace, that peace is covenantal reconciliation through justification by faith, and that God is responsible for salvation from beginning to end.\n\nPastor Lyro closes with six applications: submit to Christ's appointed Word; value ministry in difficult places; embrace shared relational leadership; love the church despite her flaws; rest in your identity as saints by calling; and live from grace already given rather than striving to earn more grace."
