      "summary": "In this opening Sunday evening lesson of a new series on spiritual discernment, Pastor Tito Lyro begins with Solomon's prayer in 1 Kings 3, where the young king asks not merely for wisdom but for a \"hearing heart\" that can discern between good and evil. Solomon's request illustrates that discernment is more than knowledge — it is a Spirit-given attunement to God's voice. The working definition offered for the series is: spiritual discernment is the skill of thinking biblically about life, or having a Christian worldview through which all of life is evaluated.\n\nThe first part of the lesson examines the serious consequences of lacking discernment. Drawing from Hebrews 5:11-14, Pastor Lyro argues that undiscernment is proof first of spiritual immaturity (becoming \"dull of hearing\"), then of backsliding (regressing from solid food to milk), and ultimately of spiritual death. He notes that Romans 1:31 places an \"undiscerning mind\" in the same list as murder, hatred, and other grave sins — showing that failing to think biblically is not a minor weakness but a direct offense against God.\n\nThe second part turns to the blessings of discernment. Those who fear the Lord and know Him possess the foundation for discernment, and growing Christians progressively exercise their senses to distinguish good from evil. Pastor Lyro closes by announcing that the coming weeks will develop a full worldview foundation and show practically how to apply biblical discernment to every area of life. The series is motivated by the conviction that healthy, living, maturing Christians are necessarily discerning Christians."
