      "summary": "Dr. John Battle, preaching as a guest while Pastor Tito Lyro was filling another pulpit, delivered this Sunday morning sermon on 1 Thessalonians 1. Noting that Paul begins his letter with thanksgiving and a confident declaration that he knows the Thessalonians are elect of God, Dr. Battle explores the question: How do we know that we are the elect of God? He argues that election has its source entirely in the sovereign will and grace of the Father — not in any foreseen faith or merit of the creature — and that this is clearly taught in Ephesians 1:3-6, where Paul says we were chosen \"according to the good pleasure of His will.\"\n\nThe sermon identifies three categories of evidence that God has elected a person. First, the gospel has come to them — the true gospel of Christ crucified, buried, and risen, received as the power of God unto salvation. Second, the gospel came with power and in the Holy Spirit, producing full assurance — for the faith to believe is itself the gift of the Spirit, and this faith, though it may waver in difficult seasons, is the mark of a regenerate heart. Third, that faith and the Spirit's work produce visible fruit: a work of faith (outward deeds consistent with the profession), a labor of love (effort that may be invisible to others but seen by God), and a patience of hope (endurance under trial because of confidence in Christ's return).\n\nDr. Battle closes by reflecting on how God uses instruments — preachers, witnesses, parents — as means of His election. Just as Paul and his team came to Thessalonica and lived as examples of the gospel among them, we are called to bear witness to Christ in our families, neighborhoods, and workplaces, trusting that God will use us as He wills to call His people."
