      "summary": "In this Sunday morning sermon from the series on the Ephesian Church, Pastor Tito Lyro addresses Ephesians 5:22-33, with the stated plan to spend four Sundays in this chapter. He begins not with the mechanics of the husband-wife relationship but with Paul's larger context: the family is the primary theater in which Christian virtue is displayed. Colossians 3 places the instructions for wives, husbands, children, and parents immediately after a long catalogue of Christian virtues — love, humility, meekness, long-suffering, forgiveness — indicating that the family is where these things must first be practiced.\n\nThe sermon then takes up the command for wives to submit to their own husbands. Pastor Lyro addresses the most common objections: that Paul was just accommodating his culture (disproven by his consistent counter-cultural stance on many other issues), and that the word does not really mean submission (disproven by the word's consistent use throughout the New Testament, including in Jesus' submission to his parents and the Christian's submission to governing authorities). The command means that the wife is the submitting partner who defers to her husband — in everything, with acknowledged exceptions when the husband commands what is contrary to God's Word, and with the clear affirmation that no wife is called to submit to abuse.\n\nThe sermon ends with a careful and extended defense of the dignity of womanhood. Submission speaks only to function, not to worth. The greatest act of submission in history is the Son's submission to the Father — yet the Son was never inferior to the Father in essence. Male and female are equally made in the image of God, equally co-heirs of grace. The transgender movement actually degrades women by implying that womanhood is merely subjective feeling. God's design for women is glorious precisely because it comes from the God who only gives what is good."
