      "summary": "In this Sunday morning sermon on Matthew 5:8 — \"Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God\" — Pastor Tito Lyro organizes the beatitude around the theological framework of the \"already and not yet.\" Since Christ's resurrection and ascension, believers both already are pure in heart (positionally, by virtue of the new birth and union with Christ) and are becoming pure in heart (progressively, through sanctification by the Spirit). Both truths must be held together.\n\nThe \"already\" is established through the new covenant promise of Ezekiel 36, where God pledges to sprinkle clean water on His people and give them a new heart — a promise fulfilled in regeneration. Because of our union with Christ, the Father sees us as holy and pure, just as He sees the Son. Paul's list in 1 Corinthians 6:9-11 — adulterers, fornicators, homosexuals, covetous, drunkards, extortioners — is introduced with \"such were some of you,\" but the identity of believers is now defined not by past sin but by the washing, sanctification, and justification accomplished in Christ.\n\nThe \"not yet\" concerns the progressive work of sanctification. Because believers have been given new hearts, they now pursue purity — and there is no true pursuit of purity without first having a pure heart. This pursuit is not achieved through extraordinary gifts or new revelation but through the ordinary means of grace: reading, hearing, memorizing, and obeying the Word; prayer; accountability within the body of Christ; and attending the sacraments. The sermon closes with the vision of 1 John 3:2 — \"when He is revealed, we shall be like Him, for we shall see Him as He is\" — as the ultimate fulfillment of this beatitude."
