      "summary": "In this opening Sunday morning sermon of a new series on Psalm 119, Pastor Tito Lyro introduces what he calls the \"Mount Everest of the Psalms\" — the 176-verse acrostic masterpiece that stands at the heart of the Bible. He candidly admits to having planned and feared this series for about two years before beginning it, citing the Psalm's sheer length, the enormous wealth of scholarly commentary on it, and the inadequacy he felt in approaching something that humbled Augustine, Calvin, and Spurgeon alike.\n\nThe sermon identifies the dual subject of the Psalm: it is about the Word of God (referenced nearly 180 times through eight Hebrew synonyms — law, testimonies, precepts, statutes, commandments, judgments, and two words for \"word\") and about the God of the Word (addressed directly approximately 275 times). The poem is structured as a Hebrew acrostic — 22 stanzas of 8 verses each, one stanza for every letter of the Hebrew alphabet — presenting what the pastor describes as the \"A through Z\" of Holy Scripture, a complete and exhaustive tribute to its subject.\n\nPastor Lyro connects the Psalm to the Reformation principle of Sola Scriptura, noting that Calvin ordered the altar to be removed from the center of the sanctuary and replaced with a pulpit bearing an open Bible. The Psalm calls for Scripture alone to hold the central place in the heart of every believer. He closes by noting that the Psalm was written in a context of fierce personal persecution — yet its author in verse after verse turns not to his enemies or his circumstances but to the Word of God. It is this passion that the series hopes to cultivate in the congregation."
