      "summary": "In this Sunday evening exposition of Psalm 119:121-128 (the 'Ayin' stanza), Pastor Tito Lyro addresses what he calls \"the humble brag\" — the Psalmist's opening declaration in verse 121, \"I have done justice and righteousness,\" which at first sounds like self-righteous boasting. Pastor Lyro argues that this is in fact a declaration of grace-driven obedience: the Psalmist knows that any righteousness he has displayed is itself God's work in him, and he therefore appeals to God to be faithful to His own work by delivering him from his oppressors.\n\nThe sermon works through the stanza thematically. The oppressor of the believer is identified as \"the proud\" — the insolent, presumptuous person in rebellion against God, a category the Psalmist includes us in every time we sin. The Psalmist asks God to be his surety or guarantor, just as God acted as Abraham's guarantor in Genesis 15 by walking between the divided animals alone.\n\nVerse 123 — \"my eyes fail from seeking your salvation\" — is unpacked as the believer's characteristic posture of longing: looking back to Christ already come (all the promises are yes and amen in Him) and looking forward to His return. Titus 2:11-14 is brought in to show that both past grace (the first coming) and future grace (the second coming) teach us to live godly, sober lives in the present. The sermon closes with the Psalmist's declaration that he loves God's commandments above gold, considers all God's precepts right, and hates every false way — a hatred that is not emotional but informed and Scripture-driven, and that calls believers to educate themselves in the truth so they can identify and oppose falsehood."
