      "summary": "In the third part of his series, Mr. Isaiah Couch turns to the Covenant of Works — the one he confesses wrestled with the most in his Reformed journey. Coming out of dispensationalism and Arminianism, he initially believed the doctrine was absent from Scripture and made God's relationship with Adam purely transactional and loveless. This lecture traces how those objections dissolved as he worked through the writings of covenant theologians and examined Scripture afresh.\n\nCouch establishes that while the title 'Covenant of Works' appears in the late 16th century, the substance of the doctrine is found in Ursinus, Luther, and Augustine. He answers the dispensationalist objection (Ryrie) that covenant theology forces the New Testament back into the Old by pointing out that the New Testament itself does exactly this — interpreting Adam, Moses, and David as types fulfilled in Christ. He also engages John Murray's Reformed objection, showing that Murray's own description of the 'Adamic administration' contains all the hallmarks of a covenant.\n\nThe lecture's theological center is the distinction between Adam's natural state as a creaturely being (Genesis 2:7-9) and the covenant relationship God graciously condescended to establish with him (Genesis 2:15-17). Drawing on Voss and Tipton, Couch explains that Adam had no inherent claim to eternal life — God voluntarily offered it on the condition of obedience. Far from being loveless, this was an act of extraordinary divine condescension. The probationary tree, Tipton argues, was God's invitation to Adam to live by every word from his mouth rather than by bread alone.\n\nThe climax of the lecture is the contrast between the two federal heads. Adam's violation plunged all humanity into death and guilt. But Christ, the true and better Adam, recapitulates and reverses Adam's failure. His wilderness temptation mirrors Adam's garden test, and the very temptations hurled at him on the cross echo Satan's assault in Eden. Christ remains obedient in every test, advances from probationary life to consummated life, and his perfect covenant-keeping is imputed to all who are united to him by faith."
