      "summary": "In this Sunday morning address, Mr. Isaiah Couch shares his personal journey into covenant theology, having been asked by the session to speak on what God has taught him. He opens with Psalm 25:10-14 — \"The secret of the Lord is with those who fear Him, and He will show them His covenant\" — and sets out to survey three foundational covenants: the covenant of redemption, the covenant of works, and the covenant of grace.\n\nMr. Couch begins by establishing what a covenant is, drawing on O. Palmer Robertson's definition: \"a bond in blood sovereignly administered.\" He walks through Genesis 15, where God cuts a covenant with Abraham, noting the striking detail that God alone passes between the divided animals — no two parties walking together, but one sovereign Lord pledging Himself. This points forward to Christ walking the path of blood alone at Calvary as the covenant's ultimate fulfillment.\n\nThe covenant of redemption (Pactum Salutis) is presented as foundational — the eternal, intra-Trinitarian agreement in which the Father elects a people, the Son undertakes to accomplish their redemption through perfect obedience and substitutionary death, and the Spirit applies that work to the elect. Ephesians 1:3-14 is expounded as the New Testament's fullest expression of this Trinitarian work. The covenant of works is then explained through Genesis 2:15-17: Adam was given a probationary command, the keeping of which would have advanced him from a state of probation into a state of glory. Adam's failure as humanity's covenantal head means that all his posterity inherit guilt and death. This necessitates a new covenantal head — Christ, who fulfills the covenant of works through perfect obedience and bears its curse in death, thereby securing for His people both forgiveness and the advancement to glory that Adam forfeited. The covenant of grace is traced from Genesis 3:15 forward through redemptive history, characterized by God supplying what He demands."
