      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from the Nun section of Psalm 119 (verses 105-112), exploring the great Reformation theme that the Word of God is a lamp to our feet and a light to our path. He opens with Thomas Guthrie's famous description of the Bible — armory, laboratory, mine, guidebook — before tracing how the Protestant Reformation recovered the Bible from the medieval church's suppression and shone it as a lamp on the path of millions. Geneva's motto under Calvin, 'post tenebras lux' (after darkness, light), captures the spirit of what Scripture does: it leads those who are walking in the dark.\n\nThe sermon's central image is the ancient traveler with a small clay oil lamp, illuminating only the ground immediately in front of him. Pastor Lyro draws from this the pastoral insight that God promises grace for the present moment, not a script for years ahead. Matthew 6:33-34 supports this: seek first the kingdom today, and do not borrow tomorrow's trouble. The Bible gives life-encompassing guidance through its principles while not mapping out specific decisions down the road. Anxiety tries to live tomorrow today; the lamp says, here is the next step.\n\nA crucial section addresses who can actually use this lamp: only those who believe in Christ and are illuminated by the Holy Spirit. The natural mind cannot receive the things of the Spirit (1 Corinthians 2). Pastor Lyro shares his own conversion — from staunch evolutionist to a man who could see nothing but God's truth in Scripture — as evidence that the Spirit's illumination, not new information, is what makes the Bible light. He then turns to Proverbs 6:20-24 to address parents directly: tying the Word around your neck means letting it govern your head. The question for every parent is not whether their child makes Harvard or learns table manners, but whether they know Jesus Christ.\n\nThe sermon closes with a practical taxonomy of how the Word guides: direct commands (no prayer needed — just obey), principles derived from Scripture, the character of God as a pattern for our own, and biblical examples God approves or disapproves. Memorization is essential. The Psalmist's commitment in verse 112 — to incline his heart to God's statutes forever to the very end — is offered as the model. And the path of obedience, Pastor Lyro insists, is not a gloomy duty but the path of joy forevermore, because that is where Jesus lives."
