      "summary": "In this sermon on Hosea 14, Pastor Tito Lyro preaches the final chapter of the book as God's last call to a people on the brink of Assyrian conquest. Even after Israel's child sacrifices and idolatry, even when Assyria stands at the gate, God calls his people to return to him. The sermon's key insight is that this is who our God is: the Lord is not slack concerning his promise, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance (2 Peter 3:9). There is hope of repentance until the last breath.\n\nPastor Lyro walks through Hosea's four-point structure: the call to repentance (vv. 1-2), the confession of sin (vv. 2-3), the commitment to forgive (vv. 4-7), and the care of God (v. 8). The sermon is especially pastoral on the nature of repentance: it is a command, not a suggestion; it has three necessary elements (comprehension, confession, and choice); and it has seven recognizable traits (God-focused, hates sin not consequences, accepts responsibility, etc.). The seven practical elements of biblical confession are presented as tools — not laws — for practicing genuine reconciliation.\n\nThe heart of the sermon is God's response to repentance in verses 4-7, where he bursts forth in promises of healing and restoration with such richness of imagery — lily, Lebanon, olive tree, vine, wine — that we can almost hear the joy in his voice. Pastor Lyro connects this to the three parables of Luke 15: the lost sheep, the lost coin, and the prodigal son, all of which culminate in the Father running to embrace the returning sinner. This is our God, and this is our pattern for forgiving others.\n\nThe sermon closes with a personal note: on the way to church the previous day, Pastor Lyro saw a nephew who had been in the grip of substance abuse for fifteen years. The message of Hosea 14 is for families with prodigals — there is hope, because we have a God who forgives and restores as long as there is breath in the lungs. Parents with covenant children who have walked away are urged to claim God's promise and to pray, for God is the Lord of the harvest."
