      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from Psalm 12, a Psalm without a specific historical occasion, which he describes as an expression of the general experience of the godly in every age. The Psalm moves from despair to joy without any change in external circumstances — the wicked are equally present at the end as at the beginning. What changes is not the situation but the Psalmist's thinking: he turns to God, remembers God's promises, and his perspective is transformed.\n\nThe sermon identifies the opening as a pattern for the godly in every culture: Help, Lord. When lies are spoken loudly and the faithful are silent, the world feels as though the godly have disappeared. Pastor Lyro draws an extended parallel to the present moment in America, where ideologies representing a tiny fraction of the population dominate public discourse precisely because those who know the truth have stayed quiet. The Psalmist's charge is both diagnosis and call: we are lying when we fail to speak what is true, and God hates lying.\n\nThe second major movement of the sermon is Ephesians 4:25-32, which Pastor Lyro reads as Paul's practical instruction for how to speak truth in a lying culture. The passage covers truthful speech, righteous anger, honest work, edifying words, and the absence of bitterness. Bitterness in the church — holding grudges, refusing forgiveness — is itself a form of lying. The person who is bitter is acting as if Christ's forgiveness does not apply, and this too is how the godly testify to or against the truth.\n\nThe sermon closes by affirming the Psalmist's pattern: the only thing that changed between verse 1 and verse 8 was how the Psalmist thought about God. He went from depression to joy without the wicked going anywhere. Peace and joy are found in Christ, not in circumstances. Meditating on whatever is true, noble, just, pure, lovely, and of good report — Philippians 4:8-9 — is the path to experiencing the God of peace."
