      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches an evening sermon from 1 John 2:1-2, a passage that balances the call to holiness with the comfort of Christ's ongoing advocacy. He opens with a famous Bob Newhart comedy sketch in which a therapist's entire therapeutic method is two words — Stop it — to illustrate the point that at a basic level, the solution for sin in the Christian life is simple: do not sin. But he carefully distinguishes simple from easy, and addresses the objections of those who believe telling Christians not to sin is either naively impossible or illegitimately legalistic.\n\nThe sermon's first movement defends the legitimacy of commanding Christians to obey. Grace is given precisely so that we can obey (Titus 2:11-12); election is unto obedience (1 Peter 1); and the end goal of the gospel is a people zealous for good works. Legalism is not the same as serious obedience — it only occurs when one trusts in obedience as the grounds of divine acceptance. The apostle John's pastoral tenderness in addressing his congregation as his little children further shows that the call to holiness is delivered with love, not harsh demand.\n\nThe heart of the sermon treats the nature of Christ's advocacy and propitiation. Christ stands before the Father as the righteous one — his righteousness is the basis on which he can plead for sinners. He is not pleading against the Father's anger but was sent by the Father out of love to be the propitiation (1 John 4:10). The term propitiation connects to the mercy seat in the most holy place: Christ is not only the high priest who brings the blood, he is the blood, and he is the mercy seat on which the blood is poured. Romans 3:22-26 is read as showing that God himself justifies the one who has faith in Jesus, looking at the Son and seeing the believer in him.\n\nThe sermon closes with a liberating application: obedience looks the same for every category of person — single, married, young, old. It is faith in Christ, trusting that he stands before the Father for you, resulting in hard work to obey, and when you fail, turning to Christ to be forgiven and continuing. The knowledge that you have an advocate frees you to risk for Christ without paralyzing fear, because even when you fail, he forgives."
