      "summary": "This is a firsthand missionary report to the congregation from Teenie Lyro, who served approximately four years in Lebanon. The presentation takes the form of a slide-show talk with photos narrating her ministry journey. She begins with an appeal to young people facing decisions about service, encouraging them that the fear and sense of sacrifice they feel is normal — everything laid at the foot of the cross for Christ's sake is worth it in the eternal perspective.\n\nThe bulk of the report describes two phases of ministry. The first was high-volume work serving hundreds of people, learning the language, building relationships, and learning to say goodbye as people moved in and out of her life. The second phase shifted to low-volume, intensive relational ministry focused on a small group of families and a cluster of children with disabilities. The project of working with disabled children — getting them recognized, enrolled in schools, and building community with one another — was the most fruitful and emotional part of the ministry. The week before leaving, one of the children demonstrated that she could read, prompting tears of shock and joy.\n\nThe report is honest about the hardships: Lebanon's economic collapse, a national revolution, the COVID border shutdown of 2020, gas lines stretching twelve hours in hundred-degree heat. One family ate meat only on the Thursday the missionary visited. The economic crisis that reduced normal meals to a luxury also opened doors, as desperate people began to let down their facades and speak honestly — which opened conversations about the gospel.\n\nTeenie reflects on the spiritual challenges of missionary life: the experience of not belonging anywhere, the loneliness of living alone in a foreign country, the grief of the church split that felt like years of labor had crumbled. She found encouragement through a Psalm 127 reflection — unless the Lord builds the house — and through an unexpected Zoom prayer meeting with a stranger in an identical situation. She closes by drawing on Jeremiah 29:11 in its proper context: a promise to exiles, and since all believers are exiles (1 Peter 1), the promise of God's plans to prosper his people applies to this ministry and to her ongoing transition."
