      "summary": "Guest speaker Pastor Doug Leaman, who serves as a missionary in Brazil with the congregation's support, preaches from Proverbs 16:18-21 on the subject of pride. The sermon opens by noting that the Reformed doctrine of total depravity stands in direct opposition to the world's prevailing orthodoxy of self-esteem and positive thinking. Like a mousetrap spring under tension, the sinful heart will fall back into pride unless continuously pressed open by the Spirit, prayer, and the exercise of godliness.\n\nPastor Leaman surveys the theological debate over which sin is the chief sin — unbelief (Brakel), pride (Augustine and Ambrose), or idolatry — and suggests these may not be alternatives but rather a cluster, with pride as the chief idol of the heart. He spends significant time in Ezekiel 28, where the king of Tyre is described as engaged in ego-latria (self-worship), and where the prophet appears to look through the king to Satan's original fall — iniquity was found in him, his heart was lifted up because of his splendor. Pride, Pastor Leaman argues, is the sin of centering the entire world around oneself rather than around God's glory and others' good.\n\nThe sermon turns to Hosea 2 to connect pride with idolatry. The adulterous wife pursues her false lovers because she believes they will bring her bread and water — life and prosperity. This is precisely how all idolatry works: we love false gods because we trust they will serve our self-interest. God's response is to hedge up her way with thorns — to bring hardship that breaks the power of the false loves and drives the heart back to him. This disciplining love is how God deals with pride in the lives of his people.\n\nPastor Leaman closes by warning against multiplied consequences of pride — in marriages, families, churches, and nations — before pointing to the antidote in Philippians 2. Christ humbled himself to death on the cross; his righteousness and humility become ours by faith. The congregation is urged not to be destroyed by seeing pride in their own hearts but to rejoice in Christ's perfect work and rise up to serve him, knowing that his obedience has been credited to them."
