      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from Psalm 4, which he introduces as historically paired with Psalm 5 in Hebrew morning and evening liturgy — Psalm 4 as the evening Psalm, ending with David lying down in peace and sleep. The occasion was likely the persecution under either Saul or Absalom, and the Psalmist Charles Spurgeon called it another choice flower from the garden of affliction, noting that much of Scripture would not exist were it not for the afflictions of the saints.\n\nThe sermon identifies the Psalm's three-part structure: a prayer to God (v. 1), a call to man (vv. 2-5), and a contrast between the peace of God and the peace of men (vv. 6-8). Nothing changes in the external circumstances between verse 1 and verse 8. The wicked are still prowling. What changes is how the Psalmist is thinking — and that transformation of thinking moves him from despair to joy, from sleeplessness to rest.\n\nA significant portion of the sermon focuses on Psalm 4:1, where David calls God the God of my righteousness. Pastor Lyro argues this reflects the Old Testament saints' awareness of alien righteousness — they did not stand before God on the basis of their own moral performance but on the basis of a righteousness God counted as theirs. Romans 3:25-26 confirms that God passed over the sins of Old Testament saints knowing his Son would pay for them. This connects the evening Psalm to Paul's letter and to the ongoing intercession of Christ (Hebrews 7:25, Romans 8:34).\n\nThe sermon closes with a pastoral application: the secret of David's peace is not better circumstances but right thinking about God. The enemies in verse 6 cry out for material prosperity — grain and wine. David cries out for the countenance of God, and receives gladness that exceeds any material increase. Verse 8 — I will both lie down in peace and sleep — is presented as the great pastoral promise of the Psalm: the people of God can act boldly and rest peacefully because the Lord alone is their security."
