      "summary": "Preaching from Hebrews 3:7-19, Pastor Tito Lyro sets the passage within the broader argument of Hebrews: the pastor-author is writing to a congregation flirting with abandoning Christ to return to Judaism, and he pleads with them — as a father pleads with a child heading toward destruction — to consider Jesus, because Christ is better than everything they could find outside of him. The immediate passage consists of the longest continuous Old Testament quotation in the New Testament apart from the Jeremiah 31 passage in Hebrews 8, drawn from Psalm 95:7-11 about Israel's wilderness rebellion.\n\nPastor Lyro explains why this ancient Psalm applies directly to the church today using two arguments: all biblical history is the church's history (1 Corinthians 10:11; Romans 15:4), and Christians are participants in a new Exodus through the cross of Christ (Luke 9:30-31). Our baptism into Christ is the fulfillment of Israel's crossing of the Red Sea (1 Corinthians 10:1-2), and our feeding on Christ by faith fulfills Israel's nourishment with manna. This present life is our wilderness wandering — we are pilgrims and sojourners (1 Peter 2:11) journeying toward our eternal rest, and we are prone to become attached to the things of this world as if they were our final home.\n\nThe sermon focuses on the first of four administrations the Holy Spirit gives in the passage: listen to the Spirit in order to avoid hardening your heart. To harden the heart is to disobey God's voice and act according to one's own desires — and the more one does this, the easier it becomes (the seared conscience Paul describes). Israel's wilderness journey was marked from beginning to end by grumbling and complaining, and the result was catastrophic: every man over twenty who left Egypt died in the wilderness — approximately ninety deaths a day for forty years. The application is direct: the people of God today are in their own wilderness wandering, and the Spirit urges them to examine whether their hearts are hardened in grumbling or softened in thanksgiving, whether they are looking toward the Promised Land or longingly back toward Egypt."
