      "summary": "Preached on the occasion of a baptism in the congregation, Pastor Tito Lyro directs a word to fathers — and to all who have a stake in raising the next generation — from 1 Thessalonians 2:10-12. Paul's ministry to the Thessalonians served as the pattern of a faithful father: he had a clear goal for them, he conducted himself before them with integrity, and he taught them through exhortation, comfort, and emphatic charge.\n\nThe sermon opens with a frank assessment of the crisis of fatherhood in both culture and church. Citing George Barna's research, Pastor Lyro notes that four out of ten evangelical parents list a good education as their top goal for their children — while facilitating a meaningful relationship with Christ ranks third. The biblical goal is clear: that children would walk worthy of God who calls them into his kingdom and glory. The Word of God must function as the plumb line for all fathering decisions.\n\nPaul's conduct before the Thessalonians (v. 10) models what children must see in their fathers: genuine devotion to God, righteousness governed by Scripture, and blamelessness that produces a clear conscience. His teaching (v. 11) involved three activities that must be present in every father's relationship with each child individually: exhortation (instruction aimed at practice), comfort (coming alongside with encouragement and genuine pastoral care), and charging (the emphatic demand that produces obedience, enforced through biblical discipline). The sermon addresses spanking directly and without apology, grounding it in Proverbs and noting that the pastoral reluctance to apply the rod is based on cultural accommodation rather than Scripture.\n\nThe sermon closes by pointing fathers under conviction to the Heavenly Father as both model and hope. God does the very things for his people that Paul describes, and those who had no faithful earthly father can look to him as the perfect Father who never abandons his children."
