      "summary": "In this second part of a two-session study on Genesis 22, Elder Scott Hollander delivers a structured and textually rich examination of the themes, typology, and covenant context of Abraham's binding of Isaac. The lesson opens with a geographical argument: the mountains of Moriah, where Abraham offered Isaac, are the same region where Solomon built the temple (2 Chronicles 3:1), where David offered sacrifices at Araunah's threshing floor (2 Samuel 24), and where scholars believe Jesus was crucified — Golgotha, now inside the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, outside the ancient city walls.\n\nThe core argument of the lesson is that Genesis 22 presents Isaac as a type of Christ in remarkable detail: miraculous birth, only son, three-day figurative death and resurrection (Hebrews 11:17-19), carrying his own sacrificial wood, willingly submitting to the will of his father, and becoming the means by which all nations are blessed. Elder Hollander demonstrates through a Hebrew vocabulary chart that the passage is dominated by nine instances of seeing vocabulary — the root *ra'ah* and the interjection *hinneh* — establishing the theme 'Abraham sees that Yahweh sees.'\n\nThe title Yahweh Yireh (Genesis 22:14), commonly rendered Jehovah Jireh and translated 'the LORD provides,' is shown to mean literally 'Yahweh he will see.' The English words 'video' and 'provide' both trace to the Latin root for seeing (*videre*), demonstrating that to provide is to see ahead and act on what one sees. God's providence and provision are rooted in his divine foresight and foreordination.\n\nThe lesson concludes with analysis of the re-expression of the Abrahamic covenant in verses 17-18, including newly added promises (the seashore simile, possession of the enemies' gate), and with close attention to the internal repetitions of 'only son,' 'took,' and 'went together' that thread through the narrative with theological significance."
