      "summary": "In this first session of a two-part study, Elder Scott Hollander introduces Genesis 22:1-19 through the lens of John 8:56, where Jesus declares that Abraham 'rejoiced that he would see my day; he saw it and was glad.' Most commentators connect this vision to the Abrahamic covenant appearances in Genesis 12-17, but Elder Hollander argues — drawing on Syriac church fathers and careful attention to the text — that Jesus was specifically pointing back to Genesis 22, where Abraham saw the day of Christ through the typological events of the binding of Isaac.\n\nThe session walks verse by verse through the passage, examining the significance of the test announcement in verse 1, the remarkable redundancy of 'your son, your only son Isaac whom you love' and why Isaac counts as Abraham's only son despite Ishmael's existence, and the three-day figurative death of Isaac from the moment God gave the command (interpreted explicitly by Hebrews 11:17-19 as Abraham expecting literal death and resurrection). The lesson also explores Abraham's statement to the young men — 'we will come back to you' — as a declaration of faith in resurrection, and verse 8's 'God will provide for himself the lamb' as emphasizing God as the actor while possibly being prophetic beyond Abraham's understanding at that moment.\n\nThe session slows over verses 9-12, noting the deliberate narrative deceleration and possible Hebrew wordplay between *malach* (angel) and *ma'achelet* (knife), and introduces the question of what it means for Abraham to have 'stretched out his hand' — whether reaching for the knife or placing his hand on Isaac's head in a gesture from Leviticus 1:4. The session closes by previewing the next lesson's topics: the full meaning of Yahweh Yireh, Mount Moriah's geographical connection to the crucifixion, and the covenant expansions of verses 17-18."
