      "summary": "Continuing the Abuse series, this fourth session examines how to minister to those who have been abused, opening with a matrix of all possible household configurations — Christian/Christian, Christian/unbeliever, unbeliever/Christian, unbeliever/unbeliever — and what each combination implies for how the church and civil authorities can be involved. The critical determinant is who is a Christian, defined here as someone willing to be identified with and accountable to a local church. The session describes patterns common to abusive husbands and abusive wives across these configurations.\n\nThe core of the lesson establishes a goal that goes beyond the secular counseling model. The secular approach aims to stop the hurt and stop the harm. The biblical approach affirms these objectives while adding two others: heart holiness in both the abused and the abuser, and winning the abuser over to righteousness. The prevalent assumption in most counseling today — even in integration models — is that an abuser cannot fundamentally change; Pastor Lyro identifies this as more influenced by sobriety movements than by Scripture. The Spirit of God is not limited by anyone's sin.\n\nThe session then addresses the question the abused person is often thinking but may not state: where was God when this was happening? The answer is drawn from Psalm 119 (which was written in the context of affliction and persecution), Psalm 10, Psalm 82, Jeremiah, and the story of Joseph. God will defend the cause of the oppressed — not always in this life, but certainly in the life to come. God promises to sustain us through affliction rather than exempt us from it, and the affliction itself can be a sanctifying instrument. The session also addresses what God wants of the abused person: trust in him and his providential protection, reliance on the institutions he has ordained, and in the case of the abused wife, 'aggressive submission' grounded in the beauty of God's created order while never abandoning the self-protection principle of the sixth commandment."
