      "summary": "Preaching from Matthew 5:38-48, Pastor Tito Lyro examines Jesus's teaching on radical love in the Sermon on the Mount — a passage often admired as fine ethical teaching until one reaches the closing demand to be as perfect as God the Father. The sermon begins by establishing that Jesus is not abolishing or replacing Old Testament law but correcting the religious leaders' distortions of it. The formula 'you have heard it said' refers to the traditions of the scribes and Pharisees, not to Moses. Jesus is going back to the root — the radical — of what following God means: a love that loves even enemies.\n\nThe sermon unpacks four specific expressions of love toward enemies commanded in verse 44: love your enemies with active compassion (thinking of them as sinners who need Christ, not merely obstacles to your comfort); bless those who curse you by responding to verbal attacks with kind words; do good to those who hate you by actively serving them; and pray for those who persecute you — sincerely, for their blessing and conversion. The model is Jesus himself, who prayed from the cross 'Father, forgive them,' and whose prayer was answered weeks later when thousands who had called for his crucifixion were converted at Pentecost.\n\nPastor Lyro addresses common misreadings of 'turn the other cheek,' making clear it does not require pacifism (John the Baptist affirmed soldiers' calling; Romans 13 gives the state the sword) or passive acceptance of ongoing abuse (an abused wife should call the police and then the elders). The backhanded slap to the right cheek was an ancient insult, and Jesus is primarily addressing how we respond to personal affronts driven by wounded pride. The sermon closes by grounding the call to radical love in the character of God himself — who sends rain on the just and unjust — and inviting the congregation to identify someone who has wronged them and do something kind for that person this week."
