      "summary": "Launching a new series on the first year of Jesus' public ministry — primarily covering John 1 through 4 with material from Matthew, Mark, and Luke — Pastor Tito Lyro begins at Mark 1:1 to examine the foundational claim: this is the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God. The sermon provides essential historical orientation: Jesus was born around 5 BC (Dionysius Exiguus miscalculated by five years in AD 525), his three years of public ministry run from approximately AD 26 to AD 30, and the most chronologically reliable Gospel is John, which enables scholars to establish the timeline from the number of Passovers it records.\n\nThe word 'gospel' is unpacked in both its Roman cultural sense (joyful tidings that change the world) and its Jewish theological sense (good news that the promises of God are about to be fulfilled). Together these yield the sermon's working definition: the gospel is joyful news that the promises of God are about to be fulfilled in a way that will change the world. Importantly, the gospel of Jesus Christ is not merely his death and resurrection but the entire story of his life — everything Mark records is good news.\n\nThe sermon also develops what the title 'Son of God' means. The religious leaders of Jesus's day understood it as a claim to equality with God, which is why they sought to kill him (John 5:17-23). The title means equal in nature, power, authority, and glory with the Father. It is on the confession that Jesus is the Son of the living God that Christ promised to build his church (Matthew 16). The sermon closes by noting that Mark inseparably ties the gospel of Christ to the preparatory ministry of John the Baptist, who is the fulfillment of both the Malachi forerunner promise and the Isaiah voice-in-the-wilderness promise."
