      "summary": "This sermon in the 1 John series addresses the third test of genuine faith: do you love those who belong to God? Pastor Tito Lyro opens with the contrast between the world's view of love—captured in Tina Turner's 1984 hit 'What's Love Got to Do with It,' which counsels against letting love into relationships for fear of a broken heart—and John's insistence that love is essential, not optional, for those who know Christ.\n\nThe sermon establishes that the basis for Christian love is divine love: Christ laid down His life for us (v. 16), which is both the foundation that makes our love possible and the pattern our love is to follow. Pastor Lyro carefully traces John's distinction between Satan's family (exemplified by Cain, whose jealousy and self-centeredness led to murder) and God's family (whose members are to love the brethren sacrificially). He emphasizes that laying down one's life includes giving material resources, and that John pointedly uses the singular 'brother' to prevent the abstraction of love into a vague feeling for 'the brethren' as a faceless group.\n\nPractical application covers the 'proximity principle' (love those who are close to you), the 46 unique 'one another' commands of the New Testament, and the call to ask whether one's life is characterized by giving or by taking. The sermon closes by noting that genuine Christian love for other Christians actually provokes the world's hatred—because it reminds the world of Christ's love, which the unregenerate heart recoils from. The answer to Tina Turner's question: love has everything to do with it."
