      "summary": "In this Sunday morning sermon, Pastor Tito Lyro preaches on Acts 20:32-38 as a farewell message for a family — Tonya, Sadie, Ezra, and Eli — who are leaving the congregation after nearly two decades to move to North Carolina. Drawing on Paul's emotional parting with the Ephesian elders, he reflects on what it means for Christians to say goodbye and what Paul placed before his beloved Ephesians as his final words.\n\nPastor Lyro begins by establishing that the pain of farewell is real and right — when someone leaves, a piece of us goes with them. But the Christian goodbye is different because the bond of brotherhood in Christ is not dissolved by distance. Citing Ephesians 4, he reminds the congregation that believers share one body, one Spirit, one Lord, one faith — an uncommon union, like what Jonathan Edwards described of his marriage to Sarah, that transcends geography. The departing family and those who remain are still co-laborers for the glory of God and the discipling of the nations.\n\nThe heart of the sermon is Paul's farewell word in Acts 20:32: \"I commend you to God and to the word of His grace.\" Pastor Lyro explores the word 'commend' — to place before someone, to commit to another's care — and argues that this act of setting God and His Word before one another ought to characterize all Christian relationships, not merely moments of departure. We cannot give to others what we do not have; the only way to commend God's Word to others is to be so filled with it that it overflows when people encounter us, as Psalm 119 describes.\n\nThe sermon closes with the inheritance promised through the Word to all who are sanctified: God Himself as the ultimate inheritance, along with freedom from sin, eternal life, and the new heavens and earth. The congregation then gathered to pray over the departing family, committing them to God and His grace as they begin their new chapter."
