      "summary": "This sermon on 1 John 5:6-13 takes its organizing image from the courtroom: John lines up a series of witnesses whose testimonies cannot be refuted to prove that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, fully human and fully divine, the only Savior of the world. Pastor Tito Lyro notes a textual issue in verse 7 (the Comma Johanneum), explaining that the longer reading is a late addition not found in early manuscripts, while assuring the congregation that its content is true and that the passage's meaning is unchanged by its inclusion or exclusion.\n\nThe sermon examines each witness in turn. The water and the blood (baptism and crucifixion) testify that the same Jesus who was baptized was also crucified—refuting the Gnostic heresy that a divine Christ merely inhabited the man Jesus temporarily. The Holy Spirit testifies through the Word, and the Spirit and the Word cannot be separated. The Father testified at the baptism and transfiguration and through the whole sweep of Scripture. All three earthly witnesses—Spirit, water, and blood—agree as one. To deny them is to call God a liar.\n\nPastor Lyro draws an important application: the false teachers went wrong because they filtered Scripture through neo-Platonic philosophy (the physical is inferior to the spiritual) rather than interpreting all of life through the lens of Scripture. This same error produces logically absurd conclusions in our own day, such as the claim that a person's identity is divorced from their body. The sermon concludes by unpacking the testimony itself—God has given us eternal life, and this life is in His Son—and affirming that John wrote this passage for the comfort and assurance of believers, that they may know they have eternal life."
