      "summary": "This sermon on Matthew 5:7 was prompted by the explosion in Beirut and the remarkable mercy shown by Lebanese Christians in its aftermath. Pastor Tito Lyro uses the occasion to consider the fifth beatitude: \"Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy.\" He traces the internal logic of the Beatitudes—from poverty of spirit, to mourning, to meekness, to hungering and thirsting for righteousness—showing how the Beatitudes first drive us inward to see our own emptiness before turning us outward in mercy toward others.\n\nThe sermon distinguishes two movements that must be held together: the mercy God has poured on us, and the mercy we are to show others. We can only be merciful to others because God has been merciful to us in Christ the merciful high priest who was made like us in every way, bore our temptations fully, and still pours inexhaustible mercy on those who come to Him. Psalm 103:17 and Lamentations 3 are cited to show that God's mercy is from everlasting to everlasting—it can never be exhausted.\n\nThe sermon then defines mercy through the parable of the good Samaritan (Luke 10:30-37): mercy relieves the consequences of sin in the lives of others. The early church identified the good Samaritan with Jesus Himself, and Pastor Lyro follows that interpretation, showing how Jesus healed the bruised and fanned the flickering wick to flame, and how this is to be our pattern. The sermon is honest about why we fail to show mercy: inconvenience, our own plans, and various rationalizations—noting that the priest and Levite were covenant members of God's people, not villains. The sermon closes with the challenge: have you stopped for the bruised and broken, or have you passed by on the other side?"
