      "summary": "This Sunday school sermon works through Psalm 119:41-48, the Vav section, which Pastor Tito Lyro notes is structurally unique—every verse begins with the conjunction 'and' in Hebrew, and grammatically each pair of verses forms a single sentence. He opens with three pointed questions: Who has lacked boldness to share the gospel? Who has felt ashamed of the Christian position? Who would like to grow in boldness for the Lord?\n\nThe passage divides into two movements: a prayer for boldness (vv. 41-43) and a commitment to boldness (vv. 44-48). The prayer for boldness is anchored in God's hesed—His covenantal faithfulness and mercy—which is great not because of us but because of His own name. From that foundation the psalmist prays for words from God's Word to answer those who reproach him, and most strikingly, his greatest fear is not speaking God's truth but being kept from speaking it. Unlike us who fear even mild disapproval, the psalmist fears God's disapproval above all.\n\nThe commitment section shows the pattern characteristic of this psalmist: he not only prays but acts. He commits to obeying God's Word continually—which is training for heaven, where His servants serve Him forever. He describes walking 'at liberty' in a wide space—a metaphor for the safety and freedom that comes from following God's Word, contrasting with the popular notion that God's commands are restrictive. The sermon reaches its climax with historical examples of ordinary believers who spoke before kings with extraordinary boldness: Polycarp, Justin Martyr, and John Hus. The closing point is that boldness flows from conviction and love—embarrassment about the Christian faith betrays unbelief, while love for the Word that one truly believes produces natural, joyful boldness."
