      "summary": "Preached on the occasion of the covenant baptism of Malachi James Hunter, this sermon makes the comprehensive biblical case that the children of believers are members of the visible church of Jesus Christ. Pastor Tito Lyro is careful to use the term 'covenant baptism' rather than 'infant baptism,' distinguishing the Reformed practice from any view that all infants should be baptized: only the children of those covenantally united to Christ by faith are eligible.\n\nThe argument moves through four lines of evidence. First, Paul addresses the children of believers as part of the 'saints' in both Ephesians and Colossians—the children addressed in Ephesians 6:1 are a subset of the saints addressed in 1:1. He explicitly calls children of a believing parent 'holy' in 1 Corinthians 7:12-14. Second, Jesus on two occasions welcomed the children of covenant believers, with Luke specifically describing one occasion as infants brought by their parents. Third, the Old Testament prophets promised God's Spirit and Word would be upon His people's descendants (Isaiah 59, Jeremiah 31-32), and Peter at Pentecost declared the promise is 'for you and for your children.' Fourth and most extensively, baptism replaces circumcision as the covenant sign: Romans 4 shows circumcision was a sign and seal of justification by faith alone—and this sign was given to infants; Colossians 2 explicitly connects spiritual circumcision and spiritual baptism; Galatians 3 makes baptism the new way of becoming Abraham's seed.\n\nThe sermon closes with a robust application on raising covenant children in the nurture and admonition of the Lord, citing William Hendriksen's line: 'the heart of Christian nurture is to bring the heart of the child to the heart of the Savior.' Parents who have failed are encouraged to repent and begin again—parenting can only be done in the grace of Jesus Christ."
