      "summary": "Drawing from 1 John 2:28–3:3, Pastor Tito Lyro unfolds the magnificent truth that believers in Jesus Christ are adopted children of God. He situates the passage within John's larger argument in 1 John — that getting Jesus right is paramount — and shows how chapter 3 opens a new emphasis on the identity of God's people as his family. Central to the sermon is the wonder that God adopted his people from a pool described in Romans 3 as entirely unrighteous, which means that since nothing in us attracted God to us, nothing can ever separate us from his love.\n\nThe sermon gives careful attention to what adoption means, drawing on the Westminster Shorter Catechism and three key Pauline texts. Paul's use of the legal term 'sons' (rather than 'children') is explained: in the first-century world, women could not be adopted with full legal rights, so Paul's language declares that all believers — male and female — receive the full inheritance rights of sons of God. Through the Spirit of adoption, every believer can cry 'Abba, Father,' enjoying an intimacy with God that the Old Testament saints could only partially glimpse.\n\nPastor Lyro catalogues the privileges that flow from adoption: freedom from sin's dominion and God's wrath, God's name upon us, access to him in prayer, his compassion and protection, security in his hands, the sealing of the Holy Spirit, and a glorious eternal inheritance. These realities have present consequences: they produce courageous, holy living. Because the Father sees the believer through the righteousness of Christ, even in our worst moments of failure we remain his children. The hope of being conformed to Christ's image at his return (1 John 3:2) motivates ongoing purification.\n\nThe sermon closes with a sobering distinction: God is not the Father of all people. Without faith in Jesus Christ, none of these privileges apply, and only judgment awaits. But the gospel's simplicity is that at any moment a person may come to faith in Christ and become a child of God, inheriting everything the sermon has described."
