      "summary": "In this sermon from Ephesians 5:6-14, Pastor Tito Lyro continues the congregation's walk through the book of Ephesians, focusing on Paul's exhortation to walk as children of light. He begins by connecting this passage to the preceding section: while Ephesians 5:1-5 governs how Christians are to walk in love toward one another within the church, verses 6-14 are primarily addressed outward, governing how believers relate to the unbelieving world around them.\n\nThe heart of the sermon centers on the danger of being deceived by empty words. Paul anticipated that someone would minimize the sins he listed in verses 3-5, and Pastor Tito identifies this as Satan's one unchanging strategy since Genesis 3: 'Has God really said?' These empty words do not come from a talking serpent but from friends, family members, songs, celebrities, and social media. The antidote is a firm grasp of Christian identity. Paul does not say that believers are 'in' the light — he says they are light. Just as unregenerate humanity is not merely trapped in darkness but is itself darkness, the regenerate believer has been made light in Christ. This identity transforms temptation: you do not have to follow through on sin because that is no longer who you are.\n\nThe sermon then traces the fruit of the light — goodness, righteousness, and truth — as the evidence that believers are walking consistently with their identity. Drawing on Matthew 5:14-16, Pastor Tito explains that Christians are lighthouses, not light sources: the light is in them because Christ, the Light of the world, dwells in them. The light shines not through spectacular achievements but through the accumulated 10,000 ordinary moments of faithful Christian living: obedient children, honest workers, faithful marriages, peacemakers, and humble worshipers. When these rays are focused together, the world sees a brilliant light it cannot ignore, bringing glory to God whether through conversions or through the persecution the light inevitably provokes."
