      "summary": "In this sermon from Ephesians 5:22-33, Pastor Tito Lyro takes an unusual approach: he sets aside the passage's main application (husband-wife roles) in order to look at the illustration Paul employs — Christ's love for his church. His thesis is that marriage was not invented to illustrate Christ's love as an afterthought; rather, marriage was created from the beginning to display the relationship between Christ and his church, which is why any distortion of marriage is so serious. With persecution of the church appearing increasingly likely in the current cultural climate, the pastor argues that only believers deeply enamored with Christ will be able to endure.\n\nThe sermon proceeds through the specific claims Paul makes about Christ. First, Christ is the head of the church — not merely its source (the egalitarian reading) but its authoritative Lord and ruler, as Ephesians 1:20-23 confirms. Being head means that Jesus is the boss, the master, the Lord, and there is no other head; anyone who claims to be the vicar of Christ usurps Christ's unique place. Second, Christ is the Savior of the body: there is no biblical category of salvation outside the church, and the visible church's local expression is the place where salvation is found and nourished. Third, Christ loved the church and gave himself for her — the cross is the supreme, unrepeatable declaration of that love, accomplished to sanctify and cleanse the church by Word and baptism, with the goal of presenting her to himself glorious, holy, and blameless.\n\nFinally, Christ nourishes and cherishes his church. He provides everything she needs through Word, Spirit, Lord's Supper, Lord's Day, prayer, and fellowship, and he does so not out of reluctant duty but out of genuine affection. The sermon closes with the image of the glorious wedding day when Christ will receive his spotless bride — a day whose glory is defined entirely by holiness rather than by size, success, or cultural relevance — and with a hymn: 'Living, he loved me; dying, he saved me; rising, he justified freely forever; one day he's coming — oh, glorious day!'"
