      "summary": "In the first of a three-part series on the Shepherd Psalms, Elder Nick Anderson treats Psalm 22 as a prophecy of Christ's priestly office. He notes that Spurgeon called these three psalms 'the cross, the crook, and the crown,' and that Psalm 22 reads less like prophecy than history — its description of the crucifixion (pierced hands and feet, garments divided by lot, mockery of trust in God) is so precise that Matthew Poole argued it cannot properly be applied to David at all. When Christ uttered 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?' from the cross, he was claiming the whole psalm as his own.\n\nThe sermon works through both sections of Psalm 22. The first section (verses 1-21) is a series of complaints and expressions of confidence: David — and Christ — oscillates between the felt abandonment of God and the rock of God's holiness and historical faithfulness. The elder emphasizes that Christ's suffering was not only physical. The psychological and spiritual suffering — feeling desolate, abandoned, crushed — was the worst the eternal Son of God had ever endured, as he who had always enjoyed the Father's presence experienced its withdrawal for our sake. The second section (verses 22-31) shifts to praise: Christ knew his resurrection was coming, endured the cross for the joy set before him, and knew that the fruit of his death would include people from every nation repenting and turning to God.\n\nThe sermon closes with three practical implications of Christ's priestly office: he offers prayer and sacrifice for us continuously at the right hand of the Father; his sacrifice is once-for-all and actually effective, unlike the repeated animal sacrifices of the Old Testament; and because he sympathizes with our weaknesses — having been tempted in all ways as we are, yet without sin — we can come boldly to the throne of grace in time of need. The elder quotes a moving description of Christ bringing our prayers before the Father and making our holy desires his own, concluding that this reality should drive us to deeper prayer and deeper comfort in suffering."
