      "summary": "Preaching from Ephesians 6:5-9, Pastor Tito Lyro opens with a careful contextualization of the slave-master relationship in the first century, noting four crucial differences between ancient slavery and the antebellum chattel slavery familiar to modern Americans. First-century slavery was not racially based, was practiced across all occupations (medicine was primarily a slave profession), and was so widespread that it would barely have registered as an institution to the first Christians. At least a third of the Roman Empire's population was enslaved, and many had sold themselves voluntarily because freedom brought no economic security. The New Testament regulates slavery without condoning it — just as it regulates divorce and polygamy — while the book of Philemon shows Paul quietly planting the seed for its eventual dissolution among Christians.\n\nThe heart of the sermon is the application of the Lordship of Christ to the workplace. Paul's command that slaves obey their masters 'as to Christ' and 'not with eye-service as men-pleasers' (verse 5-6) sweeps away the common approach of giving minimum effort when no one is watching. Christ frees us not from work — which was created before the fall — but to work more effectively. Every lawful vocation, whether skilled trades, medicine, homemaking, or poetry, carries equal dignity when done for the glory of God. The sermon challenges the cultural divide between 'spiritual' occupations (ministry, missions) and so-called secular ones, arguing that if God has called you to wash windows, you should be the best window-washer to the glory of God.\n\nPastor Tito concludes with the promise of verse 8: whatever good anyone does, he will receive the same from the Lord. Even Christians robbed of their rightful wages or fair recognition can rely on the reward of inheritance — and that reward is Christ himself. Masters are held to the same standard: they are to show the same concern for those who work for them, knowing that their own Master in heaven shows no partiality."
