      "summary": "In this sermon from Ephesians 5:25-33, Pastor Tito Lyro addresses the calling of husbands as loving leaders in the family. He begins by acknowledging that the family is under coordinated spiritual attack — and argues that the most powerful counter-attack is not political engagement but faithful obedience to God's design. A husband who is not loving his wife is not merely failing his marriage — he has become an instrument of Satan in the very war he thinks he is fighting.\n\nThe sermon opens with a crucial observation: Paul addresses love and submission specifically because these are the two elements cursed in the fall (Genesis 3:16). The wife's natural inclination is to want her husband's role rather than to submit to him; the husband's natural inclination is to be either a tyrant or an idiot — someone selfishly focused only on himself. Neither can be a godly spouse without the regenerating work of the Spirit. Drawing on Colossians 3:19 as a parallel text, the pastor adds a warning Ephesians does not include explicitly: do not be bitter toward your wife. Bitterness is chronic anger, and it is the enemy of a godly home — it makes us miss the grace of God and defiles everyone around us.\n\nThe sermon's centerpiece is seven ways to love your wife as Christ loved the church. Love unconditionally — not based on her worthiness, just as Christ loved us while we were still sinners. Love sacrificially — giving up rights and interests for her good; the husband is the initiator, not the responder. Love with a purifying love — your love should make her more spiritually beautiful, not more comfortable in sin. Love her as yourself — because she is a part of you, not like a part of you. Nourish her materially, spiritually, emotionally, and physically. Cherish her — like her, enjoy her, treasure her, and express appreciation openly. And lead her — in the word, in righteousness, in decision-making, as prophet, priest, and protector. The sermon closes with a simple summation: love Christ, and everything else will fall into place. His grace has already been given; we do not need to wait for another act of divine empowerment — Christ has died and risen once, and that is sufficient."
