      "summary": "In this introductory sermon to a series on 1 John, Pastor Tito Lyro covers the background and main message of the epistle rather than expounding the text verse by verse. He opens with a defense of systematic biblical preaching — citing the famous story of Karl Barth preaching love for Jews in 1939 Germany and saying, simply, 'It was in the text' — arguing that going through books of the Bible commits the preacher to say whatever the text says regardless of who may be offended.\n\nThe sermon surveys the key introductory questions. On authorship: though 1 John contains no name, the internal evidence — including the eyewitness language of verses 1-4 and the shared vocabulary with the Gospel of John — virtually confirms the apostle John as author. John was likely a teenager when called, was probably a blood relative of Jesus, called himself 'the disciple whom Jesus loved,' and was the only apostle to die a natural death. He eventually became pastor of the church at Ephesus, succeeding Paul and Timothy. On dating: the letter was probably written in the early AD 90s. On audience: the absence of Old Testament quotations (only one reference to Cain in chapter 3) suggests a predominantly Gentile readership, possibly the churches of Asia Minor.\n\nThe occasion for the letter is false teaching. Paul had warned the Ephesian elders in Acts 20 that wolves would come from both outside and inside the flock, and by the time John wrote, that prophecy had come true. The false teachers — called deceivers, false prophets, and antichrists throughout the letter — denied the incarnation (Docetism), claiming either that Jesus was a phantom or that the divine Christ left before the crucifixion. They also taught that faith in Christ required no obedience and no love for the brethren. John wrote to protect his beloved church.\n\nThe sermon closes with the main message: 1 John gives three objective tests of saving faith that yield assurance — not the subjective secret knowledge the false teachers offered, but visible markers any believer can examine. Do you believe what the Bible teaches about Christ? Is the direction of your life marked by growing obedience to God's word? And do you love the brethren — not missionaries in distant lands but the people around you who have every opportunity to annoy you?"
