      "summary": "This Sunday School lesson continues the detailed examination of the office of deacon, working through 1 Timothy 3:8-13 and the full theology of the deaconate established in Acts 6. Pastor Tito Lyro begins by reinforcing the threefold reason for the office: to free the elders for the Word and prayer, to promote unity in the congregation, and to lead the church's ministry of mercy. He draws the connection that the two offices together -- elder and deacon -- embody the gospel itself as Word and deed.\n\nThe lesson carefully distinguishes what the deacon is not: not an elder, not a building manager, not a social services director focused primarily on outside the congregation, and not a future elder in waiting. The deaconate is a distinct calling with its own dignity and glory.\n\nA detailed survey of the five moral categories in the qualifications follows: self-controlled speech (including the vital matter of confidentiality), self-control with alcohol, non-greed (with practical notes on how the church handles money with multiple accountability layers), theological orthodoxy, and upstanding moral character. The question of 1 Timothy 3:11 is revisited -- whether these are female deacons or wives of deacons -- with the conclusion that the better interpretation is wives, though women's involvement in deaconal work is affirmed and celebrated.\n\nThe lesson closes on 1 Timothy 3:13: those who serve well as deacons obtain good standing and great boldness in the faith. The deacon's only reward is the 'well done' of his Master. When deacons faithfully do their work so that elders can do theirs, the Word spreads and the church is blessed -- as Acts 6:7 demonstrates."
