      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro continues the Church Government series by surveying the major models of church government present in Christendom and establishing from Scripture that Christ alone is the head of His church. The lesson begins with a review of the foundational concepts of authority and submission from the previous week, anchored in Hebrews 13:17, and moves immediately into a survey of the five major models of church governance: Episcopacy (rule by bishops), Independent Congregationalism (democratic rule by the congregation), the Single Pastor model, New England/Puritan Congregationalism (elder rule in independent congregations), and Presbyterianism (connected elder rule with a system of courts).\n\nThe survey is descriptive rather than evaluative at this stage, simply mapping the ecclesiological landscape. The form of government of the Bible Presbyterian Church is then examined: its three offices (minister, ruling elder, deacon), its four Presbyteries, its adoption of the Westminster Standards with system subscription, and the specific ordination vows taken by officers and ministers—vows that have been essentially unchanged since 1706. Students are directed to bpc.org for access to all constitutional documents.\n\nThe theological climax of the lesson is the demonstration from multiple Pauline texts that Christ alone is the head of the church. Colossians 1:15-18, Colossians 2:18-19, Ephesians 1:22-23, Ephesians 4:11-16, and Ephesians 5:23 are all marshalled to establish this point. The New Testament authors themselves explicitly denied claiming headship (Paul in 2 Corinthians 1:24, Peter in 1 Peter 5:3). This stands in direct contrast to the Roman Catholic position (the Pope as vicar of Christ with full, supreme, and universal power) and the Anglican position (the monarch as supreme governor of the church). The Westminster Confession's chapter 25 is quoted approvingly: 'The claim of any man to be the vicar of Christ and the head of the church is unscriptural, without warrant in fact, and is a usurpation dishonoring to the Lord Jesus Christ.'"
