      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro presents lesson four of the Church Government series, focusing on the New Testament use of elder-related terminology and the positive scriptural teaching of a plurality of elders who are to rule each local congregation. The lesson begins with a comprehensive review of the series to date and then examines how the Greek word 'presbyteros' (elder) is used in the New Testament: out of 67 occurrences, 60 relate to the office rather than mere age, and the first use specifically for Christian leaders appears in Acts 11:30 when the Antioch church sends famine relief to the elders of the Jerusalem church.\n\nThe lesson establishes that the three titles—elder, bishop/overseer, and pastor/shepherd—are synonyms referring to the same office with the same persons, describing three different functions: ruling, oversight/keeping the flock together, and leading the flock toward God. The interchangeability is demonstrated from Acts 20:17-28 and 1 Peter 5:1-4, where the same persons are simultaneously called elders, overseers, and shepherds. Notably, 1 Peter 2:25 calls Christ Himself an 'overseer/bishop,' setting the pattern for what elders should be and do.\n\nThe plurality of elders in each local congregation is demonstrated positively from Acts 14:23 (elders in every church), Acts 20:17 (the elders of the Ephesian church), Philippians 1:1 (bishops and deacons in the single church of Philippi), and James 5:14 (the earliest New Testament book). This is not merely a Presbyterian conviction: Mark Dever (Southern Baptist), Martin Bucer (continental Reformed), and Iain Murray (Reformed Church of Australia) all affirm the biblical pattern.\n\nFinally, the lesson addresses the modern evangelical attempt to distinguish 'elder-led' from 'elder-ruled' churches, arguing that this is an incoherent attempt to preserve congregationalism while acknowledging the biblical data. The word used in 1 Timothy 5:17, 1 Thessalonians 5:12, 1 Timothy 3:4-5, and Hebrews 13:17 consistently means 'to rule,' not merely 'to lead,' and the Jerusalem Council's decree demonstrates that elders exercise genuine command authority over the churches."
