      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro devotes lesson five of the Church Government series to the question of whether there is one kind of elder or two. After a thorough review of the previous four lessons, he surveys the historical debate within Presbyterian circles: the Westminster Assembly identified three roles (pastor/minister, teacher, and church governor), the Scottish commissioners pressed for full parity within one office, John Calvin similarly identified multiple functions, and Charles Hodge controversially denied the divine origin of the ruling elder office entirely. Today, all mainstream Presbyterian bodies acknowledge some functional distinction within the eldership, even if they label it differently.\n\nThe heart of the lesson is a detailed grammatical study of 1 Timothy 5:17 and the key Greek word 'malista' (translated 'especially'). Pastor Lyro demonstrates through six parallel New Testament uses—Galatians 6:10, Philippians 4:22, 1 Timothy 4:10, 1 Timothy 5:8, 2 Timothy 4:13, and Titus 1:10-11—that 'malista' consistently introduces a subgroup that shares the characteristics of the whole group but possesses an additional distinguishing characteristic. Applied to 1 Timothy 5:17, this means: all elders rule well (the whole group); some elders also labor in preaching and teaching (the subgroup). This is not a hierarchy of status but a distinction in function and calling.\n\nThe lesson addresses the relationship between the universal qualification 'able to teach' (1 Timothy 3:2) and the specific calling to labor in preaching and teaching. Every elder must be able to disciple, mentor, counsel, and refute heresy—this is the one-on-one teaching work described in Titus 1:9. But not every elder is called to the public ministry of the Word on the Lord's Day. The session closes with a preview of the next lesson: the qualifications for elders and deacons according to God's Word—because having a plurality is not sufficient; these elders must also be biblically qualified."
