      "summary": "This Sunday School lesson is the sixth installment in the Church Government series. Pastor Tito Lyro leads the class through a comprehensive review of the biblical qualifications required for the office of elder, drawing primarily from 1 Timothy 3:1-7 and Titus 1:5-9. He uses Colin Marshall and Tony Payne's framework from The Trellis and the Vine to organize the qualifications into three C-categories -- Conviction, Character, and Competency -- emphasizing that the vast majority of the requirements are moral rather than skill-based, and that they describe the floor of Christian manhood, not a ceiling reserved only for special leaders.\n\nThe lesson addresses who may serve as elder, explaining from 1 Timothy 2:12 and the creation-order argument that only men are qualified. He also surveys 19th-century Princeton theologian Samuel Miller, who distilled the requirements into seven characteristics including authentic piety, sound judgment, doctrinal soundness, prudence, good reputation, broad vision, and persistent prayer.\n\nA significant portion of the class explores the congregational basis for elder election. Citing Calvin, Bucer, and Thomas Witherow, Pastor Lyro argues that officers of the church must be chosen by the people. He walks through Acts 1 (election of Matthias), Acts 6 (the seven deacons), and Acts 14:23 (appointment by showing of hands), demonstrating that the biblical pattern is the Holy Spirit working through the congregation's vote.\n\nThe lesson closes with the qualification that an elder must rule well, noting that how a man governs his family is the proving ground for how he will govern the church. An assignment is given to read Ezekiel 34 and identify what faithful and faithless shepherding looks like."
