      "summary": "This Sunday School lesson transitions from the office of elder to the office of deacon. Pastor Tito Lyro begins with three closing points on eldership: that Scripture requires a plurality of elders in every local church (historically called a 'perfectly formed' church); that elders represent their congregation to other churches in a pattern of inter-church accountability illustrated in Acts 11, 15, and 21; and that elders ordain other officers, as seen in Acts 13 and 1 Timothy 4:14.\n\nA section on the Great Western Presbytery shows how biblical inter-church accountability functions today, with the pastor explaining that Presbytery decisions are binding on member churches and that non-denominationalism is functionally a one-congregation denomination contrary to the biblical model.\n\nThe main body introduces the office of deacon. God invented deacons so the church might reflect the unity of the Spirit in the bond of love. John 13 provides the theological paradigm: just as Jesus served his disciples by washing their feet -- tangibly and concretely -- deacons are to embody that same Christ-like service in the congregation. Acts 6 then provides the historical institution of the office: the apostles neither dismissed the mercy ministry nor abandoned the Word; they created a new office so that both could flourish. The result in verse 7 is that the Word spread and disciples multiplied.\n\nThe lesson concludes with a survey of the qualifications in 1 Timothy 3:8-13 and the character of the deacon's calling -- a man of proven godliness who is already doing the work before receiving the title, motivated not by power or prestige but by the desire to hear 'well done' from his Master."
