      "outline": [
        {
          "title": "1. Review of Deacon Qualifications (1 Timothy 3:8-13)",
          "subpoints": [
            "Five moral categories: self-controlled in speech, self-controlled in drinking, not greedy for money, theologically orthodox, and upstanding moral character",
            "The deacon must keep confidences -- ministry brings knowledge of private struggles that must not be shared",
            "Verse 10: must be tested first; the basis for an internship process before ordination",
            "These are not super-Christian requirements but the ordinary expectations for every Christian man"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Timothy 3:8-13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "2. Are the Women of 1 Timothy 3:11 Deaconesses?",
          "subpoints": [
            "The Greek word gunaikas can mean either 'wives' or 'women' -- context must decide",
            "Arguments against a separate office of deaconess: Paul would not insert a new office between deacon verses 10 and 12",
            "The husband-of-one-wife requirement in verse 12 naturally follows qualifications for the deacon's wife in verse 11",
            "Deaconesses lack a separate testing requirement (verse 10 applies to deacons, not to verse 11)",
            "The better translation is 'their wives' -- most Protestants since the Reformation agree"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Timothy 3:11-12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "3. Women Are Called to Deaconal Work",
          "subpoints": [
            "The office of deacon is reserved for men, but the work of service is open to all",
            "Women can minister to the sick, dying, mentally and physically impaired; share their faith and resources",
            "Women can counsel, mentor, teach children and other women, lead women's Bible studies, plan missions",
            "The boundary from 1 Timothy 2:12: women should not preach in worship services or teach men with authority"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "1 Timothy 2:12",
            "Titus 2:3-5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "4. Jesus's Revolutionary Ministry to Women",
          "subpoints": [
            "Jesus spoke publicly to women when other rabbis would not (Luke 7; John 4; John 8)",
            "He ministered to women who were sick and suffering (Mark 1; Luke 13; Matthew 9; Mark 7)",
            "He received women's service and financial support (Luke 8; Martha and Mary)",
            "Women -- Mary Magdalene and others -- were the first and primary witnesses of the resurrection",
            "Jesus addressed women as 'daughters of Abraham,' placing them on equal spiritual footing with men"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Luke 7",
            "John 4",
            "John 8",
            "Luke 8:1-3",
            "Mark 16:1-8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "5. The Most Important Message: Men Must Stand Up",
          "subpoints": [
            "Women in most congregations are more spiritually engaged than men -- this is a rebuke to men, not an excuse",
            "Most 'women's problems' in the church are actually failures of male leadership",
            "When men lead as godly, humble, self-sacrificing husbands, women will happily flourish in their God-given roles",
            "The heartbeat of complementarianism: not 'women sit down' but 'men stand up and be men'"
          ],
          "scripture": [
            "Genesis 3",
            "Ephesians 5:25"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "This Sunday School lesson continues the examination of the office of deacon, beginning with a detailed look at the five moral categories of qualifications in 1 Timothy 3:8-13: self-controlled speech (including confidentiality), self-controlled with regard to drinking, non-covetousness, theological orthodoxy, and upstanding moral character. Pastor Lyro emphasizes that these are not elevated super-Christian standards but the baseline expected of every man who professes faith in Christ.\n\nThe lesson then addresses the hotly debated question of whether 1 Timothy 3:11 describes a female deacon, a deaconess as a separate office, or simply the wives of deacons. Pastor Lyro walks through the grammatical evidence and contextual arguments, concluding that the better interpretation is that Paul is speaking of the deacons' wives -- a position held by most Protestant scholars since the Reformation. However, this conclusion affirms rather than diminishes the necessity of women's involvement in deaconal work: the office is reserved for men, but the service itself is open to all.\n\nA substantial portion of the lesson surveys Jesus's revolutionary ministry to women -- his public conversations with women, his healing of women, his willingness to receive their service, and most significantly, his choice of women as the first and primary witnesses of the resurrection. This establishes the basis for understanding women's ministry in the church: not limited by a lack of worth or ability, but shaped by the complementary design of God.\n\nThe lesson closes with a challenge to men: women's spiritual engagement outpaces men's in most congregations, and when women have ventured into teaching and authority roles, it has usually been because men abdicated their responsibility. The message of complementarianism must be 'men, stand up' -- not 'women, sit down.'"
