      "summary": "In this second sermon in the Psalm 119 series, Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from the first stanza (verses 1-8) and organizes his exposition around three themes visible in the text: the delight of an obedient life (verses 1-3), the duty of an obedient life (verses 4-5), and the determination to live an obedient life (verses 6-8). He opens by noting that the entire Psalm is a meditation on happiness -- the Hebrew word 'blessed' that opens both verse 1 and verse 2 is the word for happy. Psalm 119 is a detailed expansion of Psalm 1, and the psalmist personally testifies to finding full happiness in God and his Word -- more than gold or riches.\n\nThe sermon's second movement addresses duty. Pastor Lyro is careful to distinguish genuine biblical obedience from legalism. Legalism is the attempt to earn God's acceptance through rule-keeping; but God calls us to duty because we have already been accepted in Christ, not in order to be accepted. We obey as those who belong to God and are united to Christ, pressing on to lay hold of what Christ has already laid hold of us for. The Psalmist's cry in verse 5 -- 'Oh, that my ways were directed to keep your statutes' -- shows that he recognizes he needs God's strength even to fulfill the duty he is committing to.\n\nThe determination section addresses verse 6-7, where the Psalmist states his resolve in a series of 'I will' declarations. God calls his people to determine to obey, not to resign themselves to failure. But the climax of the sermon is verse 8's haunting cry: 'O do not forsake me utterly.' The Psalmist looks at his own past failures and present suffering and fears that God may be withdrawing. Pastor Lyro asks: on what basis can any of us have confidence that God will not forsake us, given how many times we have violated his law? The answer is the cross of Calvary -- specifically, the cry of Christ from Psalm 22:1: 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?' Because God forsook his Son in bearing our sin, those who are in Christ can know with certainty that they will never be forsaken."
