      "summary": "This first installment of the Discipleship series introduces the series, defines the key term, and establishes the biblical foundation for disciple-making. Pastor Tito Lyro begins with a class discussion, gathering preliminary answers to questions like 'What is a disciple?' and 'What is discipleship?' before working through the Scriptures to sharpen those answers. The series is designed to complement the church's study of Kevin DeYoung and Greg Gilbert's What Is the Mission of the Church?, whose conclusion -- that the church's primary mission is to make disciples of all nations -- the series takes as its starting point.\n\nA significant portion of the lesson traces the history of the Greek word for 'disciple.' Originally a term for a learner attached to a teacher (and the root of the word 'mathematics' via Pythagorean usage), the word accumulated meaning over time to include dependence on an authoritative teacher, learning by imitation, and belonging to a community of fellow disciples of the same master. In the New Testament, Jesus's favorite word for his followers carries all of this freight.\n\nLuke 14:25-33 provides three marks of a genuine disciple: a love for Jesus that makes every other love look like hatred by comparison, a daily willingness to carry the cross (die to self), and a readiness to forsake all for Christ's sake. These are not advanced-level Christian goals -- they define what it means to be a Christian at all.\n\nThe Great Commission in Matthew 28:18-20 then frames how disciples are made: by baptizing (introducing people to Christ through evangelism) and by teaching to observe all that Jesus commanded. Evangelism, Pastor Lyro argues, is not a separate category from discipleship -- it is the opening chapter of it. The series closes with a comprehensive definition of discipleship and a preview of the topics ahead: marriage, parenting, singleness, education, and relational life as arenas of mutual disciple-making."
