      "summary": "This session continues the examination of foundational expectations for Christian parents, picking up with the third of five foundational stones: godly child-rearing is covenantal. Using 1 Corinthians 7:13-15, Pastor Lyro shows that the children of at least one believing parent are 'holy' — set apart in a special covenantal relationship with God. This does not mean automatic salvation, but it does mean that the children of faithful parents are not spiritual orphans: they have been placed under the umbrella of God's promises. This covenantal status is both grounds for confidence (God promises to be a God to our children) and grounds for watchfulness (the child is still a sinner who needs regeneration and must eventually make a personal profession of faith).\n\nThe fourth foundation stone — that the final responsibility for child-rearing belongs to the father, not the mother — receives extended treatment. Ephesians 6:4 addresses fathers specifically: bring them up in the training and admonition of the Lord. This requires masculine initiative: knowing what is happening in the family, knowing each child, and never defaulting to 'go ask your mother.' The father must also ensure that children honor the mother; if they are more responsive to dad than to mom, this is a failure of leadership. The fifth foundation is that young children are not equipped for independence and should be given high levels of parental authority early, with that authority gradually transferring to influence as they mature.\n\nThe session closes with a substantial treatment of the covenant promises of God to faithful parents. Psalm 102:28 and 103:17-18 promise transgenerational blessing for those who keep the covenant. The conversion experience of the lifelong believer — 'I cannot remember when Jesus was not my Lord' — should be the normal testimony in covenant homes, not the exotic exception. Parents who have failed are called to repent and to be faithful now, in whatever stage their children are. God uses parents as means of grace just as he uses preachers; the existence of means does not undermine sovereign grace."
