      "summary": "This session addresses the positive biblical how-tos of raising children, working through a harmony of Ephesians 6:1-4 and Colossians 3:20-21 and then digging into the practical instruction of Deuteronomy 6. Pastor Lyro begins by reminding the congregation that all Christian obedience — including parenting — flows from the new creature reality brought about by Christ's redemptive work. Because of Christ, children obey parents, and parents raise their children in the Lord.\n\nA quick harmony of Ephesians 6 and Colossians 3 yields seven shared observations: the glory of God is the goal of both parenting and being parented; the only limitation on gospel obedience is the Word of God; certain parenting styles naturally discourage children; fathers specifically tend to exasperate their children; obedience is grounded in the moral law (the Fifth Commandment); fathers must take an active and central role; and God attaches a blessing to this obedience. An important application flows from the fact that Paul addresses children as part of the church at Ephesus: baptized children are members of the church, covenant members who are to be raised as Christians — not as pagans who must become Christians from scratch.\n\nTwo negative commands are then unpacked: do not provoke your children to wrath (Ephesians 6:4, meaning do not make them angry through an unreasonable parenting style), and do not embitter your children (Colossians 3:21, meaning do not make them bitter). The primary sources of bitterness are identified as hypocrisy, inconsistency, harsh words, broken promises, excessive criticism, and conditional love. These negatives discourage children not only from obeying their parents but from obeying the Lord who commands that obedience.\n\nThe session then turns to the positive commands. Training involves what is done to and for children (structure, discipline, modeling); admonition involves what is said to children. Deuteronomy 6 provides the framework: the Word of God must dominate every aspect of family life — formal devotions, informal conversation at every moment of the day, and the modeling of parents' own lives. Fathers are ultimately responsible for knowing what all the teachers in their children's lives are teaching them, since every influence is a teacher."
