      "summary": "This session is a direct continuation of the previous lesson on children in corporate worship, completing the case and adding practical guidance. Pastor Lyro begins with a review of why children should be present in worship — from the Abrahamic covenant through the New Testament — and adds a brief but weighty warning from Judges 2:10, where the generation that saw God's mighty acts failed to pass them on and the next generation served other gods.\n\nA fresh exegetical argument is introduced: in 1 Corinthians 1:2-3, Paul writes to 'the saints' at Corinth. By the time he reaches chapter 7:14, he says the children of at least one believing parent are 'holy' — and the word is the same Greek word used for 'saints' in chapter 1. Therefore, the children of believers are saints in the New Testament sense — members of the church Paul is addressing. This is the theological ground for their presence in the gathered body: they are not observers but participants.\n\nThe three failures of faith addressed in the previous lesson (disbelief about the importance of worship; disbelief about preaching and regeneration; failure to do the work of training) are continued and deepened here. The Ezekiel 37 passage receives extended exposition: God commanded Ezekiel to preach to dry, dead bones — and through the preached Word, God's Spirit gave life. The conclusion: if God can give life to dead bones through preaching, he can give life to a child who does not yet understand. To remove children from the preaching of the Word is to remove them from the very means God has appointed for their regeneration.\n\nThe session concludes with specific practical guidance for the whole congregation. For parents: training begins at home; explain each element of worship to children in real time; discipline and return rather than removing for the whole service. For those without young children: pray specifically for the covenant children; help families; resist the urge to distract children. The final word is an exhortation to active, expectant engagement in every element of worship, trusting that God blesses faithfulness."
