      "summary": "This final session of the Discipleship series provides both a comprehensive review of everything covered since February and a closing exposition of the passage with which the series began and now ends: Ephesians 4:11-16. Pastor Lyro summarizes the series in six movements: the synonymity of Christian and disciple; the community-based definition of discipleship; discipleship as the means of perseverance; the Word-centered nature of all true discipleship; its multi-generational character; and its primary locus in the family.\n\nThe heart of the lesson is a close reading of Ephesians 4:11-16. Christ gave pastor-teachers to the church for two purposes: to equip the saints for the work of ministry, and to equip them for the edifying of the body of Christ. The word 'equip' (katartizo) means to bring to completion, to make fully adequate — the saints are equipped by the preached and taught Word so that they in turn can build up the body. This structure implies that all members, not just leaders, are responsible for the church's growth. Three fours in verse 12 make clear that the pastor equips the saints unto (not for) the work of ministry and unto the edifying of the body — these are the saints' own tasks.\n\nThis equipping happens through the teaching of the whole Bible — 2 Timothy 3:16-17, 2 Timothy 4:1-2, and Acts 20:17-27 all converge on the centrality of the Word. Significantly, the physical gathering of the body is essential; the kind of mutual edification Paul envisions cannot happen through a podcast. The session then turns to the two works the saints are equipped to do: service (ministry — humble, relational, often physical assistance) and edification (building each other up toward the fullness of Christ). The imagery of 1 Thessalonians 5:14-15 — warning, comforting, upholding, being patient — is entirely relational and embodied.\n\nThe lesson closes with a direct challenge: if your Christian life consists only of attending church on Sunday with no intent to minister to anyone else, you are the anchor holding the body back from growing in love. Immaturity when one should be mature is sin. The whole body must do its part — speaking the truth in love, serving one another, and driving each other toward the fullness of Christ."
