      "summary": "This second installment continues the Discipleship series with a 30,000-foot overview of what discipleship involves, why it matters, and how it should happen -- all developed from Ephesians 4:11-16. Pastor Tito Lyro reviews the five definitions of discipleship from the previous lesson, identifies two constants (discipleship always aims at Christ-likeness and always centers on the Bible), and re-establishes that every Christian is a disciple and a disciple-maker simultaneously.\n\nThe lesson introduces the essential intergenerational dimension of discipleship through Psalm 78:1-8, which involves four generations of passing on the praises of the Lord. The haunting verse of Judges 2:10 -- where one generation failed to pass on what they knew, and the next generation did not know God -- shows the stakes. Second Timothy 2:1-2 then demonstrates the personal chain of transmission: Paul to Timothy to faithful men to others still.\n\nThe lesson surveys six elements of what discipleship involves, grounding each in a specific Scripture: learning and loving God's word (John 8:31-32); pursuing peace and edifying one another (Romans 14:19); bearing with one another (Colossians 3:12-13); encouraging holy living (Hebrews 3:12-13); lighting a fire for love and good works (Hebrews 10:24); and the goal of unity of faith, maturity, and the fullness of Christ (Ephesians 4:13).\n\nThe lesson addresses why discipleship matters with a particular focus on Hebrews 10:23-26: discipleship is the means God uses to preserve us until the return of Jesus Christ. Finally, the beginning of how-to is raised: discipleship only happens where people want it, we must think about where we ourselves need discipling, and the two pillars are the Lord's Day and doing life together. The lesson closes with Ephesians 4:15-16 -- speaking the truth in love and every member doing his or her part."
