      "summary": "This third lesson in the Discipleship series continues the overview begun in Part 2, reviewing what discipleship is, why it matters, and now focusing on how to do it in practical, everyday life. Pastor Tito Lyro opens with a quick review, using the illustration of water and ice at 32 degrees to capture the mutuality of discipleship: every Christian is simultaneously being discipled and discipling others.\n\nThe lesson works through three methods of discipleship from Ephesians 4: speaking the truth in love (truth used to build up, never to harm), every member doing his or her part (no bench players in the church of Christ), and being filled with the Spirit (which Ephesians 5:18-21 describes not as a mystical experience but as concrete actions: speaking the word to one another, giving thanks, submitting to one another).\n\nA section on natural discipleship relationships walks through Titus 2 (older men training younger men; older women training younger women), marriage (Ephesians 5), parenting (Ephesians 6), and the pastor-to-future-pastor relationship of 2 Timothy 2:1-2. The 44 unique 'one another' commands in the New Testament cover every relationship in the church.\n\nThe Lord's Day is identified as the center and focal point of the discipleship process -- not merely an attendance obligation but the peak from which the rest of the week flows. A substantial list of practical suggestions for doing life together concludes the lesson: scheduling lunch with someone, reading the Bible together, praying together, working out and talking about Scripture, hosting people for meals. The only limits, Pastor Lyro says, are the Ten Commandments and your imagination."
