      "summary": "This fourth installment of the Discipleship series focuses on the cross-generational nature of discipleship in the local church, drawn from Titus 2:1-8. Pastor Tito Lyro establishes the foundational premise that every segment of the church -- old and young alike -- is called to learn, grow, and change. Claiming 'you can't teach an old dog new tricks' as a reason for not changing is sinful and unbiblical; to be a Christian is to be ever-changing through sanctification.\n\nThe lesson then works through each group Paul addresses. The older men are called to be sober (level-headed), reverent (worthy of respect), temperate (self-controlled), and sound in faith, love, and patience. The word 'sound' means healthy -- a healthy faith includes both what you believe and how you live it out. The older men carry the responsibility of modeling love to the entire congregation.\n\nThe older women are called to reverent behavior, freedom from slander (the word for 'devil' in Greek means 'slanderer'), sobriety, and to be teachers of good things. Their primary teaching role is directed toward the younger women, and there are seven things they are to impart: love for husband and children, discretion, chastity, homemaking, and submission to their own husbands. The section on homemaking is carefully nuanced -- it is not a prohibition on working outside the home (Proverbs 31's capable woman is quite active economically) but a matter of primary focus and mindset.\n\nThe younger men are called to be sober-minded -- to think through life from the perspective of God's word. Titus himself is to be a pattern of good works, doctrinal integrity, reverence, and incorruptibility. The lesson closes by emphasizing that these cross-generational discipleship dynamics require actual relationships -- you cannot speak life into someone you've never had a real conversation with."
