      "summary": "This fifth installment of the Discipleship series introduces marriage as a primary venue for discipleship. Pastor Tito Lyro begins by noting that marriage provides a place to practice most of the 'one another' commands of the Bible, and that all relational passages apply to the family -- not just explicit family texts. Three passages will be examined in this phase of the series: Ephesians 5:22-33, 1 Peter 3:1-7, and Genesis 2:18-25.\n\nBefore examining the wife's role, the lesson establishes the family as the basic unit of society and church, tracing the order of institutions in Genesis: marriage (Genesis 2), civil government (Genesis 9), and the church (Genesis 12). The household is the unit on which everything is built. American individualism is counter-biblical, and the family is under attack precisely because its destruction would collapse both society and church. The lesson draws from Colossians 3:12-17 to argue that Ephesians 5:22-33 is an application of Colossians 3:17 ('whatever you do in word or deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus').\n\nThe main body of the lesson examines the wife's call to submission from Ephesians 5:22-24. Pastor Lyro works through three common objections: that Paul didn't write Ephesians (now rejected by virtually all scholars), that the teaching is culturally bound (refuted by the fact that Paul regularly goes against first-century culture on other moral issues and grounds his teaching in creation order, not custom), and that the word 'submission' doesn't actually mean submission (refuted by surveying the same Greek word used of Jesus submitting to his parents, citizens to government, and believers to God).\n\nThe head-and-body imagery of verses 22-23 is explored: a body that does not respond to the head is sick, not admirable. Importantly, the wife's command to submit is simultaneously a loud word to the husband: he is always leading, and always either Christlike or Satanically. The lesson closes with Ephesians 5:24's broad scope ('in everything') and a call to receive the command from the hand of the Lord before looking for exceptions."
