      "summary": "This sixth installment of the Discipleship series continues the examination of marriage as a venue for discipleship, focusing on how the wife disciples her husband. Pastor Tito Lyro begins by reviewing the four characteristics of the wife's submission established in Part 5: it is willing (middle-voice verb -- 'submit yourselves'), Christian (as is fitting in the Lord, Colossians 3:18), specific (to her own husband, not to men in general), and respectful (she does not disparage her husband).\n\nThe lesson then addresses exceptions to the general rule of submission: the wife is not called to submit if the husband asks her to sin -- whether worship of false gods, sexual immorality, lying, murder, or physical abuse. The last example is given particular weight: physical abuse falls under the murder commandment, and she is not called to endure it silently. The lesson cautions against expanding exceptions too broadly; if it takes many mental steps to connect something to a sin, the heart should be examined.\n\nA substantial section affirms the dignity of women and argues that submission does not imply inferiority. The example of Jesus submitting to the Father -- without any ontological inferiority -- provides the theological framework. Colossians 3:11 and 1 Peter 3:7 establish that in Christ there is no difference between men and women in standing and worth. Genesis 3:16 explains why submission feels unnatural: it is the result of the curse, and only a regenerated heart empowered by the Spirit can practice the kind of submission Paul describes.\n\nThe lesson then turns to the main thesis: the wife disciplines her husband through her submission, her godliness, and her role as his helper. From 1 Peter 3:1-6, the wife's chaste and godly conduct can draw even an unbelieving husband toward Christ without a word. The 'gentle and quiet spirit' of 1 Peter 3:4 is not emotionlessness but a peaceful, non-contentious demeanor. Finally, Genesis 2:18 introduces the wife as 'helper' (ezer) -- a word used almost exclusively elsewhere in the Old Testament for God helping man. She is divinely suited to help her husband in a way no other creature can, and in that helping she disciples him."
