      "summary": "This session continues the examination of the husband's role as a discipler, beginning with a strong call to recognize that the family is under satanic attack and that faithful obedience to God's calling — not primarily political engagement or mere prayer — is the greatest weapon the church has. Pastor Lyro situates this in the Pauline parallel epistles of Ephesians and Colossians, drawing out the unique addition Colossians makes: the command that husbands not be bitter toward their wives. The word is a middle-voice verb meaning 'do not make yourself bitter,' signaling that bitterness is a self-generated posture. Bitterness typically arises when a husband has projected an ideal onto his wife rather than loving the real person God gave him; it leads to jealousy, competition, and disunity, and it is a mark of a man without Christ.\n\nThe lesson then turns to the positive command of Ephesians 5: husbands are to love their wives as Christ loved the church. The first of seven qualities of this love is explored at length: it must be unconditional. Unlike the world's love — which is contingent, feeling-based, and self-serving — the biblical love defined in 1 Corinthians 13 is entirely other-oriented. The husband is to seek the good of his wife whether she deserves it or not, just as Christ loved the church while we were still sinners and enemies (Romans 5:8, 10). The husband, like Christ, must be the initiator; he does not wait for his wife to take the lead in love. This also explains why prenuptial agreements are generally inappropriate in Christian marriage — they presuppose an end to a covenant commitment that should be unconditional.\n\nThe session closes with a pastoral reminder that even those who are unmarried or female benefit from this teaching: it helps everyone understand Christ's love, equips believers to disciple married couples, and prepares women to know what qualities to seek and encourage in men. The series will continue with the remaining six ways a husband is to love his wife."
