      "summary": "This third session on the husband's role as a discipler completes the survey of seven ways the husband is to love his wife, drawn from Ephesians 5. After reviewing the first way (unconditional love), the lesson moves through the remaining six: sacrificial, purifying, as himself, nourishing, cherishing, and leading.\n\nSacrificial love follows the pattern of Christ giving himself for the church — love always costs something, and the call is not merely to a willingness to sacrifice but to actual self-giving now, in daily life. This dovetails with the clarion call of Philippians 2:3-4 to esteem others above oneself. Purifying love means the husband is an instrument of God in making his wife more spiritually beautiful. If he is instead dragging her down or failing to lead her toward Christ, he is proclaiming a lie about Christ by the way he lives. Loving the wife as himself flows from the one-flesh union of Genesis 2: she is not like a part of him, she is part of him, making it as self-defeating to harm her as to harm one's own body. Nourishing encompasses material provision (1 Timothy 5:8), emotional understanding (1 Peter 3:7), spiritual leadership, and the sexual dimension of the marital relationship (1 Corinthians 7:3-5). Cherishing calls the husband to treasure, honor, and regularly praise his wife.\n\nThe seventh way — leading — is addressed most extensively. The husband leads in the Word (which requires him to be a student of Scripture), in righteousness (by growing in holiness himself), and in decision-making (which may at times mean decisions the wife does not prefer). The husband's leadership is summarized under four offices: prophet (proclaiming God's word to the family), priest (interceding for the family), provider (material care), and protector (against physical danger, false teaching, and spiritual attack). The session closes with a pastoral word: above all, the husband disciples his wife by being a follower of Christ himself, and the key practice that enables this is a life of ongoing, honest repentance."
