      "summary": "Preaching from Hebrews 4:14-5:11, Pastor Tito Lyro expounds the book's central pastoral appeal -- \"don't give up\" -- and grounds it in the person and work of Christ as the believer's great high priest. The sermon opens by contrasting the passage with the Roman Catholic doctrine of indulgences, which in 2008 offered forgiveness for those who completed a pilgrimage and certain rituals. Scripture teaches that there is only one mediator between men and God, and that one mediator has already done everything necessary.\n\nThe first movement focuses on Hebrews 4:14: the believer has a great high priest who has passed through the heavens. Pastor Lyro explains that without a high priest, there is no access to God -- no greater catastrophe is possible. The Levitical high priest could only enter the Most Holy Place once a year, hoping God would accept his offering, and had first to offer for his own sins. Jesus is great because his sacrifice needed to be offered only once, it was accepted (proven by the resurrection), and he entered the heavenly sanctuary -- not a copy -- with his own blood. Therefore, hold fast your confession and do not let go.\n\nThe second movement unpacks Hebrews 4:15: Christ sympathizes with our weaknesses because he was tempted in all points as we are, yet without sin. Crucially, his sinlessness made his temptations more intense, not less. Like a metal bar that is bent all the way through rather than breaking halfway, Jesus bore the full force of every temptation. He knows exactly what the congregation is going through and can help -- 1 Corinthians 10:13 promises that God always provides a way of escape.\n\nThe third movement turns to the throne of grace in Hebrews 4:16. Because of Christ's intercession, believers can come to God boldly at any time for anything. The throne that was once a throne of judgment has become a throne of grace for those in Christ. There we receive mercy for the past and grace for the present and future. The sermon closes with an urgent appeal: flee to Christ, not away from him, in times of trial."
