      "summary": "Elder Scott Hollander preaches from Judges 3:12-31, focusing on two main points: man's need for a Savior, and God's equipping of his servants for that saving work. The sermon opens by walking through the passage's narrative: Israel falls into the sin cycle described throughout Judges -- they sin, God disciplines them through enemies, they cry out in repentance, God raises a deliverer. The Moabites oppress Israel for 18 years under King Eglon before God raises up Ehud. Ehud assassinates Eglon and leads Israel to a decisive military victory, bringing 80 years of peace. The brief concluding verse introduces Shamgar, who killed 600 Philistines with an ox goad.\n\nThe first main point establishes that Israel's behavior is a mirror of human nature in general. Paul in Romans 3 says all -- Jews and Gentiles alike -- are under sin. We are no different from the Israelites in our need for a Savior. Ephesians 2:1-5 describes our natural condition as dead in trespasses and sins, but God who is rich in mercy made us alive together with Christ. Even as believers we face the disciplining hand of God when we sin, and that discipline is always intended to restore us to repentance.\n\nThe second main point -- God's equipping of his servants -- is developed through three applications drawn from the characters of Ehud and Shamgar. First, God desires your weaknesses for his purposes. Ehud was left-handed, which the Hebrew describes as literally \"restricted in his right hand\" -- an odd characteristic for a man from the tribe of Benjamin (\"son of the right hand\"). Yet this handicap was precisely what allowed him to conceal a weapon where no guard would look. 1 Corinthians 1:27 confirms that God regularly chooses the weak to shame the mighty. Second, God desires your courage. Ehud proceeded through multiple points of no return, culminating in the assassination of a foreign king in his private chamber. The courage to serve Christ -- to share the gospel, confront sin, and suffer for him -- is the same kind of courage needed. Third, God desires your willingness. Shamgar had no sword; he used an ox goad. Whatever you have in hand, use it for the Lord. The sermon closes with a call to repentance for any still in a cycle of unrepentant sin, and an encouragement to every believer to serve in whatever capacity God has equipped and placed them."
