      "summary": "This tenth Sunday school lesson in the eschatology series continues examining the biblical teaching on the Great Tribulation, with particular focus on the Olivet Discourse in Matthew 24. After reviewing the timeline of historic covenantal premillennialism — including the unique position that the new heavens and new earth arrive at Christ's physical return, allowing for sinful people to still exist during the millennium — the lesson turns to the structure and content of Jesus's teaching in Matthew 24.\n\nThe Olivet Discourse is understood to address both the destruction of Jerusalem in 70 AD (especially in Luke 21) and the final Great Tribulation — with Jesus moving between these two events as a prophet might, seeing them as a unified panorama from a prophetic distance, much as one sees mountain peaks blend together from afar. The marks of the tribulation include: false Christs and prophets performing signs and wonders; nations rising against nations with famines and earthquakes; worldwide proclamation of the gospel; and a lawless figure or system that leads people away from Christ.\n\nJesus's analogy of the days of Noah is examined at length: a universal flood-level judgment on the nations, preceded by years of unheeded warning and public preaching, in which God's people endure while unbelievers carry on as usual. The lesson closes with a preview of the rapture question, which will be addressed in the following lesson through 1 Thessalonians 4, and with a brief discussion of the confession's position that all infants dying in infancy go to heaven."
