      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro opens this second lesson by providing important historical context: the Bible Presbyterian Church was founded in 1938 and was the first American Presbyterian body to formally amend the Westminster Standards, revising Confession chapters 32-33 and Larger Catechism questions 82 and 84-90 to align with a premillennial return of Christ. Alongside those amendments, the founding General Synod passed a resolution preserving freedom of conscience for officers on eschatological matters, provided they affirm the visible, physical return of Christ and do not contradict the system of doctrine in the standards.\n\nThe doctrinal heart of the lesson is Westminster Confession of Faith 32.1, which teaches that after death the body returns to dust while the soul immediately returns to God. The righteous are made perfect in holiness and enter the highest heavens to behold the face of God; the wicked are cast into hell in torment. There is no purgatory and no reincarnation — only two destinations, confirmed by Hebrews 9:27 ('appointed once to die, then judgment'). Pastor Lyro surveys the biblical vocabulary for this period: Sheol, Hades, Paradise, Abraham's bosom, heaven, and Gehenna.\n\nFor the righteous, the intermediate state means freedom from sin and misery, close communion with Christ, and conscious waiting for the resurrection. However, the pastor cautions against over-idealizing this period — the American church has become quasi-Gnostic in treating the soul's state in heaven as the ultimate destination. Scripture consistently treats the bodily resurrection as the ultimate hope: Revelation 6 shows the martyrs actively longing for it, and 2 Corinthians 5 shows Paul regarding the bodiless state as 'nakedness' he does not desire permanently.\n\nFor the wicked, the intermediate state is a conscious holding period — like a convicted criminal awaiting sentencing. Using Revelation 20:4-5 and John 5:28-29, Pastor Lyro defends two future resurrections: the resurrection of the righteous at Christ's return and the resurrection of the wicked at the close of the millennial reign, after which comes the final judgment."
