      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro opens this third lesson by reminding the class that one generation of Christians will not die — those alive at the Lord's return will be transformed without dying (1 Thessalonians 4:15-17). With that, he frames the enduring tension of Christian life: we live looking for the Lord's return while simultaneously preparing to meet Him in death. The guide for today's study is Westminster Larger Catechism Q. 86, amended by the Bible Presbyterian Church to include reference to the millennial reign.\n\nThe lesson begins with a crucial clarification: heaven in the intermediate state is for the regenerate — specifically the invisible church, the collection of all elect souls throughout history. This is not identical to the visible church, which contains both believers and unbelievers (as Jesus taught in the parable of the wheat and tares). Groups that equate church membership with heavenly membership, such as Roman Catholicism and Federal Vision proponents, therefore err in a significant way. Only those truly regenerated by the Holy Spirit and united to Christ by faith enjoy communion with God after death.\n\nUpon death, the souls of believers are made perfect in holiness — freed from indwelling sin and therefore able to fellowship with God directly. This perfection does not mean omniscience; souls remain finite creatures. But they are morally perfect, they are in intimate fellowship with Christ, and they are resting. Hebrews 12:22-23, Revelation 14:13, and Philippians 1:23 all point to this blessed state. However, the pastor insists that this is not the ultimate destination: the intermediate state is only desirable because it is temporary. The souls of the martyrs in Revelation 6 are actively longing and crying out for the resurrection, and Paul in 2 Corinthians 5 describes his bodiless state as 'nakedness' — something he does not desire permanently.\n\nThe lesson closes by affirming that even bodies in the grave remain united to Christ awaiting the resurrection, and that for the wicked, the intermediate state is a conscious holding period before the resurrection of condemnation described in John 5:28-29 and Revelation 20:5."
