      "summary": "This fifth lesson continues the survey of millennial systems, covering postmillennialism and introducing historic premillennialism. After a brief review of amillennialism, Pastor Tito Lyro turns to postmillennialism, carefully correcting a widespread misconception: classical postmillennialism — as held by the Puritans, Jonathan Edwards, Charles Hodge, and B.B. Warfield — affirms a future literal millennium on earth. The idea that postmillennialism does not involve a future physical kingdom is a very recent development associated with the Federal Vision movement. The classic postmillennial view expects the gospel to spread through the church's ministry until Christianity is universally dominant, followed by a great revival among ethnic Israel; then Christ returns after the millennium for a general resurrection.\n\nThe lesson addresses the reconstructionist/theonomist wing of postmillennialism — thinkers like Rushdoony, Gary North, Greg Bahnsen, and David Chilton — who call not only for gospel transformation but also for the reimplementation of Old Testament civil laws. Pastor Lyro clarifies that while all Christians are 'theonomists' in the broad sense of following God's law, the specific movement goes further by seeking to apply OT civil law to national governance. The reconstructionist movement has had a historically divisive effect in churches.\n\nThe pastor then introduces historic premillennialism, the position of the Bible Presbyterian Church and his own. It holds that Christ will physically return before a literal thousand-year earthly reign. All three Reformed positions agree on a post-tribulational return of Christ; they disagree about whether that tribulation is past or future. Historic premillennialism expects the church to go through the future Great Tribulation — there is no rapture removal before it. The lesson closes by distinguishing historic premillennialism sharply from dispensational premillennialism, noting that dispensationalism would disqualify a candidate from ordination in the BPC. The key difference is the identity of the church in relation to Israel — the subject of the next several lessons."
