      "summary": "This sixth lesson begins a multi-part study of the nature and identity of the church, which Pastor Tito Lyro argues is the most important doctrinal issue for understanding eschatology. The core of the difference between historic premillennialism (the church's position) and dispensational premillennialism is whether Israel and the church are the same entity or two entirely separate peoples of God. The pastor quotes Charles Ryrie and Lewis Sperry Chafer to show that dispensationalism's defining feature is the absolute separation of Israel and the church.\n\nDispensationalism, formalized by J.N. Darby in England and popularized in the US through prophetic conferences and the Scofield Reference Bible, teaches that God pursues two distinct purposes: an earthly kingdom for ethnic Israel and a heavenly people called the church. Original dispensationalism taught different modes of salvation in different eras, but modern proponents have abandoned that in favor of affirming faith-based salvation throughout redemptive history. The irreducible core remains the distinction: all OT promises to Israel must be literally fulfilled for ethnic Israel; the church is parenthetical — 'plan B' after Israel rejected Christ. This leads directly to a pre-tribulation rapture and two resurrections into separate eternal destinies.\n\nThe pastor then surveys the six ways the New Testament uses the word 'church,' arguing that the sense most relevant to the origin of the church is the institutional church — the body to which sacraments were given, which exercises discipline, and which is called the pillar and ground of the truth (1 Timothy 3:15). Using the three marks of a true church from the Belgic Confession (preaching, sacraments, discipline), he traces the first appearance of all three marks to the Abrahamic covenant in Genesis 17: circumcision is commanded as the sacrament (sign and seal, per Romans 4), anyone refusing it is cut off (discipline), and Abraham is charged to command his household in the way of the Lord (preaching). The lesson closes with the bold assertion that the church began with Abraham in Genesis — several thousand years before the Pentecost of Acts 2."
