      "summary": "In this third installment on the nature and identity of the church, Pastor Tito Lyro brings the doctrinal argument to its conclusion. He begins by dispelling the charge of 'replacement theology': the claim is not that the church replaced Israel, but that Israel and the church are one continuous entity. God does not have two brides. God through Christ has one wife, one bride throughout redemptive history — the Israel of God.\n\nThe centerpiece of the lesson is Ephesians 2:11-19, where Paul describes the condition of Gentiles before Christ: aliens from the commonwealth of Israel, strangers from the covenants of promise, without hope and without God in the world. These are not merely political metaphors — being outside of Israel meant no access to the Word of God, no ordinances or means of grace, no covenant promises, and no hope of the Messiah. But now, through the blood of Christ, Gentile believers have been brought near — into citizenship in Israel (Ephesians 2:19). Paul reinforces this with the olive tree image in Romans 11: Gentile believers are grafted into the same olive tree whose roots are the Abrahamic promises, not planted in a new separate tree.\n\nThe pastor argues that even in the Old Testament, the nation of Israel and the church of Israel were not identical — lepers were national citizens but excluded from the religious assembly. True Israel has always been defined by circumcised hearts (Deuteronomy 30:6). Paul in Ephesians 2 pointedly calls the circumcised Jews those circumcised 'in the flesh by hands,' implying that Gentile Christians whose hearts are circumcised by the Spirit are actually more truly Israel.\n\nThe lesson concludes by anchoring the new covenant in Jeremiah 31 through the argument of Hebrews 8, the longest continuous quotation of the OT in the NT. The new covenant was made with the house of Israel and the house of Judah — to benefit from it, one must be part of that Israel. This settles the question: the church is Israel and Israel is the church, one people through all ages. The practical implication for eschatology: Old Testament prophecies to Israel find their fulfillment in the church, not in a future ethnically separate Jewish kingdom."
