      "summary": "Having established the covenant unity of Israel and the church over the previous three lessons, Pastor Tito Lyro now turns to the specific prophetic events surrounding the return of Christ. This lesson opens by laying out the order of events in historic covenantal premillennialism: a future Great Tribulation the church will pass through, followed by the bodily return of Christ, the resurrection of the righteous, the recreation of the world, a thousand-year physical reign of Christ, a final rebellion, the resurrection of the unrighteous, final judgment, and the eternal state.\n\nThe pastor surveys three tribulation-timing systems: pre-tribulation (Christ removes the church before it begins), mid-tribulation (Christ returns at the midpoint), and post-tribulation (Christ returns after it ends). He notes that all three Reformed eschatological positions — amillennial, postmillennial, and historic premillennial — agree on a post-tribulational return of Christ. The pre-tribulation rapture is associated exclusively with dispensationalism, which the BPC does not permit for ordained officers. The Westminster Standards are silent on the tribulation because the Westminster Assembly was itself divided on eschatological matters.\n\nThe Olivet Discourse — Matthew 24-25 (with parallels in Mark 13 and Luke 21) — is read in detail. The discourse was delivered on the Mount of Olives, Thursday evening before the crucifixion, in response to the disciples' questions about the temple. Pastor Lyro then surveys the three main interpretive approaches to Revelation 6-19: preterism (all fulfilled in the first century, primarily in AD 70), historicism (a running historical allegory from the first to second coming), and futurism (describing events still future). The pastor holds the futurist position.\n\nThe lesson concludes by examining the preterist arguments — the references to Judea, 'this generation,' and the standing temple — and showing that several elements in Matthew 24 cannot fit a first-century fulfillment: the return of Christ is said to come immediately after the tribulation (v. 29), the tribulation is described as the greatest in world history (v. 21), and the gathering of the elect with a trumpet blast (v. 31) is clearly the language of the final resurrection, not AD 70. Furthermore, if the book of Revelation was written in the AD 90s under Domitian — as testified by Irenaeus and other early fathers — it cannot be prophesying events that had already occurred twenty years earlier."
