      "summary": "In this twelfth installment of the Eschatology series, Pastor Lyro turns to the central passage for understanding the two resurrections: Revelation 20:4-5. He begins by reviewing the structure of Revelation 19-21, arguing that chapters 20 and 21 serve as a detailed commentary on the grand vision of chapter 19, with the whole operating in a recapitulatory rather than strictly sequential manner — similar to the relationship between Genesis 1 and Genesis 2.\n\nThe interpretive crux of the lesson is the Greek verb translated 'came to life' (or 'lived') in Revelation 20:4-5. This verb appears twice in rapid succession, just 13 words apart in the original. Most amillennial and postmillennial interpreters understand the first occurrence as a spiritual resurrection (regeneration) and the second as a physical one. Pastor Lyro challenges this reading on grammatical and contextual grounds: the verb is identical in form in both instances, and throughout the entirety of the book of Revelation the same root word consistently denotes physical resurrection (as in Rev. 2:8 and 13:14). To make an exception in verse 4 without any contextual signal requires special pleading.\n\nReaching beyond Revelation, Pastor Lyro surveys John 5:28-29, Acts 24:15, and 1 Corinthians 15:23-24 to demonstrate that Scripture consistently presents two distinct resurrections — one for the righteous and one for the unjust — separated by an interval of time rather than occurring simultaneously. He engages the grammar of Acts 24:15 at length, showing that Paul's anarthrous (article-less) use of 'just' and 'unjust' describes qualities, not a single simultaneous event, and includes an untranslated 'also' that separates the two groups.\n\nThe lesson concludes with a discussion of the nature of the resurrected body: the bodies of the condemned will be suited to eternal suffering without relief; the bodies of the redeemed will be patterned after Christ's glorious body — physical, touchable, capable of eating, and yet imperishable and incapable of sin. The lesson ends with a pastoral call: all of these doctrines are of secondary importance compared to the foundational question of one's present faith in Jesus Christ."
