      "summary": "This thirteenth and final lesson in the Eschatology series is devoted to the practical goal of all eschatological study: righteous, godly living in the present age. Pastor Lyro opens with a brief Q&A reviewing what the series has covered, including a discussion of the theological puzzle of unbelievers in the millennial kingdom and why God currently permits evil to continue (His patience in saving the lost).\n\nThe central text is Titus 2, which Pastor Lyro argues is the New Testament's most direct statement of the relationship between Christ's two comings and the believer's present conduct. The first appearance of the grace of God — the incarnation, life, death, and resurrection of Jesus Christ — trains believers to deny ungodliness and live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives. The second appearance — the blessed hope, the glorious appearing of Christ — motivates believers to live in eager, holy anticipation, zealous for good works.\n\nPastor Lyro emphasizes that the two comings of Christ are united in purpose: the first redeemed a people from lawlessness; the second will complete that redemption at the resurrection. Both appearances of Christ in Titus 2 use the same Greek word (epiphaneia), signaling their theological unity. A properly held eschatology therefore is never merely academic but always produces practical sanctification.\n\nThe lesson — and the series — closes with a pastoral exhortation: all the details of eschatology, however interesting and important, must ultimately serve the goal of producing a church that is pure and zealous for good works, living each day in the light of the Lord's imminent return."
