      "summary": "This sermon is the second in a two-part treatment of Ephesians 5:6-14. The previous week examined the positive dimension of being children of light; this sermon focuses on the more challenging negative dimension: exposing darkness. Pastor Tito Lyro opens by drawing on Thomas Chalmers' famous sermon 'The Expulsive Power of a New Affection,' applying its logic to light: when the light of Christ invades our lives, darkness is expelled — not only from our hearts but from the world around us through our witness.\n\nThe sermon carefully defines what Paul means by 'fellowship with darkness' (v.11): it is not mere contact with the world (which is unavoidable and necessary) but participation in, approval of, or silent complicity with its works. Drawing on Romans 1:18-32, the sermon catalogs concrete works of darkness — suppressing the truth about God, ingratitude, idolatry that elevates creation over Creator, the lies of gender ideology, homosexuality, and a long list of relational and moral failures. The church that refuses to name these things has taken itself out of prophetic engagement.\n\nHow do Christians expose darkness? By speaking truth and backing it with a righteous life. The word 'expose' in Ephesians 5:11 is the same Greek word used for convicting, rebuking, and convincing throughout the New Testament. The church has marginalized itself not because the world pushed it out but because it voluntarily silenced its own voice in an attempt to be culturally acceptable. We need Christians who write, reason, and speak well — bringing articulate gospel witness into every arena of culture.\n\nThe sermon closes with a surprising positive: the most powerful way to shine as light is through contentment in Christ. Drawing on Philippians 2:14-16, Pastor Lyro observes that the word 'shine' is the word for stars — and stars are brightest on the darkest nights. Grumbling is ultimately a protest against God's sovereignty. When believers endure trials, loss, or opposition with evident joy in Christ, people who have never opened a Bible read that witness and see Jesus."
