      "summary": "Preaching from 1 John 3:18-24, Pastor Tito Lyro addresses one of the most pastorally sensitive challenges in teaching: the faithful tend to be unduly convicted while those who need conviction often escape it. John has been driving home the three tests of assurance throughout his letter — right belief about Christ, obedience, and love for the brethren — and he now turns to comfort those faithful believers whose overactive consciences are condemning them falsely.\n\nThe sermon carefully works through John's logic. The word 'by this' in verse 19 points back to love for the brethren in verses 16-18 as the final objective test. Self-sacrificial love is not natural to fallen human beings; therefore its presence is evidence of the new birth. Yet John anticipates that some genuinely loving believers will question their own love. For them, he offers the dramatic comfort of verse 20: God is greater than our hearts and knows all things. This almost certainly means God is a more rigorous judge than our own consciences — and yet this same God, who knows every secret of our hearts, has declared believers not guilty because of Christ. If the most rigorous judge in existence is satisfied, no condemning thought of our own can stand.\n\nTwo blessings flow from an uncondemned heart. First, confidence before God in prayer — the uncondemned believer draws near to the throne of grace with boldness, knowing Christ as the great High Priest who sympathizes with our weakness (Hebrews 4:14-16). Second, answered prayer — not through merit, but through the alignment of our desires with God's will that obedience produces. The sermon carefully guards against a prosperity-gospel reading by noting that the entire New Testament, not just verse 22, governs our theology of prayer.\n\nThe sermon closes with verse 23's declaration that faith in Christ and love for the brethren are one commandment in two parts — inseparable. The fruit of love proves the root of faith, just as in John 15 the fruit of the branch proves its connection to the vine. Apart from Christ we can do nothing (John 15:5); therefore all our love and obedience is not meritorious — it flows entirely from our union with him. The call is to examine these fruits honestly: to repent if they are absent, and to rest in Christ's assurance if they are present."
