      "summary": "This sermon is the farewell message of Mr. Steve Battistin, a Brazilian seminary student who spent four years with the congregation before embarking on the next chapter of his ministry. The occasion is introduced warmly by Pastor Tito Lyro, who commends Steve to the congregation. Steve chooses Matthew 14:22-33 — Jesus walking on water and Peter stepping out of the boat — as his text.\n\nSteve opens with careful attention to the context: Jesus has just fed over 5,000 people, and the crowds are trying to make him king by force (John 6:15). Jesus withdraws deliberately — to rest, to refuse a political kingship incompatible with his mission, and to pray. He intercedes on the mountain for at least seven hours while the disciples battle a storm strong enough to keep them in the middle of the sea for hours. Steve highlights that the disciples were not unprotected — Jesus was praying for them.\n\nThe central section develops a theology of faith from Hebrews 11. True faith involves three elements: knowing who God is and that he exists; knowing the implications of that for one's own life; and committing to diligently seek him. Faith is always visible through its fruits — James declares you can only show faith through works. The catalog of Hebrews 11 heroes demonstrates that faith's fruits range from miraculous victories (Gideon, David, Samson) to patient endurance of suffering, imprisonment, and death.\n\nWhen Jesus walks on water and says 'It is I' — ego eimi, the divine name — Peter's faith is ignited. He steps out of the boat not as a boast but in submissive desire to follow Jesus. Yet when he looks at the waves, he sinks, and Jesus rebukes him: 'O you of little faith, why did you doubt?' Steve explains that Jesus is not asking the reason for the doubt but its purpose — why choose inconsistency after experiencing such faith? We are all spiritual Peters: saved by the God who controls the universe, yet worrying daily about far smaller things. Even so, Peter's cry 'Lord, save me' in his sinking is itself an act of faith — and Jesus uses even that little faith to bring Peter closer than he had ever been, and to lead the whole boat to worship."
