      "summary": "In this first session of a Wednesday evening series on fasting, Pastor Tito Lyro introduces the topic in the context of the Western church calendar's season of Lent. While he does not regard Lent itself as a biblical observance, he uses the occasion to examine what the Bible actually teaches about fasting. The session centers on Matthew 6:16-18, where Jesus addresses fasting as one of three acts of righteousness - alongside giving and praying - that characterize the practical life of a disciple.\n\nPastor Lyro draws out a key structural point: these three acts correspond to life's three primary relationships (others, God, and self), and Jesus frames all three with the same concern about motivation. The Pharisees fasted publicly and visibly - deliberately disfiguring their faces to gain the admiration of men. Jesus says that when the praise of men is the goal, the praise of men is all one gets. By contrast, Jesus calls His disciples to fast in such an ordinary way that no one can tell they are fasting, keeping the discipline private between themselves and God.\n\nThe session also raises important questions about the theology of fasting. The only explicitly commanded fast in the Old Testament is the Day of Atonement (Leviticus 16), and Paul warns that mandatory food restrictions can be a mark of false teaching (1 Timothy 4:1-3). Simply abstaining from food, Lyro notes, can become an exercise in self-will rather than a spiritual act. Examples from the Old and New Testaments - including Jesus' 40-day fast, the prophetess Anna, and the Antioch elders - show that fasting was a regular part of God's people's experience without being universally commanded.\n\nThe session closes with the question left open for the following week: Is there such a thing as a distinctively Christian fast, and if so, what does it look like under the new covenant?"
