      "summary": "In this concluding session of the Fasting and Lent series, Pastor Tito Lyro brings together all that has been studied and offers a comprehensive, practical framework for Christian fasting. He opens with two definitions - one broad (Martyn Lloyd-Jones: abstinence from anything legitimate for a spiritual purpose) and one narrower (voluntary abstinence from food for a spiritual purpose) - establishing that while fasting generally involves food, it can extend to any good thing given up in order to hunger more deeply for God.\n\nLyro then surveys fasting across the Old and New Testaments, noting its appearances in contexts of grief, penitence, guidance-seeking, and worship. He organizes the varieties of fasting along four dimensions: degree of abstinence (full, partial, or other), number of participants (private to national), length (a few hours to the supernatural 40 days of Moses, Elijah, and Jesus), and frequency (regular, occasional, or the continuous fast of John the Baptist). This survey demonstrates that fasting is thoroughly woven through the life of God's people without being rigidly prescribed in form.\n\nThe heart of the session is a list of ten biblical reasons for fasting: to strengthen prayer, seek God's guidance, seek deliverance, express grief, express repentance, humble oneself, show concern for God's work, minister to others, overcome temptation, and express love and worship to God. Fasting is not a spiritual hunger strike to compel God but a means of reorienting the heart toward him. Lyro closes with clear cautions: fasting is not asceticism (food is good), it is not legalistic (not commanded under the new covenant), and those with eating disorders should not fast from food. The session ends with a challenge to each person to consider this week whether fasting should be a part of their spiritual growth."
