      "summary": "In this sermon on Ephesians 5:17-21, Pastor Tito Lyro surveys the Holy Spirit's ministry throughout the book of Ephesians before focusing on the command to be filled with the Spirit. He argues that being filled is not a passive, subjective experience but an active, obedient way of life described by the four participles that follow Paul's command: speaking to one another in song, making melody in the heart, giving thanks, and submitting to one another.\n\nThe sermon challenges a common misunderstanding that being filled with the Spirit manifests in dramatic supernatural acts. Paul locates the Spirit-filled life instead in the ordinary practices of corporate worship, personal meditation on God's Word, gratitude in all circumstances, and humble mutual submission. Pastor Lyro notes that this means being filled with the Spirit is fundamentally a communal, corporate affair — it requires the presence and participation of the body of Christ.\n\nThe final section focuses on mutual submission, explaining that it is grounded not in the worthiness of the people around us but in reverence for Christ. Drawing on Philippians 2, the sermon holds up Christ's own self-emptying as the pattern for submission. Practical expressions of submission include doing good to those near you, receiving godly correction with humility, and praying for one another.\n\nThe sermon was delivered in the context of COVID-era restrictions, with the pastor noting that congregational singing had been criminalized by government order, making the exhortation to sing together an act of conscientious defiance rooted in the core identity of the Christian community."
