      "summary": "In this morning sermon on 1 Peter 4:7-11, Pastor Tito Lyro presents Peter's vision for the life of every Christian: to live for the glory of God and the good of each other. The sermon unfolds in two directions - from the foundational truth that grounds the call to godly living, and toward the specific practices that express it.\n\nPastor Lyro begins with the doctrinal basis: 'the end of all things is at hand.' Throughout the New Testament, Christ's return is not presented as an escape from present responsibilities but as the very reason to live faithfully and lovingly now. All the major events of redemption have occurred; the church awaits only the completion of the elect and the return of its Lord. John Calvin's observation anchors this: Christianity is a doctrine of life, not of the tongue, and every doctrine - including eschatology - is given to be lived, not merely debated.\n\nThe goal of this godly life is stated plainly in verse 11: 'that in all things God may be glorified through Jesus Christ.' Our lives are to be doxologies - ascriptions of glory to God in everything we do, from the mundane (eating and drinking, as 1 Corinthians 10:31 says) to the most significant relationships and responsibilities.\n\nPeter then organizes this godly living around four other-oriented practices. Prayer: pray seriously and watchfully, with a clear, focused mind uncluttered by worldly anxiety or religious vain repetition. Love: exercise a consuming, burning love for fellow believers that covers and forgives offenses - a love that is a command of the will, not merely a feeling. Hospitality: give yourself to others without grumbling, being genuinely inconvenienced for the sake of the community. Spiritual gifts: use whatever God has given - whether speaking or serving - as a steward of His grace, not as owner but as manager. Speakers must speak as the oracles of God; servers must serve with the strength God lavishly supplies. The goal in all of it is that God gets the glory, not the servant."
