      "summary": "The first lesson in the Foundations series introduces the scope of the twelve-week curriculum and begins with the most fundamental of the foundational doctrines: creation. Pastor Lyro explains that all of God's works fall under two headings — creation and providence — and that every doctrine in the series has a practical life application for the believer.\n\nThe lesson surveys the church's historical consensus on creation, showing that from the earliest Christian creeds (Apostles' Creed, the conciliar creeds of Nicaea, Ephesus, and Chalcedon) through the Westminster Standards, the church has uniformly confessed that God created all things from nothing in the space of six days. The Westminster Confession's statement — that it pleased the Father, Son, and Holy Ghost to create for the manifestation of His eternal power, wisdom, and goodness — is presented as the doctrinal standard for the church.\n\nTurning to Scripture, Pastor Lyro demonstrates that the repeated 'and God said' of Genesis 1 shows that creation is accomplished through the divine Word, a theme elaborated in John 1, Colossians 1, Hebrews 1, and Hebrews 11:3. The question of why God created is answered by the Westminster Standards themselves: God created because He was free to do so and because it pleased Him to manifest His glory. Creation was not a necessity of His being but a free act of His will.\n\nThe lesson also briefly addresses the creation of angels (following Larger Catechism Q.16) and concludes with the most important practical application: because God created all things, He owns all things, including human beings, and therefore has the absolute right to govern them and require their obedience. The doctrine of creation is presented as the foundation of all Christian ethics and the answer to the question of moral authority."
