      "summary": "In this tenth lesson of the Foundations series, Pastor Tito Lyro teaches on the doctrine of justification as part of the broader ordo salutis (order of salvation). He begins with a review of previous lessons on repentance unto life and saving faith—noting that genuine faith has three elements: knowledge of the gospel, assent to its truth, and personal trust in Christ. He emphasizes that saving faith is entirely apart from law-keeping and is itself a gift of God.\n\nThe heart of the lesson is a careful exposition of justification as a declarative rather than transformative act. Drawing from Westminster Confession Chapter 11 and Romans 3, Pastor Lyro explains that when God justifies the sinner, nothing is infused or worked within the person—rather, God declares the believer righteous on the basis of Christ’s imputed obedience and atoning blood. The ground of justification is entirely Christ’s righteousness, and faith is the sole instrument by which that righteousness is received.\n\nPastor Lyro also addresses the historical continuity of justification by faith, using Paul’s examples of Abraham and David in Romans 4 to show that both pre- and post-Mosaic saints were saved in exactly the same way. Hebrews 11 confirms that the same faith characterized all Old Testament believers. He refutes the ‘final justification’ teaching found in certain Protestant circles and associated with Roman Catholic sacramentalism, insisting that justification is a historical event that, once experienced, continues permanently.\n\nThe lesson closes with a note that this doctrine should produce assurance of salvation—since it is all God’s work—and humility, since nothing in the believer prompted God to declare them righteous. Future lessons will address sanctification in both its definitive and progressive forms."
