      "summary": "In this eleventh installment of the Foundations series, Pastor Tito Lyro distinguishes two aspects of sanctification: definitive and progressive. Definitive sanctification refers to the once-for-all act at conversion in which the believer is established as a saint, liberated from sin's dominion, and positionally declared perfect in Christ. This complements justification: while justification removes the guilt of sin, definitive sanctification establishes the believer's status as holy. Pastor Lyro cites Acts 20:32, Acts 26:18, and 1 Corinthians 6:11 as examples of sanctification spoken of as an already-accomplished reality.\n\nProgressive sanctification, by contrast, is the lifelong process of becoming more like Christ—dying more and more to sin and living more and more to righteousness. It is the work of all three persons of the Trinity and affects every aspect of the person, though it is never fully complete in this life. Pastor Lyro uses Paul's struggle in Romans 7:14-25 as an encouragement: even the apostle fought the ongoing war against sin, and the very presence of the struggle indicates ongoing sanctification. Complacency, not struggle, signals defeat.\n\nThe lesson gives particular attention to the perseverance and preservation of the saints—explaining that what is often called eternal security is rooted not in human willpower but in God's faithfulness. The elect cannot be lost because nothing can separate them from the love of God in Christ (Romans 8), and because Christ's atoning work was effectual for each person for whom He died. The doctrines of election, atonement, and sanctification are shown to be inseparably linked.\n\nPastor Lyro also outlines the ordinary means of grace through which God carries out this sanctifying work: the preached Word, prayer, the sacraments, fellowship of the saints, and the providential circumstances of daily life. The lesson closes by previewing the next session, which will address the Scriptural basis for perseverance and respond to common objections."
