      "summary": "In this twelfth lesson of the Foundations series, Pastor Tito Lyro continues the study of soteriology by first completing the topic of preservation of the saints and then introducing glorification. He establishes the biblical case for the believer's eternal security by noting that John 3:16, 3:36, and 5:24 all describe eternal life as an immediate present possession—not something that can be revoked. The life Christ gives is definitionally forever; a 'temporarily eternal' life would be a contradiction.\n\nPastor Lyro addresses common objections to perseverance with care. Hebrews 6 is explained as describing those who had external exposure to the gospel and its blessings but were never truly regenerate. 1 John 2:19 clarifies that those who depart from the faith demonstrate they were never truly part of the elect. Matthew 7:21-23 shows that external gifts and works, even in Jesus' name, are no substitute for a genuine saving relationship. The Holy Spirit's sealing (Ephesians 1:13-14) and God's ongoing sanctifying work (Philippians 1:6) are further confirmations that the elect will be preserved to the end.\n\nThe lesson then turns to glorification, introducing it via the three tenses of salvation: the believer has been saved (justification), is being saved (sanctification), and will be saved (glorification). Pastor Lyro draws from Romans 8:28-30's 'golden chain' to show that the same group predestined by God is the same group ultimately glorified—no one is added or subtracted along the way. The prophetic past tense used for glorification in that passage confirms its certainty from God's perspective.\n\nFinally, he distinguishes the intermediate state (the soul's presence with God after death) from the ultimate glory of bodily resurrection. While heaven now is better than earth, it is an incomplete state—the soul without the body is described in 2 Corinthians 5 as 'naked.' The saints in Revelation 6 cry out for something beyond the intermediate state. The Christian's true hope is not merely to die and go to heaven, but to be resurrected at Christ's return."
