      "summary": "In this final lesson of the Foundations series, Pastor Tito Lyro completes the study of glorification as the last act in God's plan of salvation. He begins with a lively Q&A review of the entire ordo salutis, having the congregation identify which of the ten categories are monergistic (God alone) and which are synergistic (God working alongside the believer). Justification and definitive sanctification are monergistic; progressive sanctification, repentance, and faith are synergistic—though ultimately all salvation is God's grace.\n\nThe lesson then focuses on the nature and content of glorification. Pastor Lyro emphasizes that glorification is a whole-person event involving both body and soul. While the soul is made perfect at death and enters the intermediate state, that state is not the believer's ultimate destination. Drawing on 2 Corinthians 5, he notes that the disembodied soul is described as 'naked'—incomplete and longing for the body's redemption. The saints in Revelation 6 cry out for vindication, confirming that the intermediate state is a waiting room, not the final destination.\n\nThe Christian's true hope is the bodily resurrection at the second coming of Christ. Using 1 Thessalonians 4:15-17 and 1 Corinthians 15:51-54, Pastor Lyro describes the event: the dead in Christ rise first, then those alive are transformed in a moment. Glorification means becoming fully conformed to the likeness of the resurrected Christ (1 John 3:2), receiving the adoption of the body (Romans 8:23). And the scope of glorification extends beyond the individual—all creation, which has been groaning under the curse since Genesis 3, will be freed and renewed. This new creation is described in 2 Peter 3 and fulfills the Abrahamic covenant promises in their fullness.\n\nThe lesson concludes with a pastoral application: every biblical teaching on eschatology is meant to produce comfort, encouragement, and holy living in the present—never fear or church division. Titus 2:11-13 captures the balance: grace teaches us to deny ungodliness and to live soberly now, precisely because we are looking forward to the blessed hope of Christ's return. Pastor Lyro closes with an announcement that future Sunday school lessons will address the mission of the church and covenant theology before beginning a new series on discipleship."
