      "summary": "The third lesson in the Foundations series addresses the doctrine of humanity — anthropology — understood as the application of the creation and providence doctrines to human beings. Pastor Lyro explains that the doctrine of God has been intentionally deferred because God's attributes (love, justice, sovereignty, holiness) become more accessible when discussed in the context of each specific doctrine rather than in the abstract.\n\nThe lesson opens with two foundational biblical affirmations: all humanity comes from the same two parents (the unity of mankind), and humanity is created in the image of God. Drawing on Genesis 3:20 and Acts 17:26, Pastor Lyro demonstrates that every nation and ethnicity traces its origin to Adam and Eve. He develops a surprising but well-reasoned argument from the law of uniformity: if genetic processes have always worked as they work today, then Adam and Eve must have carried the complete genetic information for all of humanity's physical diversity — and given that the dominant characteristics were the most prevalent in the original couple, the first humans likely had dark skin, dark hair, and dark eyes, resembling African people more than most Western Europeans.\n\nThis exegetical point becomes a powerful pastoral application: if all people come from the same two parents, there is only one race (the human race), and racism — the premise that some people are less than fully human — is as fundamentally sinful as abortion. Both rest on the devaluing of persons by denying their full humanity; both are therefore of the character of murder.\n\nThe lesson then traces the doctrine of the imago Dei: the image of God in humanity includes rationality, moral capacity, the ability to love, create, relate, and exercise dominion. The fall damaged but did not destroy the image of God; it brought spiritual death, physical death, moral corruption, and the bondage of the will — the condition described in Romans 5 and 1 Corinthians 15 as being 'in Adam.' The lesson closes with the practical implications: the doctrine of original sin explains why the world is the way it is, and why the gospel is not a self-improvement program but a rescue operation — the restoration of God's image in those who are 'in Christ.'"
