      "summary": "This session opens the Foundations series' treatment of the Doctrine of the Word (Bibliology), the fourth foundational doctrine after Creation, Providence, and Anthropology. Pastor Tito Lyro begins by defining revelation as God's self-disclosure to His creation - an act He initiates, not something humanity discovers on its own. He contrasts this with the Neo-Orthodox theology of Barth, Bultmann, and Brunner, which separated the revelatory function of Scripture from its historical accuracy, arguing instead that both are inseparably linked.\n\nThe bulk of the session explores how God has progressively revealed Himself through a series of covenants: the Covenant of Works with Adam, the Covenant of Redemption announced in Genesis 3:15, the Noahic covenant of preservation, the Abrahamic covenant of promise, the Mosaic covenant of law, the Davidic covenant of the kingdom, and finally the New Covenant which fulfills and surpasses them all. Each covenant adds to our understanding of who God is and how He deals with humanity in redemption.\n\nLyro then surveys the various modes through which God communicated His revelation: direct speech, theophanies (especially the Angel of the Lord as pre-incarnate Christ), dreams, visions, angels, prophets, the Urim and Thummim, and acts of providence. He argues that whenever God appeared in bodily form in the Old Testament, it was the Second Person of the Trinity - the one who would later become incarnate as Jesus Christ. The ultimate and final revelation is the incarnation itself, as affirmed in Hebrews 1:1-3.\n\nThe session closes with an introduction to the Old Testament's self-witness as the Word of God - the repeated authoritative formulas, the commanded preservation of the books, and the authority of the prophets - and acknowledges that the argument for Scripture's authority from Scripture is circular but rests ultimately on the convicting work of the Holy Spirit."
