      "summary": "This session continues the Foundations series' treatment of the Doctrine of the Word, examining how both the New Testament and Jesus Himself viewed the Old Testament, followed by an introduction to the doctrine of inspiration. Pastor Lyro begins by demonstrating that the New Testament authors treated the Old Testament as the final authority in disputes. Passages attributed to David in the Psalms are introduced by the author of Hebrews as the words of the Holy Spirit, showing that in the apostolic mind, human authorship and divine origin were one and the same. The Jerusalem Council settled its controversy by citing Amos 9, illustrating the Old Testament's decisive authority.\n\nThe central section examines what Jesus believed about Scripture. Lyro argues carefully that in the Sermon on the Mount, the formula 'You have heard it said' refers to the popular misinterpretations of the Pharisees, not to the Old Testament itself; the contrasting 'But I say to you' is actually a restoration of the original intent of the Law. Jesus consistently cited Scripture with 'it is written' - a perfect tense verb in Greek indicating a past action whose results stand permanently. When tempted by Satan, Jesus did not rebuke him supernaturally but quoted Scripture. His reference from Abel (Genesis) to Zechariah (2 Chronicles, the last book of the Hebrew canon) implicitly endorsed the entire Old Testament canon.\n\nLyro then surveys what the apostles believed about their own writings: Paul instructed churches to share and obey his letters as divine authority, the Thessalonian letters frame Paul's words as God's Word, and Peter classifies Paul's letters alongside Scripture. The session closes with the classic doctrine of inspiration drawn from 2 Timothy 3:16 and 2 Peter 1:21 - all Scripture is God-breathed, carried along by the Spirit as a ship is carried by wind. Inspiration is both plenary (all of Scripture) and verbal (every word), and extends to the original languages. Common inadequate views of inspiration are surveyed and rejected."
