      "summary": "This session covers the canon of Scripture - the question of which books belong in the Bible - and concludes with encouraging evidence for the Bible's reliability. Pastor Lyro begins with the Old Testament canon, showing that Jesus Himself endorsed it in Luke 24:44 by referring to the Hebrew Bible's three-part division (Law, Prophets, and Writings). His reference spanning from Abel (Genesis) to Zechariah (2 Chronicles, the last book of the Hebrew order) amounts to an endorsement of every book in between. The historical record from Philo, Josephus, Melito of Sardis, Origen, Athanasius, and Jerome confirms that the same 39 books have always been recognized as authoritative.\n\nFor the New Testament, Lyro explains that the canon was not invented by a council but recognized by the church over time. The need to identify the canon was driven by two pressures: persecution (determining which books were worth dying for) and heresy (especially Marcion's radical truncation of the New Testament to only an edited Luke and selected Pauline letters). Second-century witnesses including Irenaeus and the Muratorian Canon already list the same 27 books. The first actual vote on the canon occurred at the Council of Trent in 1546 - and that was the Roman Catholic council that added the Apocrypha.\n\nThe session addresses the Apocrypha directly: these books were never part of the Hebrew Scriptures, were not cited by Jesus or the apostles, contain historical errors, and in at least one case explicitly disclaim inspiration. They were included in the Septuagint for practical reasons but were never treated as authoritative by Jewish scholars or the early church fathers. Jerome translated a couple of them begrudgingly and finished two books in a day and a half because, as he said, they are not the Word of God.\n\nThe session closes with encouragement for believers: the Bible's textual transmission is better-attested than Shakespeare's plays, its historical claims have been repeatedly vindicated by archaeology, it contains no scientific absurdities, and its coherent unity across 1,500 years and 40+ authors from three continents is unique in world literature."
