      "summary": "This session opens the Foundations series' final category: Soteriology, the Doctrine of Salvation. Pastor Lyro begins by distinguishing the breadth of the word 'salvation' in Scripture - it encompasses the entire journey from election in eternity past through glorification at the resurrection, not merely a single moment of belief. He introduces the classical 'order of salvation' (ordo salutis): effectual calling, regeneration, repentance, faith, justification, definitive sanctification, adoption, progressive sanctification, perseverance, and glorification. Some of these are monergistic (God alone acting), others synergistic (God and the human response), but all are ultimately the result of God's grace.\n\nBefore addressing the doctrine of election, Lyro anchors the discussion in Exodus 34:6-7, where God reveals Himself to Moses as merciful, gracious, long-suffering, and abounding in goodness and truth. This is the God who has decreed election and reprobation - not a capricious or arbitrary deity but one who keeps mercy for thousands while holding the guilty accountable. With this foundation, Lyro presents unconditional election from Romans 9: humanity is as passive and inert as clay in the potter's hands, and the potter shapes some for honor and some for dishonor from the same lump with no difference in the clay itself to determine the outcome. There is no injustice in this because mercy is something nobody deserves.\n\nThe session then defends irresistible grace from John 6 and John 10, where Jesus repeatedly speaks of those the Father gives Him as those who will (not might) come to Him and be raised at the last day. From John 17, Jesus explicitly distinguishes praying for His own from praying for the world, confirming a definite group. Particular redemption follows logically: if Christ died for every human being equally and some are in hell, then God punished His Son for their sins and then punished them again - a theological impossibility given God's character.\n\nThe session closes with a careful treatment of reprobation - that God has actively ordained some to condemnation - drawing from Romans 9. Paul's response to the objection of unfairness is that the challenge is both irrelevant and irreverent: the infinite God cannot be fully comprehended by finite minds. These doctrines, when properly understood, produce praise, assurance, and humility rather than cold theological abstraction."
