      "summary": "This session on effectual calling and regeneration continues the Foundations series' treatment of soteriology. Pastor Lyro begins with a brief review of the order of salvation and then focuses on effectual calling and regeneration as two names for the same divine act: one emphasizing the means (the preaching of the Word through which God calls), the other the result (the new birth that transforms the individual).\n\nThe central passage is John 3, where Jesus tells Nicodemus that no one can see the kingdom of God without being born from above. Lyro notes that Jesus expected Nicodemus - the teacher of Israel - to have known this from the Old Testament, pointing to Ezekiel 37 (the Valley of Dry Bones) as the closest parallel: the prophet preaches to dead bones, the Spirit moves, and they come alive. This is exactly what effectual calling and regeneration are. The passage establishes that the new birth is entirely outside human contribution, just like physical birth - we don't do anything to be born; God does it.\n\nThe session distinguishes the general call of the gospel (which goes to all people) from the effectual call (given only to the elect, whereby God actually changes the person). Romans 8:30 links predestination, calling, justification, and glorification in an unbreakable chain. The Westminster Shorter Catechism Q.31 summarizes effectual calling as the Spirit's work of conviction, enlightenment, will-renewal, and enabling of faith. Regeneration accomplishes three things: enlightening of the mind (Acts 26:17-18), renewing and purifying the heart (Ezekiel 36:26), and enabling a new will.\n\nThe session addresses the relationship between regeneration and faith: 1 John 5:1 shows that believing is the result of having been born - the verb tense places the new birth prior to the act of faith. Lydia in Acts 16:14 illustrates this: God first opened her heart, and then she heeded Paul's words. The session closes with practical implications: we should be deeply thankful (nothing in us prompted God to give us the new birth), live as new people (our old man is not who we are), and boldly proclaim the gospel (God uses it as the ordinary means of regeneration)."
