      "summary": "This session addresses the final two steps in the order of salvation that occur at conversion: repentance unto life and faith in Jesus Christ. Pastor Lyro opens with a review of last week's teaching on regeneration - that God alone effects the new birth while the person is entirely passive - and moves now to the human response that follows: repentance and faith. These are synergistic steps where both God acts and the human responds, though ultimately still the result of God's grace.\n\nRepentance unto life is defined as that initial, radical, and conscious change of opinion (intellect), feeling (emotions), and purpose (will) with respect to God, oneself, sin, and righteousness. It is not the enumeration of every past sin, nor is it the reformation of life prior to believing. It is rather the acknowledgment that one is a sinner who cannot reform oneself - a repenting of being a sinner. Lyro argues that calling people to repentance is not optional for faithful gospel proclamation: Jesus' first sermon was 'Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand,' and Paul summarized his entire ministry as 'testifying of repentance toward God and faith in the Lord Jesus Christ' (Acts 20:21). A minister who never calls people to repent has departed from the gospel.\n\nThe session then addresses saving faith, which consists of three inseparable elements: notitia (knowledge of the gospel), assensus (intellectual conviction that the gospel is true and true for me), and fiducia (personal trust and commitment to Christ). Faith is not Kierkegaard's leap in the dark but a reasonable and knowledge-grounded act. Ephesians 2:8-9 teaches that even the faith itself is a gift from God - the word 'that' refers back to the entire clause 'for by grace you have been saved through faith,' and Philippians 1:29 confirms that faith is granted, not generated by human will.\n\nThe session closes with a careful distinction between saving faith and law-keeping. Romans 3 and 4 demonstrate categorically that justification is by faith apart from the deeds of the law, and Paul's argument in Galatians 3 even hangs on the singular 'seed' versus the plural 'seeds' - showing that every word of Scripture matters."
