      "summary": "In this afternoon service concluding the study of 3 John, Pastor Tito Lyro uses the final greeting — 'greet the friends by name' — as a launching point to explore the theology and practice of Christian friendship. He argues that the human desire for deep, personal relationship is rooted in our creation as image-bearers of the relational, triune God, and is therefore good and permanent — friendship is not a result of the fall but a feature of our original design.\n\nThe fall distorted friendship in multiple ways: shame, rivalry, and using people as instruments rather than ends. Sin turns friendship into idolatry (needing approval), isolation, or exploitation. Yet the desire for friendship persists because it flows from our nature. The sermon traces the story of redemption as the restoration of relationship — both between humanity and God, and among human beings themselves. Christ's reconciling work is incomplete in our telling of it unless we include the restored horizontal relationship with one another.\n\nThe heart of the sermon is a description of what true Christian friendship looks like: it is Christ-centered (pointing one another to God as Jesus did with his disciples), it is selfless (modeled on Christ's own humiliation and self-giving), it is characterized by like-mindedness in the gospel and shared life, and it actively stirs up love and good works. The illustration of children annoying their siblings becomes a picture of what we are all called to do — but toward love rather than irritation.\n\nThe sermon closes with a vision of the church as a community of deep, named friendships, mutually committed to Christ and to one another's transformation by his grace."
