      "summary": "In this first lesson of the Gender Roles in the Church series, Pastor Tito Lyro introduces the series and establishes its theological foundation from Genesis 1 through 3. He opens by observing that the best time to discuss controversial topics is when they are not currently a controversy in the local congregation—and that while the church generally agrees on these matters, the evangelical world is deeply divided. The series will co-taught with Elder Nick Anderson, using Kevin DeYoung's book 'Men and Women in the Church' as a guide.\n\nPastor Lyro begins by establishing that the existence of two differentiated sexes was God's design—not a biological necessity. He uses the analogy of complementary angles in mathematics: neither angle alone is a right angle, but together they form something complete and purposeful. Male and female are designed to complement each other, not to compete or to perform identical functions. He also connects the gospel itself to this design: according to Ephesians 5, marriage between a man and a woman was created as a living picture of Christ and His church. To blur the distinction between the sexes is ultimately to obscure the gospel.\n\nThe heart of the lesson consists of fifteen observations from Genesis 1 through 3, presented as a rapid survey. Key points include: both sexes equally bear the image of God; the man was created first and given the covenant command, establishing his unique responsibility; the woman was created as a helper, a term of profound dignity since God Himself is described this way; the man named the woman, indicating authority; and in the fall, the disorder arose specifically from Adam's failure to lead and Eve's decision to act independently. God's first address to Adam in Genesis 3:9 confirms male headship and representative accountability.\n\nThe lesson sets the stage for the rest of the series by grounding complementarianism not in culture or tradition but in pre-fall creation order—a foundation that Pastor Lyro will return to throughout the series."
