      "summary": "In this fifth lesson of the Gender Roles in the Church series, Pastor Tito Lyro turns to 1 Timothy 3 and the question of church government. He begins by reading the entire passage on the qualifications for elders and deacons (1 Timothy 3:1-13) and then explains why the question of who holds these offices is central to the gender roles discussion.\n\nPastor Lyro establishes from Acts 20:17-28 that the terms bishop, elder, and pastor/shepherd all refer to the same office, with different words emphasizing different aspects of the same role. The earliest appearance of both offices together is in Philippians 1:1, written before Acts. Within the eldership, 1 Timothy 5:17-18 distinguishes those who 'especially labor in word and doctrine'—what we today call the pastor or minister—from ruling elders, though they belong to the same office.\n\nThe diaconate is explained through Acts 6:1-6, where seven men are appointed to manage the distribution of food—a task requiring decision-making authority, not merely labor. The two offices together mirror the church's whole calling: ministry of the word (elders) and ministry of deed (deacons). The debated verse 11 in 1 Timothy 3 is argued to refer to the wives of deacons rather than a separate office of deaconess, based on the chapter's structure and the logic of the qualifications.\n\nThe crucial connection to gender roles: both offices require their holders to be men. The bishop must be 'the husband of one wife' (literally a 'one-woman man'). Furthermore, 1 Timothy 2:12 prohibits women from teaching or exercising authority—and the two distinctive functions of the elder are precisely teaching and ruling. The connection is not coincidental.\n\nThe lesson closes by addressing how to do life together well. Two errors are identified: excessive restriction (enforcing traditional female roles not rooted in Scripture) and excessive permissiveness (treating women as able to do anything unordained men can do). A wide range of ministries open to women are affirmed. But the central challenge of the series is placed on the men: the reason women sometimes overreach their calling is often that men have failed to lead."
