      "summary": "In this final lesson of the Gender Roles in the Church series, Elder Nick Anderson addresses five common objections to the complementarian position taught throughout the series. He opens with an acknowledgment that some things are difficult to understand in Scripture, and that the class should approach the subject with humility while still seeking to understand what the Bible actually teaches.\n\nThe first objection comes from Galatians 3:28 ('there is neither male nor female'). Elder Anderson shows that the broader context of Galatians is entirely about justification by faith, not gender roles—Paul is refuting the Judaizers who required law-keeping for salvation. In context, Paul is affirming equal access to God in Christ for all people regardless of ethnicity, social status, or sex. This verse cannot be used to eliminate functional distinctions without also eliminating the need for Philemon (which addresses the slave/free distinction directly), or making Romans 1 (which addresses male/female distinctions) unnecessary.\n\nThe second objection concerns Ephesians 5:21 ('submitting to one another'). Elder Anderson demonstrates that the Greek word hypotasso always refers to a relationship of unequal authority and never simply means mutual respect or deference. All 31 uses in the New Testament confirm this. The following verses (wives, children, slaves) are the explanation of what verse 21 means, not an addition to it.\n\nThe third objection is the slavery analogy: since slavery is evil and the Bible regulates it in the same way it regulates marital submission, both must be equally cultural. Elder Anderson responds by distinguishing the slavery of Paul's context from the African slave trade (which is explicitly condemned in Exodus 21:16 and 1 Timothy 1:10), and by showing that unlike slavery, male headship is described as a created good from the beginning—not merely a regulation of a sinful institution.\n\nThe fourth objection uses examples of women in ministry throughout Scripture: Deborah, female prophetesses, Priscilla, Phoebe, Junia, Euodia and Syntyche. Each is examined carefully: Deborah lacked military authority; prophetesses had limited and private ministries; Priscilla and Aquila taught Apollos privately; Phoebe's title may simply mean 'servant'; Junia's gender and apostolic status are disputed. The pattern of obscure, limited examples does not override the clear, extensive teaching of Scripture on male leadership.\n\nThe final objection is that gifted women should be allowed to use their gifts, even in pastoral roles. Elder Anderson agrees that women's gifts should be used, but argues that gifting alone does not determine the shape of church leadership. The priesthood of all believers, properly understood, is consistent with male-only eldership since the OT priesthood on which it is modeled was also male. And inward feelings of calling must be verified externally by the church's recognition."
