      "summary": "In the seventh and final lesson of the Gender Roles in the Church Sunday school series, Elder Nick Anderson addresses one of the most pressing parenting challenges of the current age: how to equip children with a biblical understanding of gender in a culture that denies objective biological reality. Drawing on Carl Trueman’s work on the modern mind, Anderson observes that today’s culture locates identity in subjective feeling rather than the body, producing a dizzying proliferation of self-defined genders. The lesson argues that parents must proactively engage their children with the biblical truth before the culture does.\n\nUsing an ABCDE framework from the course’s textbook, Anderson walks through five dimensions of God’s design for men and women: Eager Posture (woman as helper, man as leader), Body (biological sex as God-given and fixed), Appearance (men and women expressing their sex appropriately in their cultural context), Demeanor (the motherly gentleness Paul attributes to women and the fatherly exhortation he attributes to men), and Character (inner beauty as the crowning virtue of biblical womanhood; courageous, caring leadership as the crown of biblical manhood). Throughout, Anderson insists these differences are not arbitrary rules but reflections of the complementary ways men and women bear the image of Christ.\n\nA recurring concern is the shallowness of a merely rule-based complementarianism. Anderson compares superficial complementarianism to declaring that two identical basketballs are assigned different courts—unconvincing because the balls look the same. A more robust view recognizes that men and women are more like a basketball and a football: genuinely different in design, such that substituting one for the other produces immediate and obvious problems. The lesson calls the church to embrace this deeper understanding.\n\nThe lesson closes with an encouragement to stand firm in the face of cultural pressure and insults, but to do so with warmth, patience, and gospel hope. Christians will be labeled bigots and phobics, Anderson warns, but the battle is ultimately one that will be won—and it begins with fathers and mothers modeling and teaching biblical manhood and womanhood in their own homes."
