      "summary": "In this concluding session on the gift of discernment, Pastor Tito Lyro returns to 1 Corinthians 12:4-7 to review and deepen the four foundational principles established in Part 1: there is a variety of gifts, all are empowered by the Spirit rather than being natural aptitudes, all believers are gifted in some way, and the gifts exist for the common good of the church. He notes that the Spirit may actually shift a person’s primary gift over time as the congregation’s needs change—a gift is not a fixed label assigned at conversion but an ongoing empowerment for service.\n\nThe definition of discernment is refined: it is the Spirit-given capacity to examine a teaching, claim, word, deed, or appearance and determine whether it is genuinely from God. The word for \"tests\" in the New Testament (Greek: diakrino) carries the sense of dividing and judging through what is right and what is wrong. Because Satan can appear as an angel of light (2 Corinthians 11), the gift is essential for recognizing beautifully packaged falsehood. Discernment is always Word-driven; a person who claims the gift but shows no love for or growth in Scripture does not have it.\n\nSeven practical applications of the gift are unpacked through interactive class discussion: separating truth from error; helping the body understand God’s will (which the pastor describes as a park bounded by Scripture, not a single target to hit); identifying where the Spirit is genuinely at work and where counterfeit activity is present; recognizing worldliness in both its licentious and its legalistic forms; overseeing the exercise of other spiritual gifts; settling internal church disputes rather than going to civil courts; and protecting younger and newer believers.\n\nThe session closes with a pastoral word on urgency: in an age when Christianity costs little in the Western world, false teaching is at its most dangerous. The gift of discernment is therefore especially vital now. Those who do not have the gift as a primary calling are still obligated to grow in discernment through Scripture, prayer, and community—the absence of the gift does not excuse a believer from being discerning."
