      "summary": "In this expository sermon on Psalm 119:137-144, the tsadhe stanza, Pastor Tito Lyro observes that this section is structured differently from most of the psalm’s 22 stanzas. Rather than opening with a thesis and developing it across seven verses, each verse here builds upon the previous one to culminate in a single heart-deep plea: “Give me understanding and I shall live” (v. 144). Along the way, the Psalmist outlines eight foundational principles for the Christian life.\n\nThe first two principles establish the bedrock of faith: God is who He says He is (v. 137), and His Word is as righteous and trustworthy as He Himself is (v. 138). The pastor illustrates the first with a vivid example of someone claiming to follow God while rejecting what the Bible says about gender, and warns that whenever we say “my God would not do that,” we are usually substituting our own preferences for the God of Scripture. Principles three and four address God’s people: they have a passion for God’s Word that begins with honest grief over their own sin before protesting the sins of others (v. 139), and they can count on God’s promises even when—as in the story of Joseph—those promises take fifteen or more years to unfold through deeply unexpected circumstances (v. 140).\n\nPrinciple five is particularly countercultural: God’s people do not mind being held in contempt and thought of as small (v. 141). The pastor calls the congregation—especially those over forty—to model this contentment for younger believers who face mounting social pressure. Principle seven, drawn from verse 143, acknowledges that we are born for trouble in this life, and offers the profound comfort that God’s Word meets us in that trouble. A story about a mother who lost two infant children and found comfort in Matthew 26:38 (“my soul is overwhelmed with sorrow to the point of death”) illustrates how Scripture can speak directly into the deepest human pain because Jesus himself endured it.\n\nThe sermon closes with principle eight (v. 144): what we need is not new revelation, modern prophets, or additional scripture, but comprehension of the Word we already have. The plea “give me understanding” is offered as the great model prayer for every believer—and as the driving purpose of faithful preaching and teaching."
