      "summary": "In this Mother's Day sermon, Pastor Tito Lyro opens an unusual text: Paul's farewell address to the Ephesian elders on the beach at Miletus, the only sermon in Acts addressed to believers (Acts 20:32-38). He begins by reading Amy Young's extended blog post recognizing the full spectrum of motherhood—birth mothers, those who have lost children, infertile women, foster and spiritual mothers, those with estranged children, and more—and observes that every one of those categories is represented in this congregation. The passage applies not just to biological mothers but to every Christian, because the central principle—that it is more blessed to give than to receive—describes the essence of all Christian service.\n\nThe sermon's theological center is Paul's unusual framing in verse 32: the benediction he pronounces over the elders includes the call to serve. Service is not merely a means to receive blessing; service itself is the blessing. This reframes every demanding season of parenting—sleepless nights, endless diapers, countless discipline moments—as gifts from the Lord to be received, not just hardships to be endured. The pastor notes that Revelation 22 describes eternity as serving Christ forever, so those who dislike serving now may not enjoy heaven either.\n\nThe sermon is frank about the grace required: putting others before ourselves is not natural for sinful people. Our default is to see other people, including our children, as means for getting what we want. When parents measure their worth by how their children perform, they will be miserable when the children fail and only conditionally joyful when they succeed. A mother's worth is in Jesus Christ alone—not in her children's behavior or achievement. Drawing on Philippians 3:7-11, the pastor argues that the mother most consumed with Christ rather than her children will paradoxically be the best mother.\n\nThe sermon closes with five practical exhortations: love Jesus more than your children; actively want them to believe the gospel (there is no hands-off parenting in matters of faith); intercede for them persistently in prayer like Jacob wrestling with God; make the ten thousand little moments of daily life about the grace of God; and speak hard but necessary truths to your children, not crowding out that time with sports and activities. A direct challenge is issued to the children in the room: do not dare to make yourselves the center of your mother's life."
