      "summary": "Continuing his expository series through the Psalms, Pastor Tito Lyro preaches on Psalm 31, a song of David that the pastor identifies as likely covering more than one episode of suffering—it seems to encompass the chronic experience of affliction across David's life. The overarching theme is announced at the outset: God is reliably faithful and can therefore be trusted to deliver His servant. The sermon notes that Jesus himself appropriated verse 5 as His final coherent words from the cross (“Into Your hands I commit My spirit”, Luke 23:46), thereby claiming this Psalm as His own and demonstrating that God remained faithful even through death.\n\nThe sermon walks through the complexity of David's suffering, which comes from multiple sources: enemies who lay hidden traps for him, internal grief and anguish, his own sin (which he confesses in verse 10), his faithfulness to the Lord (which provokes his enemies' hostility), and the sins of others who slander him. The pastor resists the temptation to oversimplify suffering and insists that it almost always has multiple layers. David does not come before God claiming to be perfect; he comes acknowledging both his partial obedience and his partial failure, and resting his case not on his merits but on God's character.\n\nA substantial portion of the sermon examines what David knows about God that gives him the courage to seek deliverance from Him: God is a fortress and rock (like the impregnable fortress of Masada), He already knows everything David is going through without being told, He has delivered David before, He has made an unbreakable covenant with His people, and He will vindicate His people—if not always in this life, then certainly in the life to come. The sermon draws on Romans 12 and the parable of the sheep and goats in Matthew 25 to encourage believers to leave vengeance to God.\n\nThe sermon's practical conclusion draws on 2 Corinthians 1:3-4: God allows His people to suffer so that the comfort they receive from Him can be passed on to others facing similar trials. Every comfort and encouragement received from the Lord during times of suffering is not just for the sufferer but for the whole body of Christ. David models this in verse 24: having experienced God's faithfulness, he turns and exhorts the congregation to be of good courage."
