      "summary": "In this sermon from Ephesians 1, Pastor Tito Lyro addresses the breakdown of communication in contemporary society and traces its root cause not to words themselves but to the heart. After reading Ephesians 1:3-12, he observes that language predates the fall—God spoke with Adam before sin entered—and that Satan introduced sin into the world through the deliberate misuse of language (“Did God really say?”). The contemporary crisis of communication, where every statement offends someone and words no longer carry agreed meanings, is therefore not primarily a cultural or technological problem but a spiritual one rooted in unsubmitted hearts.\n\nThe sermon's body presents seven interconnected statements about God's sovereignty and how each applies to communication. God is Lord of Lords and King of Kings: we will never be in a situation where God is not ruling, so we need not panic or grasp for control in our speech. God rules all things for the benefit of His church: knowing this should transform our complaints about circumstances into praise, and our anxiety into rest. God rules over the specific details of individual lives: He appointed where we live and the families we are in (Acts 17), so the irritating people around us are not accidental. God rules over every aspect of salvation (Ephesians 1:3-6): grasping this displaces human pride with worship, and the words that flow from a worshipful heart will be marked by humility rather than self-justification. God rules circumstances for our sanctification: when we complain about our trials, we are essentially grumbling against God's plan to make us more like Christ.\n\nThe final two statements bring the series to a practical focus. God rules over relationships for our sanctification: the untimed phone call, the demanding relative, the unthankful child are all tools in God's hands, not obstacles to be removed. God rules all things for His glory (Ephesians 1:12): life is not about personal comfort or a positive self-image but about being lights that shine in darkness. Every word we speak must meet two standards—it must bring God the glory He deserves, and it must bring redemptive good to those who hear it. The sermon closes with seven searching self-examination questions about whether our speech reflects rest or wrestling with God's sovereignty."
