      "summary": "In this afternoon sermon on Hosea 6:6 through 7:2, Pastor Tito Lyro continues a study of the book of Hosea, focusing on God's desire for covenant faithfulness that comes from the heart rather than empty religious performance. Israel had tried to satisfy God with abundant sacrifices and feigned repentance (Hosea 6:1-3), but her heart remained faithless. God's response in verse 6 is definitive: *For I desire mercy and not sacrifice, and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings.*\n\nThe central section of the sermon unpacks verse 7 — 'like Adam, they transgressed the covenant' — with a careful textual discussion of whether 'Adam' refers to a place or a person. Pastor Lyro argues that the reading 'like Adam' (the ESV/NASB rendering) is more faithful to the Hebrew text and theologically richer: Israel broke covenant with God for the same reason Adam did — the desire for autonomy. Just as Adam wanted to be his own god, Israel wanted independence from the God who had rescued and bound himself to them at Sinai. The evidence of their treachery is then detailed: violence, priestly corruption, and open shameless sin committed in God's presence without remorse.\n\nThe most sobering element of the sermon is Hosea 7:2 — Israel sinned brazenly, *before God's face*, and did not even consider that God saw and remembered all of it. Their sin was not passionate rebellion but chilling indifference. The opposite of love is not hatred but apathy, and Israel simply did not care. This indifference made repentance impossible.\n\nThe sermon closes with the application that we are covenant-breakers just like Israel. Every one of us deserves the judgment portrayed in Hosea. But God provided what Israel could not produce: a covenant keeper, the Lord Jesus Christ, who kept the covenant for us. Because of Christ's work, God looks at us and sees not our faithlessness but what is well-pleasing in his sight — the righteousness of his Son."
