      "summary": "In this guest sermon by Pastor Steve Brinegar of Grace Bible Presbyterian Church in Cape Canaveral, Florida, the passage from Isaiah 42:24 through 43:3 is expounded as a word of God's fatherly care for his people in the midst of their sin and discipline. The unfortunate chapter division obscures the flow: chapter 42 ends with God's people experiencing the fury of his anger for their disobedience, while chapter 43 immediately turns with the words 'but now' to an assurance that God will not abandon them.\n\nThe sermon is structured around four things God declares about his people in Isaiah 43:1: he created them, he formed them, he redeemed them, and he called them by name. In each case, the emphasis falls on God's initiative and God's faithfulness — the security of the believer rests not on the quality of the raw material but on the skill and commitment of the craftsman. Using two personal illustrations — a badly built shelf and a disastrous plumbing repair — Pastor Brinegar makes the point that God, unlike a human craftsman, is never in over his head. He knew from the beginning what he was getting into, and he will see his work through to completion.\n\nThe calling of Abraham in Genesis 12 is used to illustrate that God's covenant people have always been called — not self-selected. Ephesians 2:8-10 grounds this in the New Testament: we are God's workmanship, created in Christ Jesus. The redemption section draws on Exodus 6 and Titus 2 to show that Christ's atonement was a full and complete payment, not a partial one, so that Romans 8:32 applies: if God gave his Son, he will freely give us all things.\n\nThe sermon closes with John 10:14 and the image of Christ as the Good Shepherd who knows his sheep by name. Sheep have no natural defenses — their only protection is the shepherd. Our security rests entirely in the faithfulness of Christ, who knows us fully and has promised never to leave or forsake us."
