      "summary": "In this guest sermon, Pastor Chris Lensch opens in Revelation 5:6-14 before moving to John 1 to trace the title “Lamb of God” from its first prophetic announcement through to its final cosmic fulfillment. John the Baptist’s declaration—“Behold the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world” (John 1:29)—would have been immediately understood by his Jewish audience as temple language: the lamb that is brought to God as a confession of debt and guilt. But the passage escalates, closing with John’s greatest claim: this is the Son of God (v. 34). Both identities together explain why Christ’s sacrifice has infinite and universal efficacy.\n\nPastor Lensch explores the paradox that John twice says he “did not know” Jesus despite them being relatives. God gave John a specific sign: the Spirit descending and remaining on the one to be baptized. This anointing at the Jordan River publicly inaugurates Jesus’ high-priestly ministry. Drawing on Hebrews 7 and 10, the sermon develops the theology of Christ as the Great High Priest who does not offer daily sacrifices for His own sins and then for the people’s, but who offered Himself once for all—being simultaneously the priest and the sacrifice, the offerer and the offering. The perfect human body prepared for Him was necessary to represent and substitute for humanity; His divine nature gave that offering its infinite worth.\n\nThe sermon pauses to consider John the Baptist’s end: imprisoned by Herod, he sends disciples to ask whether Jesus is really the one. The pastor reads this not as doubt about Jesus but as confusion about unfulfilled expectations—John seems to have expected more dramatic deliverance. Jesus’ answer is the evidence of His messianic works. This becomes an encouragement to believers who doubt in their own dark seasons: God continues to give assurance through answered prayer, unexpected friendship in times of need, and surprising conversions.\n\nThe final section surveys the Lamb in Revelation. In chapter five, John weeps expecting a lion—the warrior Messiah—but instead sees a Lamb, slain yet standing at the center of the throne. This Lamb is given the scroll of judgment because He purchased people from every nation by His blood. Chapter six introduces “the wrath of the Lamb”—a piercing image: a lamb is the most harmless of animals, yet those who have spurned this sacrifice will not escape its judgment. Chapter 19 presents two suppers: the marriage supper of the Lamb for the redeemed, and the terrible feast of judgment for the rebels. Blessed, the text declares, are those invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb."
