      "summary": "In this evening lesson from the Discernment series, Elder Nick Anderson addresses the connection between discernment and the will of God. He begins by establishing a fundamental distinction that is often missing from popular discussions: God has two wills — his secret, decreative will (his eternal decrees, which are mostly not revealed) and his revealed, preceptive will (his commands in Scripture). Seeking to know God's secret will — what he has planned for your future — is not the goal of discernment and is, in fact, off-limits.\n\nThe heart of the lesson is the argument that God's revealed will is not mysterious or elusive — it is stated plainly in passages like 1 Thessalonians 4:3: 'This is the will of God — your sanctification.' Elder Anderson summarizes God's revealed will under three categories: he wants us to be filled with the Holy Spirit, to be sanctified, and to be thankful. These three categories cover all of life and are the lens through which we make decisions.\n\nThe second half of the lesson is practical, focusing on how believers discern God's will in specific situations. Three stages are outlined: (1) mind transformation through ongoing immersion in Scripture, (2) applying truth from Scripture to situations where no explicit command exists, and (3) the daily, in-the-moment choices that flow from what is already in our hearts. Elder Anderson works through two worked examples — should I move cross-country, and should I lose weight — to show how biblical principles (not special revelation, open doors, or feelings of peace) should govern these decisions.\n\nThe lesson concludes with Psalm 40:8 as the telos: the goal is not merely to know what God wants but to delight in doing it — to have God's law written on the heart so that discernment becomes natural rather than labored. A striking statistic — that 43% of self-identified evangelical Christians deny the deity of Christ — underscores the urgency of deeply knowing Scripture as the foundation for all discernment."
