      "summary": "In this Good Friday evening service, Pastor Tito Lyro preaches on Isaiah 52:13–53:12, the 'bleeding heart of the Old Testament.' He establishes from the New Testament (Acts 8, 1 Peter 2, and six other citations) that this passage is definitively about Jesus Christ. The sermon explores four dimensions of Christ's work: his willing humiliation (becoming an ordinary human, despised and rejected), his vicarious suffering (bearing the sins of his people in their place, receiving the wrath due to them), the reality and cost of his suffering (the cup of Gethsemane, the cry of dereliction), and his subsequent exaltation (resurrection and enthronement). Drawing on Calvin, Kevin DeYoung, and William Hendrickson, Lyro drives home that Christ's substitutionary death is not theoretical — real sinners needed a real Savior who received what we deserved so that we receive what we do not deserve. The sermon closes with a call to rejoice for those in Christ, and an urgent appeal to those outside Christ to come to him without delay."
