      "summary": "This is the first lesson in a Sunday school series on Westminster Confession of Faith Chapter 1, run concurrently with a new sermon series on Psalm 119. The two series are designed to work together — Psalm 119 provides the devotional and exegetical encounter with Scripture's excellence, while the WCF study gives the systematic theological framework for what the Bible says about itself.\n\nThe lesson walks through the first three paragraphs of WCF Chapter 1. Paragraph 1 teaches that the light of nature and the works of creation and providence reveal enough of God to leave all people inexcusable — Psalm 19 and Romans 1 are the key texts. Every person in every age and place knows God exists from creation, but suppresses that knowledge. However, general revelation cannot save: salvation requires hearing the Word preached (Romans 10). God therefore revealed himself in special ways throughout redemptive history — directly, through theophanies, visions, dreams, angels, prophets — culminating in his Son (Hebrews 1:1-2). All of these prior modes are now ceased, having been fulfilled in Christ and gathered into written Scripture.\n\nParagraph 2 names the 66 canonical books as 'given by inspiration of God to be the rule of faith and life.' Paragraph 3 addresses the Apocrypha — the extra books in the Roman Catholic Bible — explaining five reasons they are not canonical: Jewish rejection, the silence of Christ and the apostles, the absence of patristic consensus, the late official Catholic acceptance (Council of Trent, 1546), and the books' own disclaimers of inspiration. The lesson includes lively Q&A from the congregation throughout."
