      "summary": "Pastor Mohammad Yamout, pastor of Tire (Tyre) Church in Lebanon, brings the morning sermon as a guest speaker while visiting the congregation that supports Tini, one of their missionaries. He preaches from 1 Timothy 4:10 — 'for to this end we toil and strive because we have our hope set on the living God who is the Savior of all, especially those who believe' — in the context of widespread global hopelessness: COVID-19, civil wars across the Middle East, Lebanon's devastating economic collapse and the Beirut explosion, and political despair in America.\n\nThe sermon argues that hope in the living God is not wishful thinking but a firm foundation built on four pillars. The living God is reasonable — the universe's complexity demands a master mind and only a fool denies it (Psalm 14:1). He is responsible — his love is unconditional and active, demonstrated by giving his Son while we were enemies (John 3:16). He is eternal — from everlasting to everlasting, unlike every government and institution in which people misplace their hope. And he is the Savior — whose redemptive plan was set in motion in Genesis 3:15, fulfilled at Golgotha, confirmed by the empty tomb, and proclaimed in the gospel.\n\nPastor Yamout speaks from personal experience: prison, tuberculosis, civil war, and flying in thin metal tubes across the sky have all been occasions when his hope in the eternal, living God was his only anchor. He closes with a call to live as pilgrims rather than settlers, to expect persecution as part of the Christian life, and to remain focused on the gospel as the reason God leaves us here. He describes what that hope looks like in practice: Tire Church, planted in a Hezbollah stronghold, growing and serving its community because its hope is set on the living God."
