      "summary": "Returning to the ongoing series on the prophecy of Hosea, Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from Hosea 12:2–14, a chapter that follows the familiar pattern of Hosea’s prophecy—indictment, consequences, call to repentance—while adding several distinctive emphases. The pastor begins by recapping the book: Hosea prophesied for over 50 years to the northern kingdom, illustrating Israel’s spiritual adultery through Hosea and Gomer’s real marriage; the major act of judgment foreseen is the Assyrian captivity of 720 BC. Chapter 12 uniquely employs Jacob as a threefold positive illustration, highlights the undoing of the Exodus as God’s judgment, and underscores God’s repeated speaking through the prophets while Israel refused to listen.\n\nThe sermon draws out four truths. First, God is entirely justified in punishing Israel for her sins. The objection that a God of love would not judge reveals that we have made ourselves the standard for God’s behavior and have underestimated the wickedness of sin. Second, God’s grace is manifest precisely in his persistent call to repentance: Jacob’s birth, his all-night wrestling match with the angel of the LORD (Genesis 32), and his exile in Syria are held up as encouragements—Israel should be wrestling with God like their forefather rather than chasing the emptiness of idols. Third, resisting God’s grace leads inevitably to separation from God. Ephraim has become a cunning Canaanite, justifying its sins by pointing to its prosperity, and has adopted the world’s philosophies rather than purging them—a warning applied to the contemporary church through Colossians 2:8. Fourth, even in judgment there is mercy: God provided Jacob a family even in his Syrian exile, and through Jacob’s line came the Lord Jesus Christ. Nothing separates God’s people from his love—but those who provoke him most bitterly will be left to bear the full penalty of their sins without an alien atonement."
