      "summary": "Preaching from Psalm 119:17-24 — the Gimmel stanza — Pastor Tito Lyro examines the Psalmist's experience of deep discouragement: he feels near death, alienated in the world, crushed with longing for God, under reproach, and falsely accused by civil rulers. All of this presses down on him precisely because he is faithful to the Word of God. The sermon's theme — drawn directly from the text — is humility and dependence in the midst of discouragement.\n\nThe Psalmist's response is striking: he does not pull away from God but leans in harder. In verse 17 he asks not for what he deserves but for God to deal 'bountifully' with him — beyond any measure of merit. He addresses himself as God's doulos, the slave purchased at a price, which was also the Apostle Paul's favorite self-description. He depends on God for life itself (v.17), for illumination of the Word (v.18), for vindication against enemies (v.22), and for comfort under the accusations of rulers (v.23).\n\nThree closing applications: first, life in this world is hard because believers are not citizens of it — we are time travelers from the age to come, living in this age according to the next, which creates inevitable friction. Second, the Psalmist's utter absorption in the Word of God — 'My soul breaks with longing for your judgments' — should be the testimony of every Christian. Third, discouragement rightly handled produces humility, which in turn opens us to the care of our brothers and sisters, to casting our anxieties on God (1 Peter 5:6-7), and to thinking more of others than of ourselves."
