      "summary": "Preaching at a Good Friday service, Pastor Tito Lyro exposits Isaiah 52:13–53:12, the passage some have called 'the bleeding heart of the Old Testament.' He opens with the universal human instinct that someone must stand in our place before God — illustrated by the Hunger Games' self-sacrificing tribute — and then grounds the identification of this passage with Jesus in the New Testament's own witness: Philip preaching Christ to the Ethiopian eunuch (Acts 8), Peter's quotation in 1 Peter 2, and six total New Testament citations of Isaiah 53, every one identifying the Servant as the Lord Jesus Christ.\n\nThe heart of the sermon is penal substitution: the Servant received what we deserved so that we receive what we don't deserve. Pastor Lyro traces the substitutionary language verse by verse — he bore our griefs, was wounded for our transgressions, crushed for our iniquities, and by his stripes we are healed. Quoting Kevin DeYoung on the reality of human sinfulness and John Calvin on beholding the disfigured Christ, he insists that what was done to Christ on the cross must be seen as done in the believer's place. He carefully refutes the notion that Christ's suffering was easy because he was God: the Gethsemane agony, the scourging that left him marred beyond human recognition (Isaiah 52:14), and the cry of dereliction all testify to genuine, total, willing suffering.\n\nThe sermon closes on the resurrection side of Good Friday: believers are called to rejoice rather than mourn, because the crucifixion leads to the exaltation described in Philippians 2. The price of the believer's soul is paid in full; nothing can separate them from God's love. Those without Christ are urged not to leave the service without running to him — the day of salvation is tonight."
