      "summary": "Preaching at Christmas from 2 Corinthians 8:9 — 'For you know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that though he was rich, yet for your sakes he became poor, that you through his poverty might become rich' — Pastor Tito Lyro expounds the incarnation of the Son of God. Paul wrote this verse not as a Christmas text but as a motivation for generosity; yet the incarnation permeates everything Christian, so the argument from Christ's condescension flows naturally.\n\nThe sermon first catalogues the riches Christ possessed before the incarnation: rich in eternal existence (John 1:1; John 17:5), in possessions (all things were created by him and for him, Colossians 1:15-17), in power (he sustains the universe by the word of his power), in honor (the angelic hosts of Isaiah 6 worshiped him), and in the love of the Trinitarian life. Then, in stark contrast, the sermon traces the depths of his poverty: from the manger to the flight to Egypt, from dependence on others for food to homelessness, from washing his betrayer's feet to the abandonment of the cross.\n\nThe reason for this descent is stated plainly — for our sakes. What happened to him is what should have happened to us. He took the penalty we deserved, becoming sin so that we might become the righteousness of God in him (2 Corinthians 5:21). The riches we receive in return are not material but eternal — adoption as children of God, the knowledge of God himself (John 17:3), and resurrection at the last day. The sermon closes with the vision of every knee bowing: those saved bowing under the weight of his grace, those who refused bowing under the weight of his wrath, but no knee remaining unbowed."
