      "summary": "This Sunday school lesson launches a new series on the twelve Minor Prophets by providing the historical, canonical, and theological framework needed to read them well. Pastor Tito Lyro opens with a reading from Nahum 1:1-8 — the prophet the group will study — and explains that the term 'minor' refers to the length of the books, not their theological importance. All twelve are the word of God; nine are directly quoted in the New Testament, and all twelve are alluded to there.\n\nThe lesson walks through an Old Testament timeline from Abraham (approx. 1900 BC) through Moses and the Exodus, the united monarchy (Saul, David, and Solomon each with 40-year reigns), the divided kingdom, and the prophetic era. The first major prophet was Elijah — not a writing prophet, but one who established the prophetic office of calling Israel back to God's word. The writing prophets begin in the 800s BC, as Assyria rises to dominance, and run through Malachi (approx. 430 BC), after which there are four hundred years of prophetic silence until John the Baptist.\n\nThe lesson identifies all twelve prophets by name, notes the canonical order (which does not match historical order, most likely for reasons related to scroll arrangement), and briefly characterizes each one. The historical order for the series will be Jonah (oldest), Amos, Hosea, Micah, Nahum, Zephaniah, Habakkuk, Obadiah, Haggai, Zechariah, Joel, and Malachi. The lesson closes by reminding the class that less than 2% of Old Testament prophetic text is predictive — the prophets primarily preached, and that proclamation speaks to the church today just as it spoke to Israel."
