      "summary": "Elder Scott Hollander preaches from Psalm 13 — one of the great lament Psalms — in what appears to be a season of pandemic isolation. The sermon's driving question is: 'Is Jesus enough?' and its thesis is that a Christian can find peace even in the deepest despair. Three points follow David's progression through the six verses of the Psalm: even Christians face deep despair; the means of grace tether the weary to Christ; and peace returns in the light of Jesus.\n\nThe sermon opens by establishing that David — a man after God's own heart, filled with the Holy Spirit, the Spirit-anointed patriarch of the Messianic lineage — fell into deep depression. His fourfold 'how long' in verses 1-2 echoes twenty-two times throughout the Psalter. David feels abandoned and alone, but Elder Hollander uses the Great Smog of London (1952) as an extended illustration: when the city was blanketed in deadly fog so dense residents could not see their own feet, Big Ben and the Tower of London were invisible — but they were there. God's hiddenness in dark seasons is not God's absence; Psalm 139 confirms there is no place where he is not.\n\nThe means of grace receive extended treatment as the practical pathway to peace: prayer, the preaching of the Word, and the sacraments are the ordinary means in which the weary are anchored to Christ. David models this by moving from moaning (vv. 1-2) to supplication (vv. 3-4) to praise (vv. 5-6). The sermon closes with the Resurrection appearance to Mary in John 20: in the depths of her grief, unable to see through her tears, Jesus simply said her name — and in that one word, everything changed. The Lord is risen, he is present, and peace returns in the light of Jesus."
