      "summary": "Opening a two-part series on the whole armor of God from Ephesians 6:10-13, Pastor Tito Lyro argues that the entire book of Ephesians has been building toward this call to spiritual warfare. Chapters 1-3 trace the church's origin in God's eternal decree and the historical application of that decree in salvation; chapters 4-6 apply that theology to the life of the church community; and the word 'finally' in verse 10 signals Paul's bringing everything to a climax: be strong in the Lord, because it's war.\n\nThe sermon develops two answers to the question of how one becomes strong in the Lord. First, through the power of the Holy Spirit who already indwells every believer — the same mighty power that raised Jesus from the dead is the power at work in the church (Ephesians 1:19). Second, through union with Christ — we are strong not in ourselves but in the Lord, fighting the war from a position of union with the conquering King.\n\nThe enemy is then carefully characterized: we do not wrestle against flesh and blood, but against principalities, powers, rulers of the darkness of this age, and spiritual hosts of wickedness in the heavenly places. Satan is a liar, a murderer, an angel of light, and a roaring lion — powerful, deceptive, and relentless. But he is a creature, a fallen angel, not omnipresent or omnipotent. And he is a defeated foe: the cross and resurrection of Christ are the decisive victory (Colossians 2:15). We fight from that victory, not toward it. The sermon closes with the command to put on the whole armor of God — not partial armor, but the full panoplia — and to stand firm."
