      "summary": "Continuing from the previous week's introduction to the whole armor of God, Pastor Tito Lyro focuses on Ephesians 6:14 and the first two pieces of armor: the belt of truth and the breastplate of righteousness. The sermon begins by noting that Paul used military imagery throughout his letters, and that the Ephesians 6 passage is his most extended military analogy — likely developed while he was in house arrest in Rome, chained to a Roman soldier day and night.\n\nThe belt of truth is treated as the foundation piece: the Roman soldier's belt held everything else together; without it the other armor would not function. Truth here is both objective (the truth of God, Scripture, and the gospel — the opposite of Satan's lies in John 8:44) and subjective (personal integrity — Psalm 51:6). Isaiah 11:5 connects this armor to the Messiah himself, who girds his loins with righteousness and faithfulness. The Christian wears what Christ wore.\n\nThe breastplate of righteousness protects the vital organs — heart and lungs. The sermon develops this primarily in terms of imputed righteousness: the justification Christ has secured before God's judgment cannot be penetrated by any accusation. But it also means righteous living — a life of integrity that refuses to give the enemy legitimate grounds for accusation. Daniel in Daniel 6 is the illustration: his enemies searched for fault and could find none except that he was more faithful to God than to the king. That must be the church's condition: truth and righteousness so deeply worn that only faithfulness to God remains as an accusation."
