      "summary": "Preaching in the afternoon service from Matthew 3:13-17, Pastor Tito Lyro examines the baptism of Jesus — the kickoff event for his public ministry. The sermon begins with the question that immediately strikes most readers: why is Jesus, who has no sin, being baptized by John whose baptism is specifically a baptism of repentance? John himself raises this objection in verse 14. Jesus's answer — 'it is fitting for us to fulfill all righteousness' — establishes that his baptism is not about his own sin but about his representative identification with the sinners he came to save, and about fulfilling the divine purpose for his mission.\n\nThe baptism is understood as the public kickoff of Jesus's ministry: before this moment, Jesus has done nothing publicly to identify himself or his mission. At the baptism, the heavens are opened and all three persons of the Trinity are visibly present: the Son being baptized, the Spirit descending like a dove (echoing Genesis 1:2 and the new creation), and the Father speaking from heaven. The Father's words — 'This is my beloved Son in whom I am well pleased' — combine Psalm 2:7 (the royal Messianic Son) with Isaiah 42:1 (the suffering Servant of the Lord), marking Jesus as both King and Servant in a single declaration.\n\nThe sermon closes with the gospel application: because the Father is well pleased with Jesus, he is well pleased with all who are in Christ — not because of their own merit, but because of Christ's. The suffering Servant stood in the place of his people, bore the wrath to come, and rose from the dead for their sake. Christian baptism is connected to Jesus's baptism: as he went through water in identification with sinners, believers go through water in identification with his death and resurrection."
