      "summary": "Beginning a study of John the Baptist's ministry within the ongoing series on the first year of Jesus's ministry, Pastor Tito Lyro preaches from Matthew 3:1-6. The sermon opens with a brief chronological orientation: Jesus was born around 5 BC, John began his ministry in the spring of AD 26, Jesus was baptized in the fall of that year, and the crucifixion occurred in AD 30. John's ministry is so inseparably connected with Jesus's that the series on Jesus's first year must begin with John — and Peter confirmed this in Acts 1:21-22, saying that the replacement for Judas must be someone who was a witness from the baptism of John through the resurrection.\n\nThe sermon establishes John as the fulfillment of both Isaiah 40:3 and Malachi 3:1 — the voice in the wilderness who would prepare the way of the Lord and the forerunner who would precede the Lord's coming. John's camel-hair clothing and leather belt deliberately echo Elijah's description in 2 Kings 1:8, marking him as the Elijah-figure prophesied in Malachi 4. The sermon also clarifies that John's title 'the Baptist' is not a theological designation but a descriptive one — he is called that because he was the one who baptized; if his theology were being described, he would be John the Presbyterian.\n\nThe heart of the sermon is John's message: 'Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.' This was the first word of John's ministry and also of Jesus's own first sermon. Repentance is the key that opens the gate to the kingdom of heaven. The entire region of Judea was caught up in John's preaching — Jerusalem, all Judea, and all the region around the Jordan came out and were baptized, confessing their sins. John's baptism was a sign of conviction of sin and repentance, not to be confused with Christian baptism (as Paul's encounter with Apollos's disciples in Acts 19 makes clear)."
