      "summary": "In this morning sermon on Luke 3:15-18, Pastor Tito Lyro examines John the Baptist's testimony concerning Jesus as the people wonder whether John himself might be the Christ. The sermon explores the people's expectation as a model of hopeful, active faith, noting that their thoughtful engagement with John's preaching was itself evidence of the Spirit at work. John, by contrast, repeatedly denies being the Messiah and explicitly declares his unworthiness — pointing all attention to the coming One who is mightier than he.\n\nThe sermon gives careful attention to John's announcement that the Messiah would baptize with the Holy Spirit and fire, arguing from the Greek that 'Spirit' and 'fire' are joined by a single preposition and therefore describe the same reality. This was fulfilled at Pentecost and continues as the Spirit's purifying work in the church. Pastor Lyro then turns to John's harvest imagery in verse 17, using it to describe the mixed nature of the visible church — where five categories of people co-exist — and the separation that will occur at Christ's return.\n\nThe sermon concludes with a sobering treatment of the certainty and finality of judgment. Death brings immediate judgment; Christ's return brings the purification of the visible church and the resurrection of the righteous; and the final judgment follows. Yet for believers this is not terror but hope — Christ absorbed the condemnation we deserve. And every day He has not yet returned is another extension of God's patience and grace toward the repentant."
