      "summary": "In this sermon on Hosea 13, Pastor Tito Lyro draws out five truths from one of the most difficult chapters in the book. The chapter presents both fierce judgment and tender mercy, and the preacher argues that without an accurate view of judgment we will never truly appreciate the mercy of God. The primary relationship Jesus has with us is not as healer or encourager but as Savior of sinners — and if we do not appreciate that, we will not appreciate Jesus for who He truly is.\n\nThe five themes are: (1) pride destroys — Ephraim's greatness was swallowed by idolatry until they became as transient as morning clouds; (2) there is only one God and Savior — Yahweh alone, revealed in Jesus Christ, and all other objects of trust are false deliverances; (3) prosperity distracts — when Ephraim had plenty, they forgot the Lord who provided it, a temptation for the most prosperous generation in history; (4) there are real consequences for suppressing the knowledge of God — God withholds His protection and gives people the godless world they want; and (5) there is always mercy with God, even beyond the grave.\n\nThe climax of the sermon is verse 14, which Paul quotes in 1 Corinthians 15: 'O Death, I will be your plagues! O Grave, I will be your destruction!' Though physical death may be the consequence for Israel's sin, God is Lord over the grave. The outer man decays but the inner man prospers. At the resurrection, body and soul will be reunited in perfection before Christ. The preacher closes with an urgent pastoral question: is anything distracting you from following the Lord? Are you looking for salvation somewhere other than Jesus Christ?"
