      "summary": "Elder Andrew Hoy delivered this Sunday school lesson in response to the intense national conversation around justice and social justice. The lesson is addressed to a broadly conservative congregation while acknowledging that young people in the church are still formulating their worldview and are vulnerable to persuasive but destructive ideas. The goal is not to dismiss the topic but to engage it first and foremost from Scripture.\n\nThe first portion of the lesson examines what social justice means — distinguishing the neutral denotation (systemic injustice is a real category) from the ideological connotations the phrase carries in contemporary discourse. Three specific red flags of leftist social justice ideology are identified: the substitution of equality of outcomes for equality of opportunity, the reduction of personhood to racial or group identity (intersectionality and critical race theory), and the oppressor/oppressed paradigm borrowed from Marxism. Each is presented as unscriptural.\n\nThe lesson then pivots from critique to exhortation. Elder Hoy offers five calls to action: do not over-correct into passivity about good works; obey Jesus in everything, which will inevitably change the world around us; consider critics honestly to see if they identify any genuine failures in Christian generosity; actively encourage and come alongside Christians doing faithful work in law, politics, foster care, addiction ministry, and church discipline; and finally, recognize our own failures and repent daily, relying on the mercies of Christ. The church as salt and light, pursuing discipleship faithfully, will be a true presence for good in the world."
