      "summary": "In this sermon on 1 John 2:3-11, Pastor Tito Lyro takes up John's concern to give believers assurance of salvation — to help them 'know that they know' Jesus. The sermon begins by noting that while everyone desires certainty, genuine assurance of saving faith is available. John provides two tests in this passage: the obedience test and the love test.\n\nThe obedience test is grounded in John's distinctly biblical use of the word 'know' — knowing God is not merely cognitive familiarity but intimate personal relationship, as with Adam and Eve. If that relationship is genuine, it will invariably produce obedience to God's Word. Someone who professes faith but whose life is characterized by disregard for Scripture is called a liar by John — not a moralistic term for terrible people, but a sober description of those in the church who claim to know Jesus while persistently disobeying Him. Pastor Lyro is careful to distinguish this from the ongoing struggle with sin that every believer experiences: John is addressing the absence of the struggle, not the presence of it.\n\nThe love test follows: the one who says he is in the light but hates his brother is still in darkness. Both tests point to abiding in Christ — remaining rooted in Him through the ordinary means of grace (preaching, the Lord's Table, baptism, prayer, and fellowship), which is what produces the fruit of obedience and love. The sermon closes with four groups identified in every local church — including the most dangerous: those who sincerely think they are believers but are not. Three diagnostic questions help hearers evaluate themselves, and repentance is held out as the simple (not easy) path for those who recognize their profession has not matched their practice."
