      "summary": "Dr. John Battle delivers the third lecture in the Runtabers Lecture Series on Historic Pre-Millennialism, defending the post-tribulational coming of Christ. He begins by grounding the hope of the rapture in 1 Thessalonians 4:13-17 and surveys the Old Testament background for the great tribulation: Jeremiah 30:7's 'time of Jacob's trouble,' Daniel's four kingdoms and the final seven-year period, and Matthew 24's description of the unprecedented tribulation immediately preceding the visible return of Christ. He calls for humility in eschatological debate, noting that many Christians have changed their views over time.\n\nThe lecture's historical section traces the pre-tribulation rapture to John Nelson Darby and the Plymouth Brethren movement of the nineteenth century, which developed dispensationalism — a system distinguishing Israel and the church as two peoples with two separate programs and promises. The Scofield Reference Bible and the Bible conference/fundamentalist movement popularized this view throughout American Christianity. Dr. Battle presents the five main pre-tribulation arguments (tribulation designed for Israel; 'church' absent from Revelation 6-19; Christians not appointed to wrath; John 14; Revelation 3) and provides Reformed responses to each, particularly emphasizing that God has never promised his people freedom from suffering — Paul himself told the churches they must enter the kingdom through many tribulations.\n\nThe lecture's exegetical heart rests on two Greek terms. The word parousia, found in Egyptian papyri and stone monuments, described an official royal visitation in which a king came in full retinue, sat on the city throne, and judged its officials — a picture directly applicable to Christ's second coming. The word apantesis ('meeting') described citizens going out to escort the visiting dignitary back into the city, not to depart with him. These two terms together show that 1 Thessalonians 4's description of believers being 'caught up to meet the Lord in the air' portrays a post-tribulational event: the church escorting the returning King to his earthly throne, not a secret pre-tribulational extraction. The lecture closes with an appeal to readiness: regardless of one's eschatological position, God will care for his people, and what matters is present faithfulness in light of Christ's certain return."
