      "summary": "Pastor Steve Brindiger, president of the board of directors of Western Reformed Seminary and a graduate of that institution, delivers the fourth and final lecture from his study at Grace Bible Presbyterian Church in Florida. His subject is the resurrection of the dead — specifically the pre-millennial thesis that the final resurrection occurs in two distinct phases: the resurrection of the righteous before the millennium and the resurrection of the unrighteous after it.\n\nThe lecture is organized in three points that move from common ground to distinctively pre-millennial territory. Point one establishes resurrection as a foundational biblical truth shared by all biblical Christianity, tracing it from Job (a contemporary of Abraham), through Philippians 3 and 1 Corinthians 15, through Jesus' declaration 'I am the resurrection and the life,' to the grand narrative arc from Genesis 3 (loss of access to the tree of life) to Revelation 22 (the tree of life restored). Point two identifies four types of resurrection in Scripture — the covenantal resurrection of salvation (death in Adam, life in Christ), temporary resurrections (Lazarus and others, who presumably died again), and the final eternal resurrections of life and judgment — and shows that these categories are acknowledged by believers of all eschatological persuasions.\n\nPoint three is the pre-millennial contribution. Drawing on Revelation 20:1-14, Pastor Brindiger argues that the 'first resurrection' of verse 4 is the bodily resurrection of believers before the millennium, not their present heavenly rest or their spiritual regeneration. He reasons that the souls of the martyrs are already depicted as present before they 'came to life,' eliminating option (a); and their death was martyrdom for Christ, not Adamic rebellion, eliminating option (b). The binding of Satan — depicted as an angel physically chaining him in the abyss — is argued to be a future event, since it cannot be reconciled with Peter's warning that Satan 'walks about as a roaring lion seeking whom he may devour' in the present age. The lecture concludes by drawing the believer's gaze forward to Revelation 21's vision of God dwelling with his people forever, where death, mourning, and pain are no more — the resurrection as the great motivating hope for present service."
