      "summary": "In this third lesson of the prayer series, Pastor Tito Lyro examines the two specific petitions Paul makes in 2 Thessalonians 1:11-12, showing how they flow directly from the framework of thanksgiving and eternal orientation established in the previous lesson. The transition word 'therefore' in verse 11 connects Paul's petitions to everything he has given thanks for — and to the kingdom-and-vindication framework he articulated in verses 5-10.\n\nThe first petition is that God would count these believers worthy of their calling. This is not asking God to make them worthy by their own effort — no one deserves to be saved by God. Rather, Paul asks God to so work in their lives that they grow up into Christian maturity, becoming in their daily walk what God has already declared them to be in Christ. This is what Paul elsewhere calls the paradox of 'become what you are.' The lesson challenges listeners: do we pray for this for our children more than we pray for their health and success?\n\nThe second petition is that God by His power would fulfill the believers' every resolve for good — the new, transformed desires that the Holy Spirit has worked in them since their conversion. Without God's power, good intentions produce no lasting fruit. Paul's example of two Bible study leaders illustrates this: one doing it for self-advancement, the other for love of the people. Paul prays for the latter kind.\n\nThe two ultimate goals of Paul's prayer are that Christ would be glorified in believers and that believers would be glorified in Christ — both ongoing now through sanctification, and fully at the resurrection. The ground for all of this is grace: the same power that raised Christ from the dead is already at work in every true believer."
