      "summary": "This Sunday School lesson picks up the series on Job by beginning the close reading of chapter 1. After a review of the seven introductory questions from the previous session, the teacher moves into the four scenes of the first chapter, using Shakespeare’s dramatic language to organize the narrative.\n\nScene one introduces our hero: Job, a real historical man who lived in the land of Uz around 2000 BC. He was rich (in the patriarchal sense: children, livestock, and reputation), and above all he was righteous. The teacher spends time on what ‘blameless’ means in this context — not sinless perfection, but consistent outward behavior matching an inward profession. God himself attested to this in verse 8. Understanding Job’s righteousness is key to the plot because it directly refutes Satan’s coming accusation.\n\nScene two introduces the villain: Satan, described by the text as ‘going to and fro on the earth.’ What is remarkable — and troubling — about this scene is that it is God who draws Satan’s attention to Job. God instigates this test. Satan’s accusation is not really about Job; Job is merely a pawn in a cosmic chess match aimed at undermining God’s worthiness to be worshiped. Satan’s thesis is that God can only ‘buy’ worship with blessings, and that if the blessings were removed, Job would curse God to his face.\n\nScene three describes the battalion of sorrows that follows in rapid, escalating succession: first the oxen and donkeys, then the sheep, then the camels, and finally all ten children — each messenger arriving while the previous is still speaking. The plot tension is now set: will Job vindicate God or curse him?\n\nScene four provides the partial resolution. Job tears his robe, shaves his head, and falls to the ground and worships — grief and faith in the same breath. His words, ‘the Lord gave and the Lord has taken away; blessed be the name of the Lord,’ are not uttered in a triumphant major key but as a mournful, grief-filled submission. Verse 22 declares that in all this, Job did not sin or charge God with wrong. Satan’s thesis was falsified, but the book is only beginning."
