      "summary": "This lesson introduces the longest and most theologically challenging section of the book of Job: chapters 4-27, which contain three cycles of dialogue between Job and his three friends, Eliphaz the Temanite, Bildad the Shuhite, and Zophar the Naamathite. The teacher notes that this section is written in Hebrew poetry and is fully inspired by God (2 Timothy 3:16), though interpreting it is complex because God later tells the friends they were wrong without always specifying exactly where.\n\nThe session covers who the three friends are — their likely origins, their personalities and rhetorical styles, and their shared theological framework: retribution theology. This is the idea that the righteous are always materially blessed and the wicked always suffer, making suffering a sure sign of personal sin. Each friend represents a slightly different approach: Eliphaz is relatively measured and seems to feel genuine compassion; Bildad is blunt and indignant; Zophar is the most brutal, mocking Job and telling him he deserves more suffering than he has received. All three are wrong, as God declares in chapter 42.\n\nThe teacher explains the dramatic role the friends play in the book's plot. They are not merely misguided counselors; they function as Satan's legal team, tasked with convincing Job that God is unjust and that he must have sinned to deserve his suffering. When physical affliction failed to turn Job from God, Satan deployed theological attack through the mouths of trusted friends. This makes chapters 4-27 a sustained illustration of how dangerous retribution theology is — and why so much biblical real estate is devoted to refuting it.\n\nThe lesson concludes with practical application: true biblical counsel sometimes wounds, but like a surgeon's scalpel it heals. The church must give and receive counsel that is rooted in Scripture and contextual, neither dismissing hard words as the counsel of Job's friends nor offering the kind of counsel that drives people away from God."
