      "summary": "This sermon on 2 Corinthians 3:1-6 examines Paul's response to the Corinthian church's demand for his apostolic credentials. Paul's answer is striking: rather than producing letters of recommendation, he points the Corinthians to their own transformed lives as the evidence that his gospel is the true gospel of Jesus Christ. The sermon develops four truths from this passage, each centered on the sufficiency of Christ.\n\nFirst, the redeemed Christian is a letter of commendation to the gospel. The changed lives of the Corinthians — once fornicators, thieves, homosexuals, drunkards — are irrefutable evidence that Paul's message was from Christ. Similarly, every believer bears Christ's name and commends or misrepresents Him by the way they live, even in their failures, because the grace of a God who forgives on His worst day as on His best remains unchanged.\n\nSecond, the change that comes through the gospel is at the heart level, not superficial. Paul's imagery in verse 3 deliberately echoes the new covenant promises of Jeremiah 31 and Ezekiel 36: a new heart of flesh replacing a heart of stone, the Spirit dwelling within and causing God's people to delight in His law rather than merely obey it externally.\n\nThird, the believer's confidence is entirely in Christ. Paul did not come with eloquence or persuasive wisdom. The church's spiritual health does not depend on the pastor's gifts. Christ is sufficient — not in the minimal sense but in the all-encompassing sense that He is all that is needed. Fourth, life comes through the Spirit who works through the proclaimed Word. The law kills self-sufficiency and drives the sinner to Christ; the Spirit gives life through that same gospel. The proper use of the law is not as a ladder to righteousness but as a mirror exposing need, preparing the sinner to receive the life only the Spirit can give."
