      "summary": "This Sunday school lesson was delivered during Pride Month and addresses the LGBTQ+ movement and homosexuality from a biblical perspective, answering six questions the teacher identifies as essential for thinking through these issues. The lesson is distinguished by its effort to be both theologically clear and pastorally compassionate, treating homosexuality neither as the unparalleled sin nor as something Scripture leaves unaddressed.\n\nThe first and most substantial section establishes from Romans 1 and Matthew 19 that homosexuality is sin — not because of cultural convention but because it contradicts God's design for sexuality and marriage. The teacher also places homosexuality within the larger context of Romans 1:28-32, noting that gossip and arrogance appear in the same list, and uses this to check the tendency to treat homosexuality as a uniquely disqualifying sin. Common revisionist arguments (Sodom's sin was inhospitality, only idolatrous homosexuality is condemned, faithful homosexual relationships are acceptable) are addressed and answered from Scripture.\n\nThe lesson then distinguishes between same-sex attraction as a struggle — which may not be sinful in itself, since temptation by definition requires some hold on us — and acting on that attraction, which is. The teacher affirms that Christians may struggle with same-sex attraction while holding that their identity is in Christ, not in their sin. Churches that formally affirm homosexuality are assessed as having stepped outside the authority of Christ, who has all authority over His church.\n\nThe session closes with six principles from author Moe for relating to homosexuals in a Christ-honoring way, emphasizing that we must love the people more than we love condemning their sin, that our own marriages must commend the biblical vision, and that the ultimate answer — as with all sin — is the gospel of Jesus Christ and the transforming power of the Spirit."
