      "summary": "This sermon was preached on a Sunday when new covenant members and children were being received into the church, giving occasion to address the raising of covenant children from Colossians 2:1-10. The text is used primarily as a launching point for teaching about the formative influence all people exert and receive at all times, and how parents and the church should respond to this reality.\n\nThe sermon opens with the sobering observation from Judges 2:10 that after Joshua's generation died, the next generation arose who did not know the Lord. This is the failure the church must labor to prevent. Everything in life is either biblically accurate or ungodly influence — there is no neutral category. Two dangers are identified: not realizing that formation is happening constantly, and realizing it but doing nothing about it.\n\nThe teacher then uses a military metaphor to identify the enemy: Satan, who walks about as a roaring lion and commands an army through the world system. Sincere Christian parents who allow uncritical consumption of worldly culture are, in effect, housing the enemy. The world's classroom is described as deceptive and relentless, constantly teaching children to place the self at the center and treat God and His authority as repulsive.\n\nAgainst this, parents are called to become skilled in biblical formative instruction — not as a single event or a disciplinary tool but as a lifetime of ordinary, constant engagement rooted in personal love for God's Word. The distinction between formative instruction (ongoing) and discipline (corrective) is important: if the Bible only appears as a club, it becomes a source of provocation rather than a treasure.\n\nThe sermon closes with encouragement: God is not looking for perfect parents but for faithful, repentant ones. He has made a covenantal promise to be God to believers and their children, and He uses parents and the church as instruments to fulfill it."
