      "summary": "Guest speaker Pastor Steve Brinegar of Grace Bible Presbyterian Church in Cape Canaveral, Florida, preaches on James 1:16-21 with a focus on verse 19: the triple command to be swift to hear, slow to speak, and slow to wrath. Drawing on the illustration of a fire captain whose years of training made him instinctively sense danger, Pastor Brinegar argues that listening to God's Word must become so ingrained in believers that it shapes every decision and response. James 1:19 is not merely practical advice for human communication — it is about how Christians receive the living Word of God.\n\nThe sermon develops each of the three commands in turn as they apply to hearing Scripture. Being swift to hear means positioning oneself to receive the Word — through faithful church attendance, daily Bible reading, and running to Scripture first when facing difficult decisions. Being slow to speak means letting the Bible speak before our own opinions and not treating it as merely one voice among many. Being slow to wrath means receiving God's truth with patience rather than reacting when it challenges our sense of what we deserve — for we deserve condemnation, and every good gift, including salvation, is entirely of grace.\n\nPastor Brinegar grounds the sermon in 2 Corinthians 4:16-18, which reframes earthly afflictions as 'light momentary' compared to an eternal weight of glory. The danger of anger is that it shifts our focus from what the Bible actually says to what we think we deserve. Samuel Miller's summary of Reformed doctrine is quoted: all that is evil in man is of himself; all that is good is of God. The sermon concludes with a call to fill both mind and heart with Scripture daily, using Psalm 119:11 as a picture of storing God's Word as a well-organized toolbox ready for use."
