      "summary": "This is the 53rd and final sermon in a series on the book of Ephesians, concluding over a year of preaching through what Pastor Lyro regards as the deepest of Paul's letters. Ephesians is about the church — those redeemed by the Spirit of God through the gospel — whose otherwise insignificant lives are connected to a cosmic plan of redemption reaching from Genesis 1 to Revelation 22. The sermon focuses on the final four verses of Ephesians (6:21-24), where Paul commends Tychicus and offers two closing benedictions.\n\nPastor Lyro draws two main lessons from the commendation of Tychicus. First, believers who are followers of Jesus Christ are genuinely interested in each other's lives. Paul, though in a Roman prison awaiting trial for his life, is more concerned with comforting the Ephesians who are worried about him than with his own circumstances — just as Christ, dying on the cross, made provision to comfort Mary. One evidence that the Spirit is working in us is concern for one another. Second, Paul fosters life in community by sending Tychicus with both the letter and an oral personal report. Quoting a sermon illustration from Dr. Nick Wilburne, the pastor depicts the church as individual lights that, brought together and focused, shine brightly throughout the world. Tychicus is described with three characteristics — beloved brother, faithful minister, fellow slave of Christ — that are not for super-Christians but for every believer.\n\nThe sermon closes by examining Paul's two benedictions: peace (the total well-being that enables God's people to relate without fear, aimed at peace among the brethren), love with faith (the indiscriminate giving love of John 3:16), and grace (God's favor resting on all who love the Lord Jesus Christ with incorruptible love). After 53 sermons and a year of ministry in Ephesians, Pastor Lyro prays that the congregation would be that powerful beam of light, together, signifying that Christ is Lord of their lives."
