      "summary": "Pastor Lyro preaches on 2 John 4-11 in what appears to be the first of a series covering 2 and 3 John. The occasion of the letter is a church that was receiving false teachers in the name of love, not realizing that love rooted in wrong doctrine about Christ is not truly Christian love. John's purpose is to show that Christian love must be grounded in the correct understanding of who Jesus Christ is.\n\nThe sermon develops three major truths from the passage. First, because Christian love is rooted in correct doctrine, we rejoice when our brothers and sisters walk in the truth. Pastor Lyro offers seven reasons why this should be our supreme joy: Jesus is the truth (John 14); God is only worshiped in truth; truth sets us free (John 8); the Holy Spirit is the Spirit of truth (John 14); walking in truth means being indwelt by the Spirit; growing in truth means growing in love; and our Lord prayed for our sanctification through truth. Second, the command to love one another according to the Father's commandment is not new — it has been from the very beginning, which means that the doctrine of the Trinity and of the person of Christ is not a later invention (not fabricated at Nicaea in AD 325) but is foundational to Christianity from the start. To receive teachers who deny that Christ came in the flesh is the opposite of loving one another.\n\nThird, apart from the full Jesus of Scripture, one does not have the God of the Bible at all. There are only two options: believe in the triune God with Christ as truly God and truly man, or do not believe in the God of the Bible. The doctrine of the hypostatic union — Christ as fully God and fully man — is essential not as an abstract puzzle but as the very basis of our salvation: as man He stands in our place, and as God He can bear the infinite wrath of God for us in a finite amount of time. The sermon closes with three practical applications: grow in love with Jesus by learning more about who He is; share Christ with one another; and be jealous for the truth of who Christ is, since theological error has a wider damning effect than behavioral sin."
