      "summary": "In this exposition of Deuteronomy 6:1-9, Pastor Lyro teaches on the Shema — Israel's most recognizable prayer and the passage Jesus called the first and greatest commandment. Preached with Israel pictured standing on the eastern bank of the Jordan after forty years in the wilderness, the sermon shows that this command to love God is not for a small ethnic group in an ancient moment but is the universal standard for every person who has ever lived, affirmed by Christ Himself in Matthew 22.\n\nThe sermon identifies four characteristics of the love God commands. It is a gracious love — we cannot produce it ourselves; God must circumcise our hearts (Deuteronomy 30:6), and we love only because He first loved us (1 John 4:19). It is a complete love — with all our heart, soul, mind, and strength, meaning every skill, talent, and relationship must be subsumed under and expressed as love for God. It is an intelligent love — rooted in what God has revealed, which means we cannot love Him without loving His Word; the command to love is immediately followed by the command to teach the Word everywhere. And it is an active love — not merely a feeling, but evidenced by obedience: \"we show that we love the Lord by doing what He says He wants us to do.\"\n\nPastor Lyro applies the text broadly to every sphere of life. The first application from the text itself is Christian education: the Word is to govern children's learning at every moment (sitting, walking, lying down, rising). Beyond that, living out love for God transforms every relationship: marriages, parent-child relationships, use of gifts and resources, and decisions about work and self-control. The sermon closes with a simple and urgent answer to the question \"Why love God?\": because as one redeemed by Christ, you cannot do anything else — if you understand what God has done for you in Jesus Christ, love is the only possible response."
