      "summary": "This sermon on 1 Timothy 3:1-13, titled 'Meddling,' is preached in preparation for the ordination of Jim Connerly as a deacon of the Bible Presbyterian Church. Pastor Lyro introduces it by announcing his intention to meddle — not just preach — both in his own life and in the lives of the congregation. He acknowledges that in preparing this sermon, the Spirit brought him great conviction about how he himself has fallen short of the mark God has set for undershepherds.\n\nThe sermon begins with the 'faithful saying' of verse 1: that if a man desires the position of a bishop, he desires a good work. Pastor Lyro explains that the word 'bishop' is another term for elder or pastor — one who looks upon, an overseer — and that 'desire' here is an earnest yearning for service, not for rank. The desire is humble when the man is already serving whether or not he holds a title, and when it is accompanied by fear and trembling at the weight of the office.\n\nThe heart of the 'meddling' is directed at every man in the congregation: these qualifications describe godly manhood, not a super-spiritual elite. Men who do not meet these qualifications should repent and pursue godly manhood. Pastor Lyro affirms the scriptural teaching that the offices of elder and deacon are only for men, grounding this not in culture but in the doctrine of creation and the fall (1 Timothy 2:12). He addresses the wives of potential elders and deacons — their support or resistance directly affects their husband's qualification for office. He also looks children in the eye and tells them that their rebellion against godly parents may be robbing the church of its leadership.\n\nThe deaconate is described as the sancho — the anchor — for the health of the church, not an afterthought or junior elder position. When the first deacons were ordained in Acts 6, the Word spread and disciples multiplied. The sermon closes with the actual ordination service for Jim Connerly, including his vows before the congregation and the laying on of hands by the elders."
