      "summary": "This sermon on 3 John 1-4 is the second message in a series on 2 and 3 John, preached in the afternoon session of the same day as the 2 John sermon. Pastor Lyro sets the table by explaining the occasion of the letter: around AD 90, the Apostle John writes to Gaius because the leader Diotrephes — who loves preeminence — is refusing to provide hospitality to traveling evangelists and is even threatening to put out of the church those who would. John writes to encourage Gaius, to address Diotrephes, and to commend Demetrius, in preparation for John's own personal visit to settle the matter.\n\nThe sermon identifies two main lessons from verses 1-4. First, Christian care extends to both body and soul. John prays for Gaius's physical health and prosperity, but his greater concern is the prosperity of Gaius's soul. The point for the congregation is that our prayer lists often reflect our priorities — abundant prayers for physical ailments, fewer prayers for spiritual growth. But the great prayers of the New Testament (particularly Paul's in the epistles) are overwhelmingly for the inner man: strengthening by the Spirit, comprehension of the love of Christ, being filled with all the fullness of God.\n\nSecond, John's great joy comes from hearing that Gaius is walking in the truth — and this should be the greatest joy of every Christian regarding the brothers and sisters around them. Pastor Lyro explains that the truth has become Gaias's own truth: not \"your truth\" in the modern relativistic sense, but God's objective truth has been subjectively appropriated by Gaius — it has become his very own. This is what sanctification looks like. The sermon closes with two applications: spiritual growth in our brothers and sisters should bring us great joy (using the illustration of a toilet tank where all the water rises together), and parents should make intentional spiritual provision for their children — family devotions, prayer, modeling love for the Word and for the church."
