      "summary": "Guest speaker Pastor Henry Flowers expounds Philippians 3:7-9 to demonstrate that there is no greater gain in life than knowing Christ Jesus as Lord. Drawing on Paul's own testimony, he shows how Paul's impressive religious pedigree—circumcision, tribal lineage, zeal as a Pharisee—became worthless the moment he encountered the risen Christ, because all these achievements could not provide the righteousness of God that comes through faith alone. Like a man who must give up cake to gain good health, Paul had to lose confidence in the flesh to gain Christ.\n\nFlowers applies this doctrine with pastoral warmth, drawing parallels to Martin Luther's struggle with self-righteousness and morbid introspection. Both men found peace not by striving harder but by resting in the finished work of Christ—his active obedience credited to them, his passive obedience bearing their condemnation. The sermon concludes with a practical call to fight the daily urge to return to self-righteousness by meditating on the Great Exchange."
