      "summary": "Elder Scott Hollander draws from the account of Daniel in the lions' den (Daniel 6) to challenge believers to obey God in their spiritual disciplines regardless of perceived consequences or threats. Setting the historical scene—Daniel at age 81, distinguished by an excellent spirit that his enemies could only attack through his faith—Hollander shows how Daniel's habitual prayer three times daily was not disrupted even when it became illegal. He poses a searching question to the congregation: is your faith so consistent that it could be used as evidence against you?\n\nThe sermon honestly acknowledges that God does not always rescue his people the way he rescued Daniel, drawing a contrast with Ignatius of Antioch who was martyred. Believers are called to grow in the ordinary means of grace—prayer, Scripture reading, and corporate worship—not because God will always deliver them from trial, but because God uses those disciplines to prepare them for whatever comes. The sermon closes with the encouragement that divine justice is certain, and that those who suffer faithfully will be vindicated at history's end."
