      "summary": "Elder Andrew Hoy leads an evening meditation on John 6:51—'The bread that I shall give is my flesh, which I shall give for the life of the world'—showing that our greatest need is not physical comfort, political security, or community, but redemption from sin and restored relationship with God. Using John 6 as a frame, he shows how Jesus systematically exposed the hardened hearts of those following him for the wrong reasons, until many departed because they wanted a Jesus who would serve their own desires rather than the Jesus who came to save them from their sins.\n\nThe sermon closes by reading the entire crucifixion account from John 19 aloud as a congregation, letting the weight of Christ's suffering speak for itself. Hoy applies the text with a searching question: 'What do you want from Jesus?' Bread, politics, family, and ideal community cannot satisfy the soul's deepest need. Only the righteousness of Jesus Christ—received through faith in the one who gave his flesh for the life of the world—can meet that need."
