      "summary": "This Sunday School lesson launches an eight-part series on prayer using Westminster Larger Catechism questions 176-189 as a guide. Pastor Tito Lyro begins by explaining the value of the catechism as a teaching tool — rooted in Scripture and systematically organized — and traces the history of the Westminster Assembly and its method of grounding every doctrinal statement in biblical proof texts. He then turns to WLC Q178, which defines prayer as offering up our desires to God in the name of Christ by the help of His Spirit, with confession of sins and thankful acknowledgment of His mercies.\n\nA central emphasis of the lesson is distinguishing authentic Christian prayer from all other forms of religious prayer. Lyro argues that the only true prayer is addressed to the triune God and offered through Christ as mediator — any prayer directed elsewhere is, in effect, idolatrous. He draws out the implications: unbelieving Jews, liberal Christians who deny Christ's deity, and others who do not pray through the God-man mediator are not praying to the God of the Bible. The session's practical goal is clear: the church should become not merely intellectual Christians who understand prayer, but a praying people."
