      "summary": "In Part 3 of the prayer series, Elder Nick Anderson addresses Westminster Larger Catechism questions 180 and 181, which deal with what it means to pray in the name of Christ and why we must pray in His name alone. After reviewing the earlier lessons, he establishes that prayer is not optional — it is commanded throughout Scripture, and a person who does not pray is a person who does not worship. The first instance of prayer in the Bible (Genesis 4:26) shows that calling on the name of the Lord is the fundamental act of religious worship.\n\nAnderson then expounds eight reasons why believers can pray with confidence in Jesus' name: God's promises, Christ's role as our advocate, His sympathy with our weakness, His suffering in our temptation, His propitiation of God's wrath, His opening of heaven, His role as the one who brings us to the Father, and the fact that the prayer of the upright is a delight to God. He closes by applying WLC Q181, which teaches that because of human sinfulness there is no access to God except through a mediator, and no mediator except Christ — therefore we pray in His name alone."
