      "summary": "In Part 5 of the prayer series, Pastor Tito Lyro addresses Westminster Larger Catechism questions 183 and 184, which together cover for whom and for what we should pray. He notes that Scripture's priorities in prayer are often the reverse of ours: there is a direct command to pray for civil leaders and for the church, but no direct command to pray for unsaved family members (though it is implied). The catechism lists the whole Church of Christ on earth as the first and primary focus of prayer, followed by magistrates and ministers, then ourselves, our brethren, and even our enemies.\n\nLyro also treats those for whom we are not to pray: the dead, whose state is fixed at death, and those known to have committed the sin unto death. He connects this unpardonable sin to the blasphemy of the Holy Spirit and to the warning of Hebrews 6 — a permanent, willful rejection of the Spirit's work with no remaining desire for repentance. On the question of what to pray for, he teaches that the primary consideration must always be the glory of God, which requires knowing the Bible. Prayer and Scripture study are inseparable: we cannot know what glorifies God without studying His Word."
