      "summary": "In Part 6 of the prayer series, Mr. Alex Lyro addresses Westminster Larger Catechism questions 185 and 186, which describe how Christians are to pray and what rule God has given for prayer. He works through fifteen characteristics of prayer from Q185, emphasizing that all of them begin before prayer itself — they reflect a whole-life orientation toward God that shapes the soul long before we kneel. Among the most important: reverential awe of God's majesty, honest consciousness of our own unworthiness, penitent and thankful hearts, and the faith that anchors prayer in the character and promises of God rather than in our own feelings or performance.\n\nFor Q186, Lyro explains that the entire Word of God directs prayer — it commands prayer, promises answers, records examples, and sets boundaries. But the special rule is the Lord's Prayer, the only prayer Christ explicitly taught His disciples. Its purpose is not mindless repetition but theological shaping: to keep our prayers from being self-centered, aimless, or shallow, and to ground them in God's glory. He closes with five application points encouraging the congregation to grow in prayer as a whole-life discipline."
