      "summary": "In Part 7 of the prayer series, Mr. Nick Newton addresses Westminster Larger Catechism questions 187 and 188, which ask how many parts the Lord's Prayer consists of and how it is to be used. Working backwards, he begins with Q188 and walks through each of the six petitions in Matthew 6, showing that God's glory — not human need — is the central theme and organizing principle of the prayer. The first petition (hallowed be thy name) covers all that God has revealed of himself; the second and third (thy kingdom come, thy will be done) look to gospel advance and sanctified obedience; the fourth (daily bread) calls us to dependence and contentment; the fifth (forgive our debts as we forgive debtors) carries the sobering conjunction \"as\"; and the sixth (deliver us from evil) acknowledges ongoing vulnerability to temptation.\n\nFor Q187, Newton explains that the Lord's Prayer has a dual function: it serves both as a template for personal prayer and as a prayer to be prayed verbatim, provided it is done with understanding, faith, and reverence. He traces the Westminster Divines' distinction between Matthew 6:9 (\"in this manner pray\") and Luke 11:2 (\"when you pray\"), showing both uses are legitimate. He closes with three practical applications: using the prayer to shape personal petitions, using it to disciple new believers, and using it to teach children to pray."
