      "summary": "In the final lesson of the prayer series, Mr. Steve Thomas addresses Westminster Larger Catechism question 189, which asks what the preface to the Lord's Prayer teaches. After a brief defense of using the catechism — showing that it is not a substitute for Scripture but a systematically organized summary of it — he unpacks three words in the preface: Father, our, and in heaven. On Father, he asks who can rightly call God their Father and what that means, drawing on Psalm 103, Luke 11, and Romans 8 to show that God is not a distant creator but an intimately involved Father who pities us, delights to give us gifts, and has adopted us through Christ.\n\nOn the word \"our,\" Thomas refutes the idea of a nebulous universal fatherhood and instead shows that the plural points to two realities: the believer's personal adoption (our interest therein) and the call to pray corporately and for others (1 Timothy 2, Acts 12). On \"in heaven,\" he describes the catechism's dual affirmation: we approach God with reverence because he is sovereign and holy, and yet with confidence because Christ has opened the way. He closes the entire series with a charge from Paul: do not put these tools on a shelf. We have learned how to pray correctly — now go forth as a praying people."
