      "summary": "This opening sermon in a series on pride and humility challenges the congregation to make 2022 a year of growing in humility by identifying and killing pride. Pastor Lyro defines pride as an excessive preoccupation with self — including one's importance, achievements, status, possessions, and rights — and demonstrates from Scripture that God not only calls pride a sin but actively hates it and promises to judge the proud. Drawing on Richard Hooker's image of peeling an oyster, he argues that pride is the deepest and most stubborn of all sins, rooted in self-worship and self-centeredness, and that it underlies almost every relational failure we experience both vertically with God and horizontally with one another.\n\nTo help the congregation diagnose pride in their own hearts, Pastor Lyro walks through 32 distinct manifestations of pride — from complaining against God and anger, to perfectionism, manipulation, unteachableness, bitterness, and being more concerned with vindication than God's glory. He emphasizes that listeners should hear these not for others but for themselves, since every person with a beating heart struggles with pride. The sermon closes by pointing to the gospel as the only remedy: genuine repentance before God, confession to Christ, and receiving His forgiveness causes pride to die and humility to grow, as the mind of Christ — who humbled Himself to the cross — is formed in the believer by the Spirit."
