      "outline": [
        {
          "point": "I. Review: Pride Is a Serious Sin",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. Pride is an excessive preoccupation with self, rights, and importance",
            "B. God hates pride and promises to judge the proud",
            "C. Pride always ends in destruction (Proverbs 16:18)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "II. Pride Begins in the Heart (Luke 9:46-48)",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. Jesus perceived the disciples' proud thoughts directly",
            "B. Pride was the root of Eve's first sin in the garden",
            "C. We must deal with pride at the heart level through confession"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "III. Pride Is Fed by Competition; Humility by Cooperation",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. The disciples competed to be greatest — Jesus silenced comparison with a child",
            "B. All gifts and abilities come from Christ; we are stewards, not owners",
            "C. Philippians 2:3-4: esteem others better than yourselves"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "IV. Humble Love Is Tolerant with Fellow Servants (Luke 9:49-50)",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. John forbade an exorcist not in their group — Jesus rebuked this",
            "B. Religious zeal can mask pride — fighting for purity must be done in humble love",
            "C. We often cloak pride under the banner of doctrinal correctness"
          ]
        },
        {
          "point": "V. Humble Love Lets God Judge Those Who Oppose Us (Luke 9:51-56)",
          "subpoints": [
            "A. James and John wanted to call down fire on the Samaritans — Jesus rebuked them",
            "B. Humble love does not seek revenge; vengeance belongs to the Lord (Romans 12:19)",
            "C. Living in humility and love is a powerful witness in a pride-saturated culture"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "This second sermon in the Pride and Humility series turns to the text of Luke 9:46-56 to show what effective service for Christ looks like when pride is mortified and humility takes root. After briefly reviewing the prior week's 32 manifestations of pride and the Bible's clear condemnation of it, Pastor Lyro examines the disciples' absurd argument about greatness — held in the very context of Jesus heading to the cross — to demonstrate that pride begins in the heart and must be confronted there. He highlights how pride is fed by competition and comparison, while humility thrives in cooperation, noting that since everything we have comes from Christ, there is no basis for boasting over others.\n\nThe sermon then moves through two episodes in the passage to show how humble love must characterize our treatment of both fellow servants and opponents of Christ. Regarding John's exclusion of the outside exorcist, Pastor Lyro warns that religious zeal often conceals pride, and that fighting for the purity of the church must always be conducted in humble love. Regarding James and John's desire to call fire down on the Samaritan village, the sermon teaches that humble love yields judgment to God, loving enemies rather than seeking revenge. The call to kill pride and grow in humility before 2022 is not merely ethical; it is deeply evangelical, rooted in living in the shadow of the blazing cross where our pride was crucified with Christ."
