      "summary": "Prompted by a meeting with fellow pastors who were worn down by their congregations' unwillingness to gather, Pastor Lyro turns to Hebrews 10:19-31 to demonstrate that Christians cannot arrive at the end of the race without one another — God has appointed mutual exhortation as one of the means by which He preserves His people to glory. The passage begins with the gospel: because Christ's blood has opened the holy place and He now intercedes as our great high priest, we have bold access to the Father, and that confidence should compel us into three gospel actions — drawing near to God, holding fast our confession of hope, and thinking carefully about how to stir each other up to love and good works.\n\nThe heart of the sermon unpacks Hebrews 10:24-25, showing that the word translated 'consider' demands thorough, strategic, care-filled attention to the spiritual state of fellow believers, while the word 'stir up' — normally meaning to irritate or provoke — is here used positively to describe the energy and intentionality we should bring to encouraging one another. This happens by not forsaking the assembly (which goes beyond physical presence to include attitude and engagement) and by earnestly exhorting one another to hold fast to the confession that Jesus is Lord. The sermon closes with the sobering warning that abandoning Christ leaves no further sacrifice for sin, and that the day when every believer will meet God is approaching — making the ministry of mutual encouragement urgent and eternal in its stakes."
