      "summary": "The third in a four-part series on Psalm 103, this morning sermon works through verses 15-18, contrasting the temporary nature of human life with the eternal covenant faithfulness of God. Pastor Lyro begins by noting that David compares human life to a wildflower — it is genuinely beautiful, to be celebrated and cherished, but also transient, fragile, dependent on God, and ultimately forgettable. This realism is not meant to depress but to humble and redirect, leading to eight applications for how we should live in light of our mortality: making the most of time, not clinging to earthly things, looking forward to eternal life rather than obsessing over preserving this one, facing death honestly, cultivating humility, resting from self-importance, putting life 'under the sun' in perspective, and lifting our eyes to the eternal God.\n\nThe sermon then pivots to the glorious contrast of verse 17: God's mercy and faithfulness are from everlasting to everlasting. The eternal God, who chose His people before the foundation of the world, is never for a moment unfaithful to them. This truth generates eleven further applications: letting God's unchanging character steady the heart, anchoring identity in His covenant love rather than in performance or circumstances, interpreting trials in light of eternity, passing on a legacy of faith to children and grandchildren, recovering a reverent fear of the Lord, and pursuing total covenant loyalty with no untouched corners of life. The sermon closes with the image that our life may be a flower, but God's love is the mountain on which we stand — brief days to be filled with obedience, gratitude, and worship."
