      "summary": "The concluding sermon in the four-part Psalm 103 series, preached in the afternoon service, brings the Psalm to its grand finale in verses 19-22. Pastor Lyro begins by noting the Psalm's literary 'inclusive' structure: it opens and closes with 'Bless the Lord, oh my soul,' moving outward from the heart of the believer through all creation and returning to rest again in the redeemed soul's worship. The central declaration of verse 19 — that the Lord has established His throne in heaven and His kingdom rules over all — is grounded in the resurrection and ascension of Christ, which Paul in Ephesians 1 and Philippians 2 identifies as the decisive event that proclaimed His universal kingship.\n\nThe sermon works through the cascading invitations to bless the Lord: first the mighty angels who excel in strength and whose entire purpose is to worship; then all the heavenly hosts — stars, planets, every created thing; and finally all God's works in all places. Yet the most intimate and ultimate invitation comes last: 'Bless the Lord, oh my soul.' To bless God is not to add anything He lacks but to ascribe to Him all that He is, to speak well of Him in reverent adoration. David commands his own soul — his entire inner life, mind, will, emotions, spirit — to engage fully in worship. This is not mechanical religion but heart-deep praise from one who has encountered the God of Psalm 103 — the God who forgives, heals, redeems, crowns, satisfies, is merciful and gracious, and whose love endures from everlasting to everlasting."
