      "summary": "In this Sunday School lesson on Psalm 2, Elder Scott Hollander presents the Psalm as the first and most quoted Messianic Psalm in the New Testament, exploring its literary structures — parallelism, chiasm, and inclusion — to help students engage more deeply with Hebrew poetry in their own reading. The lesson works through the Psalm's four movements: the futile rage of the nations against the Lord and His anointed; God's sovereign laughter and the proclamation of His king on Zion; the divine decree declaring the Son's identity and inheritance; and the summons to the kings of the earth to submit or be destroyed. Elder Hollander grounds the Psalm in the Davidic covenant of 2 Samuel 7, showing that while it had a historical dimension at every Davidic coronation, its prophetic horizon always pointed to Jesus Christ.\n\nThe New Testament use of Psalm 2 is developed throughout, with Acts 4:25-28 confirming that the conspiring kings and rulers were fulfilled in Herod, Pilate, and both Gentiles and Israel plotting against Jesus — yet doing only what God's hand and purpose had determined. Acts 13 and Romans 1:4 identify the resurrection as the public declaration of Christ's sonship, the event that proclaimed His universal kingship. The lesson closes with Psalm 2:10-12, which calls the nations to submit to the Son's easy yoke or be dashed to pieces, and notes the Psalm's literary inclusion with Psalm 1: the 'blessed' of Psalm 1:1 is answered by the 'blessed' of Psalm 2:12 — those who take refuge in the Son."
