      "summary": "Beginning with the historical and physical resurrection of Jesus Christ as an irrefutable fact attested by over 500 witnesses, this Easter-season sermon works through Philippians 3:1-16 to show that the resurrection must be the motivation for every believer's pursuit of Christ. Paul himself met the risen Christ on the road to Damascus, and that encounter transformed him from a persecutor of the church into the greatest missionary in history. Pastor Lyro identifies two errors troubling the Philippian church — Jewish legalism (confidence in religious pedigree and performance) and antinomianism (faith without obedience) — showing that both ultimately drive people away from Christ and result in self-centered lives, because legalism makes Christ unnecessary and antinomianism makes Him optional.\n\nThe sermon then offers four reasons we must pursue the risen Christ: to know Him ever more deeply, to demonstrate that our faith is genuine, because we are still far from perfect, and because Christ has laid hold of us to conform us to His likeness. The 'how' of pursuing Christ involves three disciplines: cultivating a holy dissatisfaction by looking honestly at ourselves in the mirror of God's Word; forgetting what lies behind so that past failures or successes do not paralyze or inflate us; and straining forward with the full energy of an athlete lunging at the finish line. The sermon closes with Paul's supreme desire expressed in verse 9 — not to be seen in his own righteousness or accomplishments, but to be found in Christ, clothed in His righteousness. This is the only sufficient ground for standing before God."
