      "summary": "Dr. John Battle expounds 1 Thessalonians 2:13, examining how the Thessalonian believers received the word of God when Paul brought it to them. He focuses on the emphatic construction 'for this reason'—which Paul uses to arrest attention before explaining that his thanksgiving is specifically because they received the gospel not as a human message but as the very word of God. The Greek word for 'received' carries the sense of official acceptance with personal responsibility, like a guard taking legal custody of a prisoner, and their reception of the word is itself evidence of their divine election.\n\nDr. Battle enriches the exposition with a striking historical illustration from Benjamin Franklin's autobiography, in which Franklin's Puritan ancestors under the reign of Queen Mary hid an English Bible under a joint stool—kept open with leather straps—so the father could read it to the family while a child stood watch at the door. This underscores the cost and courage that once accompanied the reception of God's word. Paul's own labor in bringing the gospel to Thessalonica is similarly costly. The sermon closes with the three-fold challenge to receive Scripture with submission (welcoming it in without resistance), with assurance (trusting it as truly God's word), and with labor (working diligently to bring it to others as Paul did)."
