      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro expounds Psalm 34, written when David feigned madness before Abimelech (1 Samuel 21), and draws out six ways believers are called to relate to God. The Psalm is introduced as normative for all believers — David as a representative Psalmist giving voice to the experience every Christian should have. The six themes appear in the order of the Psalm: celebrate, pray, taste, fear, obey, and trust.\n\nThe sermon moves through each theme with pastoral application. Celebrating God displaces gossip, slander, and complaining from our lips; praising God is contagious among the humble. Praying to God produces a visible radiance — those who look to Him are shining. Tasting God means inviting the whole person into spiritual experience, not just the intellect, because there is a sweetness to knowing God that never loses its capacity to satisfy. Fearing God is paired with enjoying Him — we must both adore Him and tremble, and losing the awe of God is a warning sign of spiritual danger. Obeying God is rewarded with His watchful and tender gaze; He cannot take His eyes off the righteous. Trusting God must be rightly aimed — He will not fulfill promises He never made, but He will unfailingly keep every promise He has given."
