      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro expounds Habakkuk 3:16-19, the climactic conclusion of the prophet's dialogue with God. The sermon is set against the backdrop of Habakkuk's double complaint: first, that God is tolerating injustice within Judah (1:1-4), and then, more shockingly, that God intends to use the pagan Babylonians — a nation more wicked than Judah — as His instrument of judgment. The theological answer God gives in 2:4 — the just shall live by faith — becomes the bedrock upon which the final doxology of chapter 3 is built.\n\nThe passage moves from fear to faith to joy in three steps. Habakkuk begins in physical trembling at the hearing of God's word (3:16), a response the sermon affirms as appropriate — authentic faith does not suppress trembling before God's majesty but rests through it. He then looks back at God's history of deliverance in 3:1-15, using the memory of past acts as fuel for present trust. The sermon's central challenge comes from the radical either-or of 3:17: even if every source of material provision fails completely — no crops, no flocks, no harvest — yet Habakkuk commits to joy. This is not a conditional trust that waits for circumstances to improve but a deliberate, faith-fueled choice grounded in the character of God as Savior. The sermon closes with the image of deer's feet on the high hills — sure-footed strength that comes not from the prophet's own resources but from Yahweh Himself."
