      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro preaches a standalone sermon on Romans 1:16-17, which he describes as the thematic foundation of the entire epistle. He begins at the end of verse 17 and works backwards, building from the ground up. The righteousness of God — a present, ongoing revelation every time Scripture is preached — is the alien righteousness that is outside ourselves, the righteousness that comes from God and is declared to be ours in Christ. The sermon expounds justification by faith alone: Christ became flesh to obey the law perfectly on our behalf, He died the death sinners deserved (most profoundly in His separation from the Father), and He rose again to guarantee life to those He represented. The double imputation — Christ's righteousness counted to us, our guilt counted to Him — is the heart of the gospel.\n\nPastor Lyro then turns to Paul's statement about not being ashamed, using Peter's denial of Christ as a mirror. Peter denied Jesus to avoid the social consequences of association with Him, and we do the same — in more sophisticated ways — whenever we fail to share the gospel out of fear of embarrassment, lost prestige, or social disapproval. The sermon ends with a direct challenge: are we ashamed of the gospel? Jesus warns that whoever is ashamed of Him, of him the Son of Man will be ashamed when He comes in His Father's glory. The only remedy is being convinced by the Spirit of the power of this gospel — the power of God unto salvation for everyone who believes."
