      "summary": "Elder Scott Hollander surveys the entire book of Ruth in a single lesson, organizing it around the three covenantal themes of redemption, hesed (loving kindness), and providence. The lesson begins by placing Ruth in its historical context — the chaotic period of the judges when everyone did what was right in their own eyes — and defining key covenantal terms: the kinsman redeemer's obligation to buy back a relative's estate, levirate marriage, and the rich Hebrew word hesed, which carries connotations of steadfast, promise-keeping, relational love. Students are encouraged to add hesed to their working theological vocabulary.\n\nHollander traces the plot of all four chapters, noting how Yahweh's hand moves through the ordinary decisions of Ruth, Naomi, and Boaz. The narrative's use of 'chance' in chapter 2 — Ruth happening to come to Boaz's field — is identified as a Hebrew device pointing to divine providence. Boaz's covenantal character is highlighted throughout: he greets his workers with Yahweh's blessing, addresses Ruth as 'daughter,' and ultimately acts as the kinsman redeemer when the unnamed relative (called Poloni Almoni — 'such and such') refuses the cost. The lesson closes by drawing the typological connections forward: Boaz foreshadows Christ, who intervened for His family when no one else could, at the highest possible cost. The birth of Obed echoes proleptically in Simeon holding the infant Jesus, and the genealogy leads directly to David and to the Messiah."
