      "summary": "Elder Andrew Hoy develops the question 'where is the true church?' from Matthew 16:13-20, using it as the basis for the evening's main question: is there salvation outside the church? He first establishes that the church is an institution — a real, tangible thing with defined boundaries, leadership, and rules — built upon men entrusted with the gospel. The foundation is both Peter and his confession, not Peter alone as Rome claims, and not the confession divorced from Peter as Elder Hoy had previously overemphasized. The true church is found where there is fidelity to the gospel and to the apostolic foundation recorded in the New Testament.\n\nThe lesson then examines the Roman Catholic position — 'extra ecclesiam nulla salus' (outside the church there is no salvation) — tracing its development from strict dogmatism through Vatican II's softening to the doctrine of anonymous Christianity. Elder Hoy rejects both extremes: the rigid RC view that ties salvation to the papal system, and the anonymous Christianity view that opens salvation to sincere seekers of any religion. The Westminster Confession's carefully qualified statement is adopted: there is no ordinary possibility of salvation outside the visible church. This is defended by showing that regeneration, justification, sanctification, and adoption are each inseparably connected to the church's ministry. The sermon closes with three vivid practical scenarios: an elderly man who stopped attending church, the question of what happens when a member stops attending entirely, and a pastoral warning to young women about men who claim to be Christians but are unattached to any local church."
