      "summary": "This lesson opens the final theological section of the prayer series, addressing the relationship between the sovereignty of God and prayer. The teacher notes that near-universal confusion surrounds this topic and proposes to spend two sessions working through it. Two foundational truths run throughout: God is absolutely sovereign, but sovereignty never cancels human responsibility; and human beings are responsible, accountable creatures, but their responsibility never diminishes God’s sovereignty.\n\nThe practical implication is stated boldly at the outset: a day without prayer is a day that denies the sovereignty of God and glorifies human self-sufficiency. If God is sovereign and prayer is unnecessary, the logical conclusion is self-sufficiency. The New Testament alone contains roughly 155 verses on prayer, and the most prominent pray-er in the Gospels is Jesus himself — who also happens to be the sovereign God. Sovereignty and prayer can only function together.\n\nThe teacher then turns to the most common version of the objection: if God has predestined who will be saved, why pray for anyone’s conversion? He answers by examining the biblical description of the unregenerate person — dead in sin, enslaved, blinded by Satan, hardened against God, and in active rebellion. Given this picture, he argues, what exactly would someone who believes in ultimate human self-determination be asking God to do for such a person? The only coherent prayer for an unconverted person is a prayer asking God to overcome that person’s resistance — which is by definition a sovereign act. The doctrine of election is not an obstacle to prayer for the lost but its only solid foundation.\n\nFinally, the teacher draws an analogy between prayer and preaching: just as God has ordained preaching as the means by which the elect come to faith, so he has ordained prayer as a means by which his will comes to pass. God ordained both the ends and the means. The sovereign God of election is prayer’s best friend: the more firmly we believe that only God can save, the more urgently we are driven to pray that he will do exactly that."
