      "summary": "This lesson presents the first two of five basic facts about the sovereignty of God and prayer, intended to close out the prayer series. The teacher begins by noting that something remarkable went unquestioned among believers in both testaments: they simultaneously affirmed God’s absolute sovereignty over all things and prayed with great urgency and fervency. Far from finding these two convictions in tension, they found sovereignty to be the very foundation of their prayer.\n\nThe first example is Daniel in chapter 9. Daniel is reading the scroll of Jeremiah, calculates that the prophesied seventy years of captivity are almost complete, and then immediately prays with fasting and sackcloth and ashes for the very thing God has clearly promised will happen. His certainty about what God was going to do did not make him pray less; it made him pray more. The more certain believers are of God’s sovereign promises, the more urgently they plead for those very things.\n\nThe second example is the first recorded apostolic prayer meeting in Acts 4, following the disciples’ arrest and release. Rather than beginning with their crisis, they began with theology — confessing that God made heaven and earth and that he had predestined even the actions of Herod and Pilate against Christ. This confidence in God’s sovereignty over their enemies’ actions gave them courage not to pray for deliverance but for boldness to keep preaching the same message that had gotten them arrested.\n\nThe second basic fact is that it is not wrong to pray so-called selfish prayers — personal petitions for health, provision, family, and so on. The teacher uses David’s fervent pleading for his dying child as the key example. God had clearly revealed through Nathan that the child would die, yet David prayed with desperate earnestness. His prayer was not wrong. The moment the child died, David accepted God’s verdict in peace, rose to worship and eat, and explained that while there was breath, he prayed to the only one who could help. The proper heart attitude for any petition is the posture of Jesus in Gethsemane: present the desire honestly and urgently, while genuinely submitting to whatever answer God gives."
