      "summary": "This lesson works through six common excuses for prayerlessness, drawn from a study text on praying with Paul. Each excuse is examined sympathetically — the teacher acknowledges that all of them are familiar from personal experience — but each is ultimately shown to be without biblical warrant.\n\nThe first excuse is busyness, which Martin Luther famously inverted: the more demanding the day, the more essential the time spent in prayer before it. The story of Mary and Martha is the key text, where Jesus gently rebukes Martha’s frantic activity and affirms that Mary, sitting at his feet, has chosen the better thing. A comic illustration of a motorist who refuses to stop for gas, then runs out again, captures how Christians rush through life without stopping to refuel spiritually.\n\nThe second through fifth excuses move from the pragmatic to the deeply personal: feeling too spiritually dry, feeling no need, being too bitter, and being too ashamed. In each case the teacher argues that the excuse actually inverts the proper response. Spiritual dryness is precisely why one should pray, not a reason to abstain. Feeling no need is a symptom of dangerous self-sufficiency, as illustrated by Joshua’s blunder with the Gibeonites and Hezekiah’s pride. Bitterness corrupts prayer at its root by preventing the honest confession of sin that real prayer demands. Shame, far from being an obstacle, is a summons — God sought out ashamed Adam and Eve, and he seeks us out as well.\n\nThe final excuse, contentment with mediocrity, is perhaps the most subtle: a form of outward religion that maintains appearances without the engine of private prayer. James 4 is the defining text — you do not have because you do not ask, and when you do ask it is with wrong motives. The remedy is not trying harder but repentance and a genuine drawing near to God."
