      "summary": "Pastor Tito Lyro delivers a standalone Sunday school lesson on self-control, the fruit of the Spirit he personally chose to study in depth as a school project because he recognizes it as an area of personal need. The lesson opens with a sweeping biblical theology of self-control — or rather, the lack of it — from Eve and Cain through the flood generation, the judges period, David, Solomon, and the entire monarchy era. Ed Welch's insight is introduced: lack of self-control is greed expressing itself, and in the Old Testament that greed characteristically expressed itself as idolatry, which always reduces to worship of self.\n\nThe heart of the lesson is the exposition of Galatians 5:13-24. Paul's command to walk by the Spirit is the key: walking by the Spirit produces the fruit of the Spirit (including self-control), while failing to walk by the Spirit produces the works of the flesh (the anti-fruit). The command is not to produce the fruit directly but to walk in the Spirit — which means a life of obedience to what the Holy Spirit says in His Word, not a mystical exercise. This fruit is singular, meaning all nine characteristics must be present in varying measures in every believer. The lesson also draws on Proverbs 4:23 (guard your heart, the source of all issues of life), Proverbs 19:15 (laziness as lack of self-control according to the Septuagint), 2 Peter 1:5-9 (diligently adding virtue to faith), and 2 Timothy 3:1-5 (love of self as the root of self-indulgence). The closing application notes that secular 12-step programs and cognitive behavioral therapy cannot produce biblical self-control, since that is the fruit of the Spirit and requires regeneration."
