      "summary": "Preaching from Colossians 3:1-17, Elder Andrew Hoy explores Paul's central exhortation to 'set your mind on things above' as the literary and theological hinge of the entire letter. He situates the text against the errors Paul was combating in Colossae — a blend of paganism, syncretism, and legalism — all of which share the common fault of drawing believers away from the sole and exclusive sufficiency of Christ. He also addresses the implicit error of earthly-mindedness, arguing that Paul is not calling for an other-worldly indifference to civic life, but rather for a Christ-centered orientation that makes true engagement with the world possible.\n\nThe sermon unfolds in three movements: the call to deliberate effort in setting the mind ('set' is an active imperative), the inseparability of mind and affections in biblical knowing, and the specific content that the mind is to dwell on — Christ's person and work, his ways, and his church. Elder Hoy emphasizes that this effort is not performed alone; believers have been raised with Christ and Christ himself intercedes for them at the Father's right hand. The sermon closes with a challenge to examine what has unduly consumed one's attention and to identify concrete steps toward setting the mind on Christ in the coming week."
