      "summary": "Returning to Ephesians 5:15-21 after a Christmas hiatus in the Ephesians series, Pastor Lyro takes up verse 19 — \"speaking to one another in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs\" — as an extended treatment of music in the worship of God. The sermon is explicitly information-driven: it addresses what the church should sing, whether instruments are appropriate, and by what criteria music in worship should be evaluated. He examines and rejects the exclusive-psalmody position, arguing that the three terms in verse 19 are not divisions of the Psalter but distinctions among types of song, and that the Bible itself records numerous songs outside the Psalter. He similarly defends the use of instruments, grounding it in the Psalter's own titles and in the Greek meaning of the word 'psalm' (to pluck).\n\nThe bulk of the sermon develops seven general thoughts on music and worship, four principles for choosing what to sing, and eight criteria for evaluating any song considered for corporate use. Throughout, Pastor Lyro stresses that music is a powerful medium that shapes the affections toward or away from truth — which is why it demands the same theological scrutiny as the pulpit. The sermon closes with a strong assertion that the category of 'Christian who does not sing' does not exist in the Bible. Singing — even singing about one's miseries, as in Psalm 13 — is intrinsic to the life of faith, and heaven itself is depicted as a place of abundant song."
