      "summary": "The third in an October Reformation series that had already addressed Sola Scriptura and Solus Christus, this sermon takes up Sola Gratia — salvation by grace alone — from Ephesians 2:1-10. Pastor Lyro frames the sermon with the Reformation's core question: how can a sinner be reconciled to a holy God? For centuries, the church had taught a synergistic salvation in which human effort complemented Christ's work. The Reformers returned to Scripture and found something entirely different: a God who saves sinners completely, apart from any human contribution.\n\nThe sermon walks through Ephesians 2 to show what man's part in salvation actually is: death, rebellion, and enmity — a negative ten trillion. It is God who, in verses 4 and 5, breaks in with the decisive 'But God,' making dead sinners alive together with Christ. The basis for this salvation is stated in verses 8-9: by grace through faith, and even the faith itself is a gift. Paul closes the circle to prevent any boasting. The sermon ends by tracing five practical effects of sovereign grace in the life of the believer: obedience, eternal life, heavenly blessings, humility, and dependence on God. The vision of Ezekiel 37 — the valley of dry bones brought to life by the preached word — is used to dramatize the power of the word of God and the Spirit of God working together to raise dead sinners to life."
