      "summary": "The first in a three-part Reformation series, this sermon takes up the foundational sola of the Protestant Reformation — Sola Scriptura, the Bible alone as our final authority for all matters of faith and practice. Pastor Lyro opens with a panoramic view of the historical Reformation, tracing its beginnings not only to Luther's 1517 theses but to earlier forerunners like Wycliffe and Huss and to the young Luther's discovery of a chained Bible in the Erfurt library. He shows how the five solas — Scripture alone, grace alone, faith alone, Christ alone, to God alone be the glory — are deeply interconnected, with Sola Scriptura as the load-bearing foundation on which all others rest.\n\nThe doctrinal heart of the sermon is drawn from 2 Timothy 3:14-17. God has spoken, and he has spoken in his word — every word of it breathed out by him. The Bible is not the only authority in the Christian life, but it is the final authority by which every other authority is measured. Its sufficiency is grounded not in the exhaustiveness of 66 books but in the sufficiency of the God who gave it. Because the scriptures are sufficient, they are fully adequate to reveal Christ for salvation, to reprove and correct the believer, and to equip for every good work. The sermon closes with a pastoral challenge: to actually believe the sufficiency of the Bible is to live under submission to it. To claim to believe it while living otherwise is to be a liar."
