      "summary": "The second sermon in an October Reformation series, this message on Solus Christus takes up Acts 4:1-12 as the New Testament foundation for the Reformation doctrine that Christ alone saves. Pastor Lyro begins by tracing the historical path from the Bible's supreme authority (Sola Scriptura) to its inevitable conclusion: an all-sufficient Savior. The early church, like the Reformers, found itself surrounded by a religious system that added human performance to the work of Christ — first-century Judaism's extra-biblical requirements mirroring the Roman Catholic sacramental system the Reformers challenged.\n\nThe sermon's centerpiece is Peter's bold speech before the Sanhedrin: what we did was good, we did it in the name of Jesus whom you crucified, and God approved of him by raising him from the dead — the empty tomb being the strongest apologetic for the gospel. From Acts 4:12 — \"there is no other name under heaven by which we must be saved\" — the sermon expands into a searching pastoral application. Eleven things that Christians commonly add to Christ in place of trusting him alone are named and dismantled: religious observances, moral record-keeping, denominational identity, family pedigree, intellect, education, natural abilities, social media image, possessions, ethnicity, and even sadness or penance. The sermon closes with 1 Peter 2, where the same Peter who spoke in Acts 4 later writes that to those who believe, Christ is precious — the cornerstone rejected by men but upheld by God."
