      "summary": "Pausing from the 1 John series to consider a fruit of the 16th-century Reformation, this sermon takes Acts 4:1-12 as its text for the doctrine of Solus Christus — Christ alone saves. Pastor Lyro traces the church's perennial tendency to add things to Christ — a tendency visible in both first-century Judaism and the medieval sacramental system — and argues that the Reformers were simply restating apostolic truth when they insisted on Christ alone. The sermon's first half follows Peter's speech before the Sanhedrin: what we did was good; we did it in Jesus' name; you rejected him but God vindicated him by raising him from the dead; and therefore there is no other name under heaven by which we must be saved.\n\nThe second half of the sermon develops a rich Christological portrait through six titles describing Christ's all-sufficiency. As the all-sufficient Shepherd, Christ leads his sheep through even the darkest valleys without abandoning them. As the all-sufficient Husband, he bears our debts and gives us his riches in a marriage where we bring only our sins. As the all-sufficient Prophet, he discloses the invisible God — without him, God cannot be known. As the all-sufficient Priest, he is both the intercessor and the sacrifice — the only mediator between God and man. As the all-sufficient King, he conquered through the cross and now rules all history for his glory. As the all-sufficient Friend, he finds the sinner wandering toward eternal death and gives him eternal joy. The sermon closes with the call to cling to Christ alone, shedding every addition — because to live is Christ."
