      "summary": "This sermon on Matthew 5:9 — \"Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God\" — is part of a series on the Beatitudes with two sermons remaining before returning to Ephesians. Pastor Lyro begins by examining John Lennon's vision of peace through the abolition of national and religious identities, finding it fundamentally misdirected: peace cannot be achieved by eliminating religion but only by reconciliation with the God against whom all humanity has sinned. He draws on Sinclair Ferguson and Augustine to establish that the deepest human problem is not international conflict but hostility toward God.\n\nThe sermon then unpacks the significance of the title \"sons of God.\" Pastor Lyro argues that Jesus deliberately chose the word \"sons\" — not the generic \"children\" — to indicate legal adoption with all the rights of a male heir under Roman law. This adoption is equally available to men and women, making it a radical elevation of status. Peacemakers are those who have been reconciled to God through Christ and now carry that ministry of reconciliation to the world. The primary means of world peace, the sermon argues, is not diplomacy or military force but gospel proclamation. The sermon's second half addresses peacemaking within the church, distinguishing true peacemaking (confronting conflict biblically, using Matthew 18) from peace-faking (avoidance), and illustrating the church's corporate bond of peace through Owen's image of sticks bound together by a rope."
