      "summary": "Preaching from Hebrews 12:12-17, Pastor Lyro presents the Christian life as dynamic, constant movement toward holiness — what the catechism calls progressive sanctification. The letter to the Hebrews was written to a congregation being persecuted and considering abandoning the faith. The Holy Spirit's response is not to minimize suffering but to show it is God-designed chastening that yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness. The three-fold command of the passage — strengthen the drooping hands (v. 12), make straight the paths (v. 13), pursue peace and holiness (v. 14) — form the sermon's structure, each drawn from Old Testament allusions the Hebrew-Christian audience would recognize.\n\nThe sermon's most distinctive emphasis is corporate responsibility. The Greek words throughout these verses are plural: all the runners share responsibility for keeping their fellow runners going. Drawing on the imagery of a marathon runner who collapses before the finish line, and on John Owen's illustration of sticks bound together by a rope, Pastor Lyro argues that the church functions as a team where no one is left behind. The means of strengthening are specific: pointing fellow believers to the cross of Christ, to his intercession, and to his coming again. The sermon closes with a warning from verses 15-17: when the church fails to come alongside its members in suffering, bitterness takes root, defiles many, and can lead to forfeiting grace — Esau's tragic example of trading eternal birthright for momentary comfort."
