      "summary": "An October Reformation series lesson on Augustine of Hippo (354–430 AD), delivered by a church member. Like the Swiss Alps, Augustine is so vast that no single lesson can fully capture him—the focus is on one peak: his doctrine of sovereign grace. Covers Augustine’s North African origins, his pagan father Patricius, his devoted Christian mother Monica, his youthful life of sin recorded in Confessions, and why learning from flawed church fathers is valuable. Augustine was Calvin’s most-quoted source in the Institutes and arguably the most influential theologian in Western Christianity."
