      "summary": "In this Sunday school lesson from the first week of a series on Jerry Bridges' book *Trusting God*, Elder Nick Anderson teaches on chapter three: the sovereignty of God. He introduces the series by noting that chapters 4-7 will address God's sovereignty in specific areas (people, nations, nature, and human responsibility), while this lesson lays the theological foundation. He opens with two words of caution: the finite cannot comprehend the infinite, and apparent contradictions in Scripture should lead us first to humility about our own understanding, not skepticism about the Word.\n\nThe lesson is organized around three main points. First, God is absolutely in control of all things — not merely things he 'permits,' but all things. Elder Anderson addresses the common objection that God limited himself in creating free will, citing R.C. Sproul: if a single molecule is outside God's control, he cannot be trusted at all. He surveys several Proverbs passages and Lamentations 3:37 to show that God's purposes stand and all things occur according to his decree — including suffering, adversity, and even evil acts, most decisively the crucifixion of Christ.\n\nSecond, God's sovereignty is not always apparent. Using the books of Esther and Ruth, the lesson shows how God orchestrates ordinary and extraordinary circumstances behind the scenes without any visible intervention. Both stories involve chains of seemingly coincidental events that together accomplish God's purposes — none of which were actually coincidental.\n\nThird, God does what he pleases and no one can thwart him. A rapid survey of texts from Job, Psalms, Isaiah, Daniel, and Ephesians establishes this beyond doubt. The lesson closes with practical application: trusting God in adversity is not a matter of feelings but of the will — choosing to believe what we know about God even when circumstances seem to contradict it, just as Christ himself chose to trust his Father in Gethsemane."
