      "summary": "In this Sunday school lesson — the second in a series on Jerry Bridges' *Trusting God* — Elder Andrew Hoy tackles one of theology's most challenging questions: are God's absolute sovereignty and genuine human freedom compatible? Drawing on chapter 4 of Bridges' book, he organizes the lesson around three views of how God interacts with the world, argues for the biblical (Determinist/Reformed) view, and shows how it can hold human freedom and divine sovereignty simultaneously.\n\nThe three views are: the Deist view (God created the world and left it to run on its own), the Interventionist view (God has all power and intervenes as necessary to accomplish his purposes, but does not decree all particulars), and the Determinist view (God decrees whatsoever comes to pass, including all particular events). Elder Hoy argues that the Interventionist view — common in evangelical circles — fails to accomplish what it sets out to accomplish. It cannot genuinely protect God's holiness, and it ends up questioning whether God is truly God at all. R.C. Sproul is cited: the idea that God decrees all things is not uniquely Reformed but is the position of any consistent theism.\n\nThe lesson then turns to the most difficult application of this doctrine: God's sovereignty over evil human actions. Isaiah 10 and Acts 2:22-23 are examined in detail. The crucifixion of Christ was both determined by God and the result of 'lawless hands' — both divine ordination and human culpability are asserted simultaneously without contradiction.\n\nThe second half of the lesson affirms that Scripture also genuinely upholds human freedom — not the libertarian free will of philosophical discussion, but a real ability to make decisions in accordance with one's nature. The session closes by naming this position 'compatibilism': God decrees all things and humans make genuine decisions, and while we may not be able to explain perfectly how this works, we affirm both because Scripture does. The practical application is that Christians can honestly say to someone who has suffered evil: God hates what was done to you, and it was not outside his sovereign plan for your good."
